      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>-ALDO A.L L'S I'ATHIST SE LF-RAISING FLO UI t PREVENTS INDIGESTION.</head>
        <p>-ALDO A.L L'S I'ATHIST SE LF-RAISING FLO UI t PREVENTS INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER:</p>
        <p>lilt DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>A great many pretty and stylish half-mourning costumes have been lately seen. One, for example, of very fine black cloth, has a simulated under- skirt of black silk, covered with a braiding pattern worked in thin jetted cord. Tke bodice is made with a wide, rounded collar of embroidered silk, under which a fichu of black crepe-de-chine is knotted. The small chemisette and the under- sleeves are also of the crepe, the upper sleeves being turned back just below the elbow with bands of braided silk. Grey and purple are both still very fashionable colours for gowns, especially some curious shades of misty purple which, although not becoming to everybody, arc decidedly stylish and uncommon. For autumn. war a dress of rough, but light-weight, cloth, in one of these purple tones, with revers of cery dark purple velvet, and vest of cream guipure run with narrow velvet, would be exceedingly nice; or the same material might be made up with a stitened skirt, and a small bolero piped with a deep purple silk and worn over a corseiet of the latter and a full vest of ivory chiffon or net. Grey clothsd fancy woollens are usually trimmed with stitched bands of black glac6, or with appliquts of jetted passe- menterie, although a few models are trimmed with purple or plum-coloured silk. Capes have been singularly out of favour this season. For travelling wear they have been replaced by tile Jacque paletots and long loose coats in various styles' and at present there is no indication of their becoming fashionable.</p>
        <p>boleros with loose fronts, deep turndown collar, and tabbed round the waist, are made of white cashmere and covered with rich embroidery in gold thread and glowing colours. These are worn with light skirts, and the hat usually tones with the colours in the bolero. The sleeves reach only to the elbow, and are there turned back with deep gauntlets. Some of the new toilettes are an amalgamation of linen and velvet and Irish guipure, the latter being laid over velvet, which gives it a great depth and richness of effect. The velvet is used in strappings and ruchings on the skirt, and is employed for collar, culls, and belt on th 3 corsage. Such a rich material as velvet gives an effective contrast to the plain simplicity of the linen. Ruchings are employed on many of the "new skirts, laid on in rows up as far as the knees, narrow and close together in front, with wider spaces between the rows at the back.</p>
        <p>_ 'ul toque is made in lace, atcl goes with any gown. The lace is draped over a brim of pleated chiffon, whilst two couteaux, in shaded tints, spangled with diamond drops, are placed on the crown to the left side. A second hat is a wide, round, white chip shape, the brim bound with dark brown velvet, and trimmed with two large oFtrich feathers, caught together in front with a chou of brown velvet, with a diamond buckle in the centre. The remaining hat is of pale blue straw, canotier shape, turned up sharply at the sides, and trimmed underneath the brim with a blush-rose and black velvet ribbons. The flat space between the brims is filled in with blush roses and foliage.</p>
        <p>stockings should be worn each pair in turn- Repairs should be attented to directly they become mecessary. Prevention is ever better than cure, and where thin places are observed, it is advisable -to darn thin over before they develop into actual holes. Thus stockings must be darned with fine worsted ; that of a coarse nature will only drag the surrounding parts, and cause the repaired part to give way. A large hole can be darned more easily if a piece of net be tacked across it first, and the threads taken in and out through this material, which :serves to form a foundation and keep the whole together. Old veils can be used for this purpose, if they are of plain net. The knees and heels of children's stockings may be considerably strengthened by darning them for some distance on the wrong side before they are used. Your loving</p>
        <p>Km GLovls.—lf you get calf-ekin or other strong kid gloves wet, rub a tiny drop of castor-oil well in while they are still on your hands. This prevents them from drying hard and stiff, as they do aftee getting wet. NEW STOCKINGS.—AIways wash these before wearing. It makes them last longer, and prevents risk ot injury to the feet from the colouring. Darning the toes and heels with silk while new is an excellent plan, and buts off the darning day considerably.</p>
