      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MOST NUTRITIOUZ.</head>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUZ.</p>
        <p>E P PS'S</p>
        <p>GPATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>3REAKFAST--SUPPEP.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>In has been stated that there will be one or two costume balls at Buckingham Palace next year. There will no doubt be a revival of State fancy-dress balls, in course of time, but they are not likely to take place until the year after next. It is over fifty years since Queen Victoria gave two magnificent fancy-dress balls St Buckingham Palace. The first was in 1342, when the guests were commanded to appear in the style of the reign of Edward 111. The second took place three yeara later, when the costumes were of the time of George 11., the Queen herself appearing in a dress of that period. The Coronation will add extra inducements for the office of Mayor or Provost next year. The Lord Mayors of London, and the great provincial cities,will certainly ha made narcnets, and it has been stated that every Mayor or Provost throughout the Kingdom, will be made a Knight,in honour of this great cecasicn. There will consequently be a le6cd deal of heart-burning.both amongst the worthy aldermen and councillors—and their wives—who are commonly supposed to attach much more importance to a title of municipal dignity, than do their 'unbend&amp; The Earl of Lonsdale, who is a great friend of the German Empercr, and recently entertained the Crown Prince at Penrith Castle is one of the best of hosts, and a thcroueb. sporteman. There is nothing in the sporting world, whether it be hunting, shooting, driving., or yachting, upon which Lord Lonsdale is not a leading authority, and Lady Lonsdale is as tend of horses and hunting, as her husband. She was formerly Lady Grace Gordon, the daughter of Lord Efuntly, and she may frequently be seen out driving in Cumberland, in the well appcinted turn ruts of Penrith Castle. as The Merquees if Buntly is chief of the Gordon clan, but the fortunes of the family were greatly involved by his grandfather, the ninth Marquees, who, as Lord Strathavon, got greatly into debt. Be devoted the after part of his life to repairing the results of hi., fermer ex- travagances. but the difficulties , have never been fully everceme. The present Marquess married a daughter of the late Sir William Broths, the millionaire Manches- ter banker, and as Sir William had no eon, and only one other daughter, it seemed likely that the Marchioness wculd inherit a goodly share of his wealth. Sir William however, only left her acme trifling bequest, with per- mission to reside at Ahoy is Castle, ana the litigation which was instituted to upset the will, has ended with- out improving the position of the Efuntle'e. The Countees of Maliteeleury, in discussing the question, "13 Society deteriorating ? " says that the great curse of the day is " that we are all gorged with amusements." When one is surfeited with the pursuit of pleasure, the result is not happiness, but depression and languor. That is not a ccreplaint that troubles the great mass of people, but, 39 referring to seciety, there is no denbt a gcod deal of truth in what the Ccuntess says. The remedy wculd be for society to return to simpler living and earlier hours. As regards drinking, and coarse manners, the past I century has seen a great improvement in Society,but as regards late hours, it has got steadily worse in turning night into day. Dining has beccene Icier and later, while after dinner amusements extend into the small honre, and dancing cnly ends as the morning papers ! appear. All this is alteted at Homburg. and other ; Spas, where from the King and Emperor down wards,all get up early, go in for reviler exercise, and generally adept reasonable hours. As for the waters, we might just as well take these fecen a bottle at home. It is the altered living that affects the "cure" and Society would be all the better and healthier if it adopted similar habits all the year round. Table decorations are cften a burden to the house- wife, at this season of the year. Summer roses are ' over, and the hardy perennials make a pone substitute. With some of the lighter of the r. maining flowers, bcw• ever, a good deal can tea done. Shirley and Iceland poppies, most of them in the bud stage weth little bunches of wheat, are t ffective,' and should be mixed with long trails of coloured chenille, or with flowing ribbons, 80 88 not too closely to suggest Harvest decora- tions. Belated sprays cf fuchsia, sweet peas, and clematis, can te pressed into use, and even in a suburban garden, greenery is neually abundant. If net, small pressed vine leaves, and feathery grasses, must be utilised.</p>
        <p>_ Table decorations should always be decided upon some time before they sre neeeed, and the colour scheme I selected ty artificial light, except of course for a lunch or other day-light entertainment. Pewter is in favour now, and most iffective it is. Goblets, cups and beakers, can bold fairly tall !prays, dishes and plates each a small low :spec:men glass, or tiny bowl. About the larger pieces of ware pink avid green ribbens should be trailed and pink and white flowers alone used. Blue flowers are rarely effective, but crimson rambler roses, and love-in-a-mist, make a beautiful mixture with silver plate. Where the store of flower-holders is small, and has been seen before, a set of small baskets, and two larger Cues can he gilded and filled with vases or cups that fit them. Moss pulled out conceals the inner vessels. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Yoke squares of coloured matting, dress a table well, and their rather bright colours susgest a Japanese scheme. Central vases of Japanese porcelain can hold westatia, pink roses, white clematis,or chrysanthemums, or, he pink paper peach-blossom that is so excellent an imitation of the real thing. Japanese table napkins, and paper lanterns, are matters of course, with paper fans, or band screens, bearing the menus, and the names of the guests. As regards "murderous millinery "there is, unques- tionably. much nonsense talked, and partisans beccme so heated in dierusicn that no settled cmviction can I be arrived at. Doubtless the plumage of harmless, nrceitary game and poultry is largely used, and doubt 1 less also many a rare bird is exterminated for leg beauty's sake. But as the retail trader is usually ignorant as to the real origin of his wares, or is unwilling to waste time and money in discussions about the matter, it is useless to expect uurchaeors to iriterfere. They can only quietly decline what appears to them suspicious, and, it it please them, transfer the undoubtful goose or turkey feathers to their own ' heads. This coming winter, feather trimmings of all kinds are likely to be much in evidence. Most of them are being built with the breast feathers of pheasants, which, massed closely together show a glowing beauty well calculated to render many of us doubtful as to whether we may, with a clear censcience, indulge in them. Soft rounded, and iridet cent, these bands are charming with a" heather mixture" gown. he new waistbands are made of soft cashing, that encircles the waist twice, and fastens with • clasp. On these clasps much ingenuity has been expended. They are smaller than of yore, dote with a hook and eye action, and are, as regards the test of them, beautifully enamelled. Deep and light shades of blue are moat often used, as beckground colours, and the little flowers' and leavre, are more or less naturalistic in tiet. A pretty gilt setting is not the least part ef the attractive- nets of these trifles.</p>
        <p>41 adADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
