      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MILL GIRLS' MAD ACT.</head>
        <p>MILL GIRLS' MAD ACT.</p>
        <p>Attempt to Fire Cockhedge Factory.</p>
        <p>Extraordinary Case.</p>
        <p>Two girls named Selina Gibbons, 16, Firth Place and Catherine Lydon, 43, Greeraall-street, appeared hAfose the magistrates at the Wrrington Police Court on Mon- day under most extraordinary circumstances. The Chief Constable informed the Bench that the Cllll was so extraordinary that he intended to take a vcry unusual course in reference to it. The girls, who were 17 years of age, were charged with having on Friday last set fire to a skip of cAton in the mixing room of Cockhedge Mills with intent to destroy the same. Practically, the girls were charged with setting fire it) Ccelchedge Mills, and had it not been for the timely discovery of their act, the result would have been a serious disaster to Warrington, a disaster which if luckily not attended with loss of life might have thrown cut of employment 600 people and possibly 1,200, and caused incalculable inconvenience and damage. The girls were charged with arson. It was possible elan probable that they had no foresight of the probable eerie tta consequences of their act, when they committed the crime and he therefore proposed after giving the facts of the case fully, to be guided by the magistrates decision as to whether he should proceed on the charge of arson or reduce it to one of wilful damage which their Clerk would advise them could be done. 1p to Wednesday the girls wsre employed at Cockhedgo Factory, and on the evening of that day were told by one in authority that they must go back to do certain work over again. They impudently refused, and with- out giving notice left their work. They seemed to have afterwards knocked about the town in company with another girl named Ada Geddes,and on Friday afternoon about a quarter past five they went to the mills and dt- manded wages which they considered were due to there. They were told by Mr Ainsworth that their wages were according to the sgreemeat under which they worked forfeited. This seemed to have angered the girls, and. they went into the mixing rocm of the mill. In that room were dezens of skips similar to the one now in court filled with cotton, as he understood, in a semi- prepared state. The material was very inflammable. Thelgirla were there seen by a lad named Patrick Farrell, iltd they told him that they were looking for some aprons they bad left. The aprons, however, never materialized in the case at all. They asked the lad for a match, and be said .he had none, and was forbidden to carry them about the worts. The girl Gteddee was with the prisoners. They went into the yard and asked 6 man named Thomas Grren for a match. He being a smoker gave them one, and the three then returned to the mixing room. Gibbons lighted the match near the skip and Lydon said "row, go on, set fire to it.' Incredible as it might seem, Gibbons, at the otheie bidding deliberately set fire to the skip of cotton In that great factory. This was seen by Ada Geddes, who narrowly escaped being an accom- plice. The prisoners hurriedly left the build- ing, and immediately after their departure , smeke and tames were seen to Issue from the room and I a smell of fire pervaded the whole premises. The bands rushed to the mixing rcom and found the skip of cotton well alight. Farrell and a man named Barrett obtained I buckets of water and extinguished the incipient fire. The matter was reported to Mr Ainsworth, and he had a suspicion that there bad been foul play. fie communi- cated with the pence, and he (the Chief Constable)went early on Saturday mornirg with Chief Inspector Phillips to investigate the matter. They found the man who supplied the girl with a match, and after ascertaining other details interviewed the girl Geddes, who, after some hssitation, made a clean breast of the whole matterlie placed the two girls under arrest and charged them with the offenv. One said "I am very sorry." The other said " lam sorry, too." Considering what might have been the result the damage was inconsider- able-50s. If the charge of arson was proceeded with prisoners would have to go to the Maims. The Chairman: Is this rocm on the ground floor The Chief Constable : Yes, I understand from Mr Ainsworth that it is not a fire proof apartment. Mr. Ainsworth said it was only partially fire proof. The Chairman : We can hardly think that this was a piece of wickedness on the prisoners' part, to deliber- ately attempt to burn the mill down. We shall reduce the charge,to one of wilful damage. It is a terrible case for girls of their age to be brought up on a charge of this character.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable thanked the magistrates for their instruction and guidance. Perhaps be said they would not require him to call any evidence except it was upon some particular point. The prisoners were then charged with committing Wilful damage by lighting the skip of cott.m. The Chief Constable informed the magistrates that both the parents of the first girl Were deaf and dumb. The Chairman said the case was a very serious .one indeed. Though there was nothing previous against the girls they could not pass the case over without in- dicting a penalty ahich they would feel. If a fine were inflicted it would have to be borne by the parents and the magistrates had therefore decided to send them to prison for a month in class two. They would not iliX there with the ordinary criminals. They hoped nit" would be such a warning to them that they would not repeat this or any other offence of the kind. The Chief Constable said that if the prisoners had been sent to the A 99 7A9 on the charge of arson they would have been liable to 14 years penal servitude. Id the last case of arson from Warrington a man received ten years' imprisonment and the cass was not so serioul as this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CHILD'S SHOCKING DEATH.</head>
        <p>A CHILD'S SHOCKING DEATH.</p>
        <p>Another Street Fatality at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Another sad street fatality occurred on Saturday morning at Sankey Bridges. when Patrick Welch, aged ' one year and ten mouths who lived with his mother at 15, Plumptoe-street, was killed by being run over. Shortly after ten o'clock a London and North-Western Railway Bitty was being driven past the deceased's residence by Alfred Woodward, when the child ran out of the house, across the footpath, and fell under the - front off-wheel. 'roe wheel passel over its head,killing the child instantly. The inquest was conducted at the Police Station on Tuesday afternoon by Mr S Brighou.e, County Coroner. Mr Wood appeared on behalf of the Londoo and North- Western Railway Company. Bridget McCoy, wife of John McCoy, labourer, 15. Plumuton-street, said d ,ceased was laer illegitimatel son and was one year and ten months old. She last saw him alive at a quarter past ten on Saturday) 'morning, when he went out of the kitchen to the front door. She flex I heard a shout, and on running to the front door. SAW him on the ground at the side of a lurry. He was 'bleeding from the head. Assistance was at hand and De I N-iden was called in, but he told her the child was dead. I Alfred Woodward, a carter its the employ of the ILondon and North Western Railway Company, and residing at the Old Patten Arms, said he had been in the employ of the Company thirteen months, and had been used :to horses all his life. At 10 o'clock on Saturday morning be left the Goods Yard with a horse and lurry to go to Peniteth. When he was passing the residence of the deceased, the child ran out of the- house and fell under the front off wheel, which was about eighteen inches from the footpath. Witness was driving on the near aids because he was passing two carts which were going in the same direction as himself. Ile was driving slowly at the time the accident occurred.</p>
        <p>It was explained to the Coroner that the driver was obliged to go round by Plumpton-street because rxcava- tions were in progress in Liverpool-road in connection with the laying of the train lines. Mr Thomas Brough, master baker, 129, Weilfield- street, said he was unloading a van about fifteen or twenty yards from the scene of the accident. He saw the child leave the footpath suddenly, and it appeared to fall towards the !my. He thought the horse had passed the house when the child came out. In his opinion the accident was unavoidable, and occurred too suddenly to enable the driver to pull up in time. The Coroner said it was a curium' thing that he con- ducted an injury at Wartington on Friday into the case of a child being run over, and thtt morning he held one at St Helens where a child exactly the same age as Welch, was run over. The jury returned a verdict of Accidental death " and exonerated the driver from blame. Mr Wood informed the Coroner that Woodward was a weeds. and careful driver. The Coroner: 1 could see from the way he gave his, evidence that he was upset abou: the accident.</p>
        <p>In 1316 the United Kingdom possessed only 551 journals, of which 14 were issued drily. Now there are no fewer than 2 448 newspapers and 2,448 magazines. making 4,894 in all. The fan was first brought into general European WM by Catterine de Medicis,wbo introduced it into France.</p>
