      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BAPTISMAL SERVICES AT HILL CLIFFE CIIAPEL,—On Saturday arid teundey baptiernal services were con- ducted it. Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel by the Rev J S Hughes. Sueh services have always a special attra:- tion for peeple outside the Ilaptiet community owing to the manner in which baptism is administered, viz., by immersion. Formerly, end for generations, the church at Hill Cliffs, held their baptismal services in the open air. Three places are pointed out as the baptizing places of former daye.—Toe Broek at Cann-lane, Appleton ; the Brock at Hough's Bridge, Higher Walton ; and the Dipping Brook in Lower Walton, which has Teen cbliterat.e4 from the lane'sc ape by the Snip Canal. The converts were haptiz .d in ters-i brooks summer and winter. To these baptisms great crowds resorted, and whilst many scoffed at them they were the means of pro- pagating Baptist views in many directional': later years a baptistry was placed in the chapel, end though there cannot be such crowds, as would meet in the open air, e;ill there are usually good congregations, with a large sprinkling of members of other denominations. Such was the case on Saturday and Sunday.—The perviee on Saturday night was held for the convenience of a num her of converts belonging to the Presbyterian Church, Runcorn, who desired to be baptz,cl in this ancient method. There were four such, and tree others belong- ing te the friends at Hill Cliffs, all being females. There was a full service. Tee pater. the Rev J S Hughes, preached from Acts, x, 47, 46, " Can any man forbid water, that these sheu!el not be baptized, which have received the Holy Ghost as well as we ? And he cerr.- -mended them to be baptized in the name of the Lord." He paid he was always pleased to administer the ordi- nance of baptism as it indicated a crowing interest in the subj-ct among the other Christian churchee, and revealed that the Baptists w' re more anxious for the spread of truth than the extension of mere denomina- tionalism. He dwelt upon the Anost'e's challenge, explainiag that the apostle evidently anticipated some objection to the baptism of thee Gentile converts, that he was fully convinced that they were fit and proper candidates for baptism. the one great condition being that teey gave proof of conversion. It was just here, Paid the preacher that they differed most seriously from ether Christian Churches Not simply on the mode of bore-ism, hut on other subjects. They held that none should be baptised except those who gave personal proof of faith in the Lori Jesus Christ. None but believers were baptised by the Aposqes, as witness the baotisms of the New Testa- ment where ail the subjects were pe-sons who gave evi- dence of faith in Christ. The Challenge of the Apostle also showed, that the possession of . the Holy Ghost already, was not a reaeon fir neglecting water baptism but a reason why it she&amp; 1 he soueht Mr Hughes gave many testimonies from modern biblical sc aolars, who did not belong to his section of the Church to shaw that they impartially and candidly acknowledged that the Baptists were in the right in the practice of baptism. It might not be known to all that in the assemble of diviees held in Westminster in 1643, infant eprinking was only carried by a majority of one vote out of forty nine persons preasmt, and that too,under the presence of weighty ecclesiastical influence. Thus they held they had overwhelming authority on their silo, oven outside the range of Scripture, and they claimed that the Scripture was a sufficient guide and was the only rule of conduct that the practice of hantiem had been changed by the majority of Christen- dom., to suit as is supposed d'fferent conditions Against that they protested and declar,d that no power neither Pone, cdeference nor convocation, had a right te change the ordinances of the church.—Mr Hughes then immersed seven candidates going down into the water with them, repeating the simple formula with each, " I hiptize thee on the profession of thy faith in the Lerd Jesus, into the name of the Father, Son and 'Holy Ghost, Amen," and as he immersed ,anh candidate the choir struck up a refrain " Praise the Lord, Halle- lujah ! " The rffireers of the ehuech assisted Mr Hughes to lift the candidates out of the baptistry. The service was orderly, reverent and impressive. On Sunday morning the service was similar, five more being immersed, four males and one femele. Mr Hughes discoursed on the hap dam of the Eunue- illustrating and enforcing hie different mints by Blue, tratiens and argunoerti from Scripture, Church History and the scholarship of the day in confirmation of their attitude and practine on this question, and making an earnest appeal for the return to this apostolic practice on the part of all the churohes as the basis of Christian . _</p>
        <p>In the evening the last service for this sm-nm=,r in the graveyard was held, and a fairly large c-mgregatinn assembled. Mr Hushes was the preacher and he selected for his text the words." For none of us liveth unto himself and none of us clinth unto himself." All the services were of an interesting and profitable chsracber and as the rrsult, other baptisms are to follow. The choir rendered good services during the day, Mr Hallas officiating at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. Matthews' Ministry. On Sunday the R.v John Francis Matthews com- menced the fifth year of his ministry at Latchford Baptist Church. The subject of the morning sermon VMS taken from Paige xlix. 16. 'B Mold. I have graven !Alen upoe the palms of my hands." In the evening " The central teaching of the Gospel " formed the the-ne of the eerrnen, the text being taken from I. Cor. i. 23, " But we preach Christ crueified." The congrega- trans at both services were good. 'Flower Service at Moore Wesleyan Church. • The annual fl ewer service was held on Sunday even- ting in the Moore Wesleyan Creipei. The Rev John Grumeil was the Preacher. During an interval in the service, while a ye'untary was relayed by the organist. Mr John Banner,•iie offering of flower; took place—first by adults, and afterwards by the children of the school, who advanced in large numeore to :he communion rail. Here the baskets; and bouquets were received and placed on a large temporary table. Many choice flower' were re- ceived and cnnveyed as an offering to the sick at the -Warrington Workhouse Hospital. Bold-street Wesleyan Church. The Rev. C H. Hocken preached at the above church on Sunday morning and delivered an impressive dis- course. In the al terneen a musical service was held, Mr. Geo. Weaver presiding. A cantata, " Penitrere, Pardon, and Peace" (J 13 Maunder), was effectively rendered by the choir, Mr 6' Hickman presiding at the organ. The duet "Love divine, ell love excelling," was e creditably interpreted by Miss L Davies and Mr S H Oilier, and the hymn " Joy of harvest" was sung by a number of the Sunday school children. The old Welsh chorale " Jesu, lover of my clout " was rendered by the male voices in the choir. The collection was on behalf of the new organ fund. The Rev. Theodore B:shop occupied the pulpit at the evening service. Open Meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. There was a large congregation in Wycliffe Church 00 .Sunday afternoon on the occasion of the open meeting in connection with the P.S.A. Slciety. On this occasion the members were aceompanied by their wives and ' friends. Mr E Bindloss, of Wsrringtoe, occep'el the chair and said it must be gratifying to those who teok an interest in these menthes to see such a large attendance. He Proceeded to give an example of the good which had attended the establishment of the P S A.. movement and spoke words of encouragement to his hearere, asking them to • endeavour to make the world a little better than they , 'found it. The speaker was Mr George Power, of Man- chester, who pointed out how diff ‘rent the world would become if people carried out the spirit of the berme theyesang instead of merely singing them. He also eetailed some ef the results which had been obtained through the infl ience of the Fl asa.nt Sunday afternoon movemmt, and gave seine of his experiences in rescue work in Manchester. Two solos entitled ' Arm, arm, ye brave" and " A Crown of Glory " were very effec- tively sung by Mr WII Wallineton. Mr W H Payton, Mll5. Bac , presided at the organ. Mr B Farrar, hon. esecretary, announced that with the new members who had joined that afternoon the roll contained 480 names. Last year the nnrcber was 560, and he urvd the mem- byre to make efforts to increase the membership beyond that of last yetr.—The devotional exercises were con- ducted be the Rev J fl Ridelette. of Haydock.--&amp; collec- tion was taken on behalf of the Society's funds.</p>
      </div>
