      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL. Cantilevers for Warrington al-loge.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL. Cantilevers for Warrington al-loge.</p>
        <p>The ordinary monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Tr, so Hall. Warrington, on TueAaY o'"niug• Tee deputy-Mayor (Alderman Fairelough) presided, and there w,re also present :—Aldermen He,t on. Snaw Green, Hutchinson, Sutton, Bolton, Monks ; Councillors S Davies, 'f B Mttldn, J Baird. W J Forshaw, Fiumpton, Tlonion, Smethurst, John Elans, A Bennett, Ad Regeis,Witinsoa,W Brough,A. Jolley,Duncan,L Ellison, William Roberts, Hulse, John 1:10oerts. Greenbee ; with J Leon Whittle (town clerk), S Wilson (deputy to c'ork)„J Fairhurst (borough treasurer). Dr J Guest Girnall (acting medical eflier of health),W H Grimsdale (electrical engineer), J 11:".a,S1 (water enginee:), W Haddock (ins engineer). THE LATE MR. J. H. GORNALL. The DEPUTY MAYOR reel the following letter : " Aucust 15th. Mrs Gernall and family rend their gratefal thanks to the Mayor and Town CDURCiI for the kind way in which they have expressed their apprecia- tion of the public woi k done by the late Medical ()Hiner of Health ; also for their sympathetic condolence in the sad loss sust.ained by the widow and family. Ribbles- dale, Grapnenhall." WITCH COMMITTEE.—THE 2,IEW POLICE BUILDINGS. At a meeting of the Watch Committee it was resolved that tenders be obtained for decorating the court room, magistrates' retiring room, and corridors at the new police premises with duresco painting.—The minutes were approved, on the melon of the DEPUTY MAYOR, seconded by Alderman HEAT3N. STEER r IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON propo:ied the adoption of the minutes of this committee, which appeired in our last issue.</p>
        <p>Councillor PENICETr seconded• Councillor TiNtaiox,referring to the Proooled purchase of land at the corner of Wileon-Patten-street and Winmarleigh-str2et, said he did not raise any obi&gt;ction to the amount proposed to be paid, but he thought the question of doing away with the hoardings at the same time should be considered. He thought the amount mentioned should not only have pail for the strip of land, but have recompensed anyone who might have any claim wito regard to the bill-posting station there. Ile did not care for hoarding,' of that description in such a locality, but the principal reason for his ohjaction was that the corner would be very awkward when the tramcare commenced running, and would be to his mind a siurce of deriver. Be hope I in any e imilar case that arose the commitree would give this matter their attention. Alderman SHAW GREEN asked for some particulars about the property in Pe sth-lane and Golnorne-street. Be thought it, was the intention of the committee to take down the property occupying the centre of Heath- lane. *Elia rec-dlention was that the property proposed to be purchased was on the left aide and running up to Heath-side Rchool. The thoroughfare bstween Walkers' brewery and that property was very wide, and he could not understand why it was proposed to purchase the property. Councillor PLUMPTON said the property had been condeerined, to his knowledge, for the iast eight or ten years as insanitary, and he did not see that they were purchasing it for any advantage except to pull it down as condemned property. ! The DEPUTY MAYOR Do you think it a desirable purchase ? Councillor PLUMPTON : For the sake of taking down insanitary property. Tao owners are, however, bound to take it down. Alderman SUTTON: Not at all. Alderman florroar said the money would be well spent. They could not widen the street on the other side where Mesars Walker'a brewery stood, but on tais side there was no' na rob prop ,rty to be pulled down. Alderman SUTTON said he sympathized with valet Councillor Tinnion said with regard to the hoarding. Be would be glad if they could have thorn removed from many places in the tows,. Certainly this hoarding had not a good appearance. At the same time he would call the attention ef the Council to the fact that probably £5 a year rent was paid for it. Councillor TINNION: £2. Alderman SUTTON said it would not be given up with- out some Consideration. Toe Town Clerk and com- mittee had certainly done all they could to get the land as cheaply as possible. If they could go further and vet Mr Crompton to do away with the hoarding for a slight consideration, or none at all, they would be very glad t do it. eillf Councillor Tinninn could help them in any way they would be glad to have his assistarce. With regard to the property spoken of by Alderman Shaw Green, he thought A'clerman Holton bad given the correct reply. In order to oh am n the ! prescribed line for widening the street it was far better ! to purchase property even if it was in an insanitary condition, as Councillor Plumpton said,for £6OO than to purchase a slice of the brewery. They would have to pay a good many six hundred pounds for land on the other side. Councillor Plumpton was in error in what be said about the property they proposed to purchase. The owner would uot be bound to pull the property down but to put It int° a sanitary condition. Councillor PLUMPTON : He cannot do it• Councillor SUTTON : That's your opinion. Perhaps other people have a better knowledge than you. councillor PLUMPTON : It is the opinion of the late Medical Officer of Health. Alderman SUTTON said the Medical Offirer of Health might have stated what it was des,rable to do but it would he umarateful to compel the owner to pulls down what he could make sanitary and good. The minutes were then agreed to. HEALTH COMMIT TEE T.:INSPECTOR NORMAN'S</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ILLNESS.</head>
        <p>ILLNESS.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Health Committee were adopted on the motion of Alderman Sutton seconded by Coun- cillor Bsird. They contained the reports on the Tubr- culosis Congress limier) by the deputation and by Dr Guest Gorman, and which we published last S sturday. Alderman SUTTON said he was sorry to hear that there was little improvement in the condition of Inspec- tor Norman. He hoped they would soon have better tows. (Hear, hear.) Tney would se:' from Dr Gornah's report how important it was that they should bring about sanitary reforms and discourage the evils whiM caused the dangerous diseases referred t'. WOOD PAVING ROUND -.3IIURCHES.</p>
        <p>The Paving and Sewerage Committee reported that they bad inspected the streets interfered with by the tramways, and the Borough Sirveyor was instructed to procure tenders where practicable for altering the level sad reinstating the sett pavements. Ton Borouffn Sur- veyor was also instructed to procure t-trfers'for the completion of the flagging and psving of linutsford- road. Alderman HEATON Moved the cpnfirmation of the minutes, and observed that a great deal f work had been done. Eta wished to point our, to save Councillor Ellison submitting his motion--" That the port,ion if Kntitstord-road fronting toe'Latohford Wesleyan Church be paved with wood bloAt,"—that Coat was being done wherever posOhle. CAincillor TINNION Sell they had only to draw the attention of the Chairman of the Committee to the matter arid the work would be done. Councillor W ROBERTS: Da you mean that where the 'present setts exist round any place of worship they will be ehenzed for wood if asked for? The Deputy MAYOR: Only in cases where the pave- ment is already being taken up I beliein,. Alde,man HEATON : Only in cases of repairing. MUSEU:n CONIMITTEE.—THE EX H I BI</p>
        <p>It was reported at a meetinz of the above ccmmittee that a f eloc ion of works from the National Competition of Schools of Art wolVd be rxlibited at the t chool of Art from Septemher 7h to 12;h. Alderman MONKS moved the confirmation of the minutes and called attention to the rxhibition. It wag intended to have a private viow oa Friday eveoinz to which the members of the Council would be invited. Alderman SHAW GREEN secouded, and the motion was se'reed ". CEMETERY COMMITTEE.—PROPO3ED DRINKING</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>FOUNTAIN.</head>
        <p>FOUNTAIN.</p>
        <p>The minutes were of a formal character, except the report of the ligistrar whinh stated that the iaterrnents for the past four weeks were :—Adults, 30 ; minors, 107 ; stillborn ar.d rirrrnstu7e, 11, (Sorresp)ndintr. period last year—adults, 37 ; minors, 45 ; stillborn and prema- ture. fiVP. The receipts for the past four weeks were, £llO 15s ; corresponding period last year. £75 Os 2d. The Chairman reported that he had met the Borough Surveyor and agreed on the position of the drinking fountain near tho gates. The minutes were passed, the mover being Councillor JOHN EVANS, and the seconder COUTICiIIOr WILZINW/N. TOWN HALL, PARK AND BATHS ccolmirrEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above cr,mmitl.ee it was resolved that the Borough Surveyor provide hurdles to stop the gaps in the fences of Victoria Park,and that the B-irouela Surveyer report on the eost of providing a drinking fountain in Victoria Park. It was resolved at a meeting of the Water C-immittee that the tender of Messrs H }Molex, and Son, Limited, for weigh bridge required at Delnti-lane Pumping Station at £46 and that of Messrs Welt and Company, of Glasgow, for feed nomp reqoired at the Winwick Pumping Station at £45 be accepted. Alderman BOLTON moved, and Councillor SMETHURST seconded the ;adoption of the minutes. Cluzcillor MALKIN suggested the provision of a bowl- ine green in Victoria Park. The DETIITX-MAYOR : It is a qaestion of expense and we are very bard up. The minutes were carried. WATER COMMITTEE TENDERS ACCEP ED. SATISFACTORY PROGREaS OF 'f HE WATER</p>
        <p>WORKS.</p>
        <p>COUOCHIOV JOIIN EVANS St.thdaltted the confirrnstion of the minutes, and repo7ted upon the progresa of the water works. With reference to the new reservoir at Winwick the outer walls and intermediate walls were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>111 E EXAMINER FRU) SENT kIBER 6 1901</head>
        <p>111 E EXAMINER FRU) SENT kIBER 6 1901</p>
        <p>complete, and the contractor was now engaged in lining the whols of the reFervoir and preparing. for tho arch ewer. At IMO-Ivor the shafts 1 2 and 3 were down at their depth and No. 4 ,was down to a depda 1 143f.r. and was only st. short of wh.tiwas revired. Toe adit from No. 1 had In=en driven 70 feet and the al.it from No. 2 in toe direction to meet the aditr frrn N). 1 shaft 40 feet. The rriservoir at Appleton was procrieditir: satisfactorily, Ond when finished the whole of tire schErne or improving the water supply at Srockton Beath, Apple:on and Eirrettoc, would be complete. Tie pump and rein ii were all, ready.</p>
        <p>, Councillor Amp Seconded. Alderman Surrop thoug:at the Council wore to be invited to inspect the woik3 and taste the wAter with a little mixture. (Lailil2htell)- COU,ciilor WILLIAM ROBERTS V.74g yPr7 glad Alderman Sutton had made the sugg.estioa (Loud laughter) The resolution on the agenda gave him Ha, oppertiMity which lie had wanted over since he had been a member of the ueurel, and that was to stip.gest that an oppor- tunity shoulo he given to every youn: or now nember to see the van i ins works helonging to the Corporation. There were SfA/10 members who knew Abiolule'y no'h- log about the sanitary works at 1.,-eigferd—flati4literi— the water works, the electric lighting eta ion, and all the other large projactg belonging to the Carpoi•al ion, It would only be caurtepaa to invite the votine.er mem- bers to sic those works, and there would be no expense. (Hear, hear.) Alderman MONKI thanc.,,ht that while the suggestion was good in the abstract it did not work out so well in practice. When a visit was male to a certain dirt- merit it was some timo ago followed by apllctiooa for increases of V7,IgPP, and if Councillor William Rohorta's suggestion was carried out meinhers weull 1114 Ce fo careful what they eaid to the Bervants of the Corpora- tion. (Hoar, hear.) I Councillor ;foam EVANS replied that the Water Corn- mitten would tio very pad to see the Council there, and I the minutes were carried. GYMNASiI3II CO iIMIT CEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes, whinh were of a Formai character, were papsed, on the motion of Council)r TissioN, secomird bc_Cmneillet SHILLCOCK.</p>
        <p>COUnCilto, TINNION xela,rod tht the evenine elaeces opened on September 9,h. Tem sebools resumed on the</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHTING COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes were of a formal chary-I'er, and their adoption was moved by Alderman MOYKS, who said ir was ii.tonded shortly to ask the memb'rs ot the Council to insnect the new statim.—The motion was senridei and carried,</p>
        <p>scheme let them wait until they had the money. S) far as the inerits of tie cantilever bridge were concerned it. was not the best. bridge Goat could be had. A new bridge wuld be better, but it was simply a mater of coat. They wanted a temporary footpath because there wooli be two lines of tramways to cross the bridge. Alderman falosass It was deeided that the. Borough Surveyor should preaent a repoit as to the widening of the brldze and a‘i to the coqt. A'd:nhnan licif.To)r : We have had it before us. CauncillcT BENNETT did not think any harm would be done if the matter were defer ,d for a !wrath. .</p>
