directed to obtain certain information it was for the Council ti OPP that i• was supplied. He (the speaker) strongly imported the amendment en that eround. He did not wish it to be understood that he supported it in order to defeat the scheme C7uneiller Jonv Roaenrs said he supported the pro- vision of a cantilever bridge. He eenteneled that £3,000 was not an extravagant sum,but £30,000 waseeepecially in face of what the Corporation had already undertaken. The rates of the borough had gone up, and if they went on spending metier as they were doing it would have the rptviencv te drive trade out of the town.(flear, hear.) He remembered Councillor Tinnion saying that there were some begremmatical scholars in the Council and he (Connciller John Roberts) was afraid he was one of them. (I.,eghter.) But he thought be was none the worse a man for it. (Hear, hear.) Councillor TINNION : I by your pardon,l said nothing of the kind. I was defending the ungrammatical scholars. (Lenehter.) Counedlor JOHN ROBERTS : I did net say it was you. it was Cyanic:Mot' A J Jolley. --(Laughter). Aid, man SUTTON: It was only a 51 re, (Lauehter.) The grammar wa.a all right. (Renewed laughter. Councillor J,IIN ROBERTS said in votene for the proposition he was doing what was in the bat interests Of the rat pacers. Alderman HEATON said his reason for supporting the amendment was 'hat if they had a cantilever bridge they would he diseatieeee with it. It Would not do what they ',ewe-el from it above and below. There was a had corner on the epposite side of the brides. He knew the erimate was as low as it reeld be and float if they carried it out they wed d net be more thao four or five years before the cost would be thrown into the river. Councillor SMETHURST : What about London Bridge? they are using cantilevers there. Councillor !Wen alluded te a cantilever bridge which had been built in Edinburgh, and sail that after it had been esed for a short time the expenditure was regretted and a new bridg