ork in the hands of other people, and Mr Longdiu was only called in at the last moment, when the trarr lines were being laid, and with reference to the bridge, Mr Webster had the whole matter in his hands. Mr Longdin had not even a letter in reference to the matter until he was asked to prepare a report. The Deenrr-Meeen . You are making a speech. Councillor SMETHURST: Put the blame on the proper heads. Alderman Weirs said he hid no intenticn to blame 1 Mr Lengdin, and if he had said anything which was thought to ref Let upon him he would withdraw it. (Hear, hear.) The DErUTY•Mayort then read the following report of the Borough Surveyor :—" To the Chairman and mem- bers of the General Purposes Committee Gentlemen, In accordance with your resolution, passed on July 29th, "That the Borough Surveyor obtain a sketch design and estimate of the cost of widening the existing bridge by means of cantilevers, and that he prepare an estimate and report on the cost of widening the bridge, the Borough Surveyor begs to report that he has had an interview with Mr j J Webster, and from him has I Obtained a report and design of a cantilever bridge in accordance with the above resolution, and the report will be read to you this afternoon. Mr Webster's estimate for the cantilever bridge is £3,125. which in- cludes the road approaches to the bridge. With refer- ence to the property which will have to be purchascd,it Iwill be remembered that the Council plesed plans tor the widening of St. Jamer-street, and this widening</p>
