        <p>Alierinan FICITCIIINSIN (continuing) said there Wal ra,t a menther of the Gennep who mould sp,nd £20,00D Or ago,ooo at the ireN.mit time wired thair tinatic-s were in such an rxbauscl state, and as th,re were :so many other great Fcherns on hand affected the town so mush. Ile thought the olan provided by Mr Webster looked very well, and that the bridge would last for a considerable number of years. At the same time he saw no nbF.Ttion to drferring the scheme for another month, altriough it seemed to be shelving the matter as far as some members of the Council ,17,?re concerned. If he thdught it was the in- tention to shelve tf,e question he should vote tor the matter being finally decided that night. Councillor WILLIAM R0117.RT3 said th,t if he thouyht the object of the amendment was to she:ye the question altogether he eh mid certainly vote for toe reeolution. But he hoped they might accept the assurance of men like Alderman Monks, that the amendment was not intended to shelve the qtystion, and while at present he was in favour of the cantilever bridge, believing that for a time it would meet the rr spin. ments.of the town,he • thought the revest for a postponement of the question for a morn Wits so reasonable, and there seemed Snell a considerable difference of opMion in the Council on the qt],ifticin, that it would be better for them all, and they would be more likely to arrive at a definite decision if toe request was complied with. Os a vote being taken th,re voted r— _ . .</p>
