      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>TRAMWAYS. ORDER FOR CARS WITHDRAWN.</head>
        <p>THE TRAMWAYS. ORDER FOR CARS WITHDRAWN.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Tramways Committee held on the 21st August. Messrs Preen. and Carde v haying advised the committee to agree with the ctritractors for the construction of Tramway Nt 10 on the schedule-I rates and prices of rx;stmg con•racs: It Wag resolved that they he authorised to enter into c-tntracts with Mr Ewart and Messrs BI inkwell and Co. Limited. accord— ingly, and that the tenier of the Britigh Them rwon Houston Company, for eight tram cars at £5,055 153 be accepted on condition that the car bodies are provided with a "Birkenhead" or "tibirion" sta ce se, double I sliding doors, iron scroll work in the hand rail, arid I other improvements introduced hY Messrs Mln 4nd Co, in the new cars supplied to the Birkenhead Corpor- ation • It was farther decided that Messrs Preece and Cardew prepare estimates and report on the construc- tion of tremwars No 4 (Scotland-road aud Winwirk- street), No 5 (Winwick-streA to Longford Bridge), and No 6 (Buttermarket-street to Manche-,ter-r tad); that the attention of the (ir' IR Committee be called to the delay in constructing the tramways caused by the alteration of gas mains in Bridgr-street, and that an application be made to Parliament in the ensuing session fo- cower to construct a tramway from Staff trd-road to the Mul- berry Tree Inn, Stocktcu Heath, and for a tramway along Grapnenhall-read and :Ackers-lane to join with tramway No 11 in Knutsford-"nad. _ .</p>
        <p>- Alderman MONKS moved the adoption of the minutes with the ,xception of that referring to the acceptance of the tender for the cars. I!, was not, he said, quite wh.at the committee decided at the time, and in addi- tion there were, certain reasons which made it deirahle that they should wait a few days longer. The reas-ms were known to him and one or two other memhere,but he was not in a position to say anything publicly about it, except that it was desirable to postpone it for the present. Members would retnernb-r that at the fart meeting the Mayor took exception to the terms which he thought had been agreed to between the promoters of the Nnrthwich and Warrington Light Railway and the Ship Canal Company. What he then said had been contradicted, but he (Alderman Monk.) would only say on this coasion that notwithstanding the contradiction he was of opinion that the Mayer was correct in what he said' That was certainly the imrression of members of the committee at the various interviews with the engineer, Mr Slotter, and he toll them most distinctly that terms had been agreed to as between himself and the engineer of the Slain Canal Company. He was pressed for them, and said he was not in a position to give them, but they would come in writing. They Came in writing, and were pat forward as the terms agre•d to between Mr Scoter and the engineer of the Slip Canal Company. What the Mayor said was correct and justified. Referring to the proposed completing of further tramways inc'uded in the order, he said the necessary preparations were now being carried out. Tenders would he submitted in due course, and mem- bers would have an opportunity of criticizing them. Councillor Join; EvaNs seconded.</p>
        <p>CO11,1E111(1. BENRETT said he was extremely glad the Committee had decided to proceed with the whole of the tramways scheme. If the tramways were to he a financial success, the bigger the linage the no nos would be the profit, hty, if not he thought all who had to hear the logs should participate in the benefits to be derived from the cars. He asked if it was proposed to take the Manchester-road line to Bruche Bridge or only t 3 the Cemetery. The TOWN CLERK To the Cemetery. Tramway No, 7 will run to Bruche Bridge, but that is not yet being proceeded with. Alderman BEATON' reerring to the reaolution with regard to the delay of the construotion of the tramwar,,a in Bridge-ltreet. said tiers seemed to be some miaaoPre- hension. From the time Mr Ewart had been in Bridge- street until the present he had never made a single application to the Committee. Be ought to d 3 that before he opened the street. The DEPOT!' MAYOR: Be starts wherever he likes and tells no one about it.</p>
        <p>Alderman PotTos s,irl there were six members Of the Gas Cimmittee on the Tramways C)mmittee, and they were criticising themselves. Alderman MONKS S Ili Mr F.lcv art did nothing without the consent of the engineers (Messrs Preece and Cardew). It was not his fault, hut possibly a misunderstanding between the Borough Surveyor and the consulting engineers. That brouht up again indirectly what he had before referred to—how little williny,ncss there war on the part of the diffp.rent offiliais to work together. They were all struggliog and each going his own way, and that was not to the interest of ttis ratepayers. He wishg,l something con'd bg done, as he said before, towards having a regular roe, ting of the officials so that these matters could be properly discussed and dealt With. (Hear. hear). Alderman SUTTON : That is the real point. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>THE WIDENING OF WARRINGTON BRIDGE. CANT:LEVER SCHEME ADOPTED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Put poses Cmamittee held on the 26m Auus lr JJ Wmater attended the com- mittee and submitted a sketch plan of the proplse.d alteration of Warrington BriAge by means of cantilevers and explained the seveeal scoemea rmbrnitted by him. Ele es imateri ths cost of cmstructing the preposed can- tilevers at £3,125, and the cost of coost:u.:ting a ett.i.l archsrl hriclae of one span atone, the welent struature at £9,156. It was reoolvad that Vie plan of the pro- posed cantilevers bs aoprovad, and that Mr Webster be requested to prepare tne nene.sary detailed drawings and specficatioes, and that a portion of the Sotingfi property ne a ill to the Post Odim Departznent, as a site for a new P)st Office on the terms submitted by the Town Clerk. _</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAYOR moved, aad Alderman Bolton Eve onded, the confirmation of toe minutes. Alde:man MONKi said he would like to take exception to the resolution About the bridge. Unfortunstely he waa not able to he present at the meetinr, of the G.-,meral Purposes Committee but it seemed to him that the rear,- : lotion had been somewhat harried. He did not belis.ve that the running of the tramway would add to the in- convenience or traffic passing over the bridge. On the contrary he thought the tramways would facilitste traffic and he suggested that the matter be deferred for a mooth. to enable the members or the Council to make further inquiries and investigations and satisfy them— selves on tee point. He would therefore propose an amendment that the minutes be confirmed with the ex- ception of the minute referred to. Councillor GREENING segonded.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST did not see why the amend- ment should be satin:tined, eqp,ci illy after the matter had been voted upon three times. If Alderman Monica had been present at the meeting of the 'committeetitho resolution in favour of the bridge would have been carried. There was a determination on the part of some members of the Council to block this scheme. (" No. no.") Weil, it appeared to him to be so. Mr Webstcr bad come to Warrington and had given them explanations which were perfectly satisfactory • and the resolution to build a bridge was carried by a majority of five. He hoped the minutes woula be carried in their entirety. Alderman B +LTON said that as the seconder of the ladoption Af the minutes he wished to state that the matter had been thoroughly thrashed out at the meet- In ff of the committee. The £17,000 scheme meant nearer £20,000 and £lO,OOO to purchase property would bring it up to £30,000. fhe £O,OOO scheme vvou;d also mean additional expense. He thought that the sooner the footpath was made sufficiently wide for pedestrians —men, women and children—the better. This was a , matter of finance, and if they wanted to carry out a big</p>
      </div>
