      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WARRINGTON CORPORATION FINANCE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CORPORATION FINANCE.</p>
        <p>A Profit and Loss Account Produced "Busy Bee" has this week furnished us with the following rejoinder to Alderman Shaw Green's letter of last week:— "I did not expect Alderman Shaw Green to sup- ply the figures 1 wanted, but as Chairman of the Finance Committee he misht ask the Borough Treasurer to furnish them, who no doubt., has ttem at his finger ends. Would Al lerman Shaw Gre.n invest any capital in a concern that simply suppiied a ha'ance sheer without a Profit and loss account t? I imagine he would say that no concern was sound that set forth its affairs in that fashion and so would any other master of finance. However taking the abstract of accounts of the Corporation of Warrington for the year ending 31st March, 1901, I have ben able to make out for your readers a profit and loss account and from this statement it will bs seen that a great saving can be effected on interest and sinking fund. I have already shown how it is possible for the Corpora- tion to save £6,500 per annum at least or 91 in the .£ on tie rates and the ratepayers are entitled to get this advantage. Profit and Loss Account, 31st March, 1901 £ a. d. £. a. d. To General District By Int. Borough Rate 42570 0 0 Debt £853000 32298 0 0 „ Water Works „ Sinking fund 15973 0 0 £53,C00 ...... _13012 0 n „ Salaries Co-- Gas £196,070 8328 0 0 p oration „ Markets 5117 0 0 Officials ... 5'64 0 0 „ Baths „ Wharves parr Hall 7l 0 0 log „ Tramways „ Electricity „Museum Technical In- stitute School 1500 0 0 „ Parks, Gar- dens, Gym „ Fire Itngine 4GO 0 0 „a r liarnen- tary Bill Ac- count 1350 0 0 ~ General Ex- pemes 835 0 0</p>
        <p>67,670 0 0</p>
        <p>67,670 0 0</p>
        <p>Your readors will probably remember that they were told some years ago by the Chairman ot the Finance Committee that it was impossible to pre- pare a balance sheet cf the Corporation. Moverthe- less a balance sheet has since been Riven and now I want to show the Corporation that it is possible to have a profit and loss account. Seven years agn your readers were advised that Warrington was under-asses.ed and the agitation has resulted in the assessable value being increased from £150,000 to £235,000. This has enabled the Corpora- tion to enter upon tee electric lighting scheme and the tramways undertaking, With the rates of the borough at their present figure, the financial scheme I have advocated is worth trying and I would earnestly recommend the Finance Committee to deal with the matter."</p>
      </div>
