      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ATHLETICS</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS</p>
        <p>Rug by Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>The great game has once more commenced and War- rington have w..n their first en cowl tet friendly with Such Shields, the Durham County Gnampionship— played ou ‘Vednesday evening at 6outl Shields. Waz- ricgton took: a strong team, and lett at 8 a.m., the kick-off being at six ()clock. The game was an exce:lent one and the Sbielal men got a ustfal lesson on the in- tricacies of Northern Union footbati. Wmington won by the excellent margin of 14 points to 7 points, Fish, who was in grand form, scoring a goal and three tries, while Harris obtained the other try.</p>
        <p>The team stayed all night at th. S.apmt town. and atrivcd back at five o'clmk on. Thursday evening, well- pleased with the outing.</p>
        <p>A South Shielda correspondent sea&amp; me the following commeuts on the game</p>
        <p>The visit of Warm/rota to South Shields on Wednes- day was one of unique interest and importance to the district becausie it marked the epoch of the introduction of Northern Unionism not ouly in the townatself but , in Durham County ot which 8 )uth Shields hold the championship honours far last season. The gate suffered cmsiderably owing to an important counter attracrion, , but notwithstanding some 2 000 people saw the COM- rnencement of the match, whicA was prompt t.) time, an experience probably without precedent under the old order of things. Warrington sent practically their full strength, the back division being intac, with tl.e exception that J Allen took the place of R Bate behind the scrummage, while D Morrison and Cunningham replaced Fell, and Edmondson among the forwards. To put it briefly, the opening stages of the game com- pletely nonplussed the home supporter, to such • degree did the visitors asa.rt their superiority. ro what extent inexperience and ignorance of rho Northern Union code among tne home players contributed to this it is difficult to say, but it was undoubtedly a great factor in the matter, because after the second half the bhieldsmen , played pith greater confidence and palpably improved I skill and energy.</p>
        <p>Though ultimately beaten by 14 points to seven, the home men breto be congrat }hated on their amount of success, and the aptitude with which they picked up the new points of the game. Doe the spectators the play came as a welcome revelation and undoubtedly fete b .hind an impression which in the long tun is hound to make the new code popular in the dis•rict. This im- pression was due, no doubt, as much to the superior merits of the Northern Union game over that of the new code as to the superiority of the visiting players.</p>
        <p>It was a fine, ope.n,apectacu'ar game, with a minimum of scrimrnagiag, and a maximum of hick combination. The 'packs' were skort and sweet-5) d.ff:rent to waat the spectators had been used to—and the passing beau- Utility clean and judicicuo. The three quarters played with splendid dasti particularly Fish on the left wing .who was liberally fed and thoroughly justified it. Isherwood, too, stood cut cocspicuctp.ly and occasionally literally tore through the fiogers of the opposition. Be put many advantages in the way of Fish who was on his wing. Admittedly one of the best msn on the field was nallam who never missed a ball, while occasionally he saved under aceedinit pressure and with rare skit:. Shields did little else during the firs: half but defend their lines and mast useful work was done in this direction by hector and Deakin.</p>
        <p>There was plenty of important business at the Nor them Union Meetings on Tuesday, and some ot it was unpleasant as well as important. Warrington were fined LIO on accou it of J Fairhurst, the old forward, who had signed on for FP:toiler's and then attached his name to a Warrington pl4Fiaq form. It was as much War- rington's fault as Fairhurst's though he too is punished by suspension up to the beginning of November. 'the worst is pet haps to come. Warrington ran a great risk of losing 2 points in addition to the fine, and their fate will be ciccided when the Northern League Committee meet. It will be a severe blow and very aggrieving if two valuable points are thus thrown away before the season commences. Salford have already lost two polnts on a. similar matter and Swinton and Rucsl3t are in similar danger.</p>
        <p>Tc-day the season proper commences, Warrington opening their Northern Lague fixtures with Batley at Wilderepool. It does not seem very long since Batley were thrashing us at Leeds in the Northern Union Cup final, and the defeat remains to be atoned for. The game should be a great one between the finalists, and ' Warrington are certain to strain every nerve in order to avenge themselves upon the " gallant youths." Good luck go with the " primrose ani blue!"</p>
        <p>Other fiVures for to-day are -- Halifax v Brought3n Ringers. Salford v Brighouse Rangers. Huns'at v Bradford. L-igh v Oxalate. Hull v Runcorn. Kick-off at 3-30 p.m.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>The result of Saturday's match at New Brighton was highly gratifying to the Warrington eleven. The reyre- sentatives of the ploasure resort were zompletelv de- feated. Batting first they had all to retire for the ag- gregate of 60 and tic score of the Warrtngtonians hrd reached 133 for the loss of seven wickets when stumps were drawn.</p>
        <p>The credit for the victory must certainly be accorded to E Gibson, whose effective bowling was the feature of the afternton'a play. His analysis worked out to eight wickets for 25 rues—a really creditable record. He bowled with splendid judgment, and his varying pacej was the cause of many batsmnr,'A retirement. In ;lye successive overs he took six wick ts for eleven runs.</p>
        <p>Good fi?lding also materially assisted in the early dis- missal et the visitors, Connor and Hawtia doing especi- ally good work in thii direction.</p>
        <p>The feature of Warrington's inn'ings wag the brilliant batting of Gibbon. who piled 00 73 and carried ,ut hii bat. He bit out with great freedom, making thirteen boundary strokes.</p>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>At the annual sports if; conri,..ction with the Oldham Cricket and Bowling Club, held at the Cricket Ground, watersheddings....n Monday, A E Eloward, of Warring- ton, won the half-mile bicycle race, with a start of 75 yards, in latirk. 20 3-ssecs.</p>
        <p>Foot Racing. B Baldwin, of Warrington, competed in the 220 yards flat race at the annual sports in connection with the Oldham Cricket and Bowling Club, and gained third position if ith a starte 10 yards.</p>
        <p>Angling.</p>
        <p>To-day, Saturday, September 7th, will be decided an angling event of considerable importance. We allude to the Shield Competition at Woreley. For this contest, teams of six, representing various clubs are pitted one against the other and the team recording the highest aggregate weight of fish caught during the two hours allotted for the contest, bring declared the holders of the handsome trophy for the ensuing twelve months. S,eing that on this occasion, Warrington will be repre- sented by five teams,it may be interesting to anglers,as well as the general public, if the names of the local Clubs and the teeing representing them be given. They are as follows :—Warrington AA.: Messrs J Shepherd, 1E Hart, jun. N Unswortb, J Podrnore, jun. N Bancroft, and L Spencer. Howley A.S. : Messrs .1 Hackney, G Spencer, G Beckett, Edward Berrr , B Dribson, and .1 Berry. Victoria A.S. : Messrs E Gilbert,N Cook, J All- cock,N Bate,S Whittaker and 8 Thomas. Mersey AS: Messrs T Hayes, T Barr, F Jolley, T Cooper, E Bill- dridge and il,Sacson. Workingmen's A.S. : Messrs F Atkinson, A Fhaw, A Sherratt,T Robinson, J Taylor,anti T Hobson. The Secretary of the Worsley Canal Fishery Board, Mr G S Smeetb.intimates that this year a record number of teems will compete. However, the best wishes of the local angling traternity will go with the teams from Warrington and it may be hoped that suc- cess may attend one of the teas.</p>
      </div>
