      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SEPTEMBER 6T5,1901.</p>
        <p>T iiE Parliament The Trade Union Labour which is Congress. Session at Swansea no lack of material upon which to exercise ittl energies. The new rule under which the presiab . for the year is ex-officio the chairman of the Parlia- mentary Committee widens the whole aspect Of the situation, while at the same time it shows the tendency to get into cloier touch with Imperial</p>
        <p>Parliament as the right remedy for much that the working classes have to complain of ia the con- ditions under which tiley live and work. Tao decision recently given in the House of Lords le what is known as the Taft Vale case, by which tit. funds of a trade union are made liable as so ary asset for the expense of legal proceeditlgs' only one among many questions which await the hand of the Parliamentary reformer. It is only fair that, if trade unions will go to law,they ought to be prepared to defray the usual risks '</p>
        <p>and</p>
        <p>penalties which losers in that luxurious game ha\ to incur. But there is a very large proportion oi</p>
        <p>most trade union funds collected for benevolent p poses, and and it is against every principle of law 54 . equity:that suchfundsshould beimperilledby claiale ' arising from quite different sources. The fen° may be in the system which has failed to desir nate certain funds as devoted to benevolent par poses ; but it islprobably far more the result of s state of the law which ignores, with a fine cynicel disregard, any such designation or distinctloo made in the assets of a were trade union. So Or as that is the case the law must be altered aei I brought into harmony with the finer instincts °` 1 humanity which prevail in these latter days. must not be permissible that the funds which accumulated from contributions made in name 0: ).; ' sick and funeral allowances, old-age pensions, 01' similar purposes are to be used to defray a lawyd6 ,f bill. At the same time, trade union officials WO ' make up their minds that such funds must also be sacred against inroads for strike pay and other &amp; claims arising from the combative section of tile' r union's work. In fact, each union, if it is to be*, body corporate, would require to have a cent , fund sufficient to meet its obligations as a litigs/3° • either inclusive or independent of the funds Off strike purposes, but certainly exclusive funds for benevolent purposes. If an under' " standing can be arrived at for the proPe: designation of the various funds of tily ; unions, without requiring them to figure on tli: ; paupers' roll when they go into court, it shoul' not be difficult to find legislators willing to the necessary laws,to set matters right. Fortune : tely the congress has found out that it has littl; to look for but opposition to all that savours cla trade unionism,from the present Government sl3 0 party in power. It has been one of the hopel°6t disgraces of the Labour part/ in the past th 3 they have habitually coquetted with the Torie!' and played their game in the constitnennie:; starting three-cornered fights, as they are n°t proposing to do in North-east Lanarkshire, ri,Pee to spite their real friends among the Liberais. r result of all this the Chairman of the Congress 137, summed up.in no uncertain terms. "As a result;cl he says, "of the aggressive external policy purstl by the party in power,and.supported by a nuineriai ;later°</p>
        <p>cally small majority of the electors, such questions as old-age pensions for workm the housing of the working classes, out of gov</p>
        <p>ten a",(1 reroil36 7. beell we 031'4 , Of the '3l r rellr tlloll3° are IP wø't°</p>
        <p>considerable capital was made by the party prior to being returned to power, ha `c )usaass</p>
        <p>conveniently shelved." It is well to Lead</p>
        <p>patent fact borne home on the conscia</p>
        <p>the workers of the country. It should I to see that in politics, as well as in the oth tions of life, men do not gather grapes of</p>
        <p>or figs of thistles, and if trade -ul;ionists</p>
        <p>succeed as Parli,mentarians they will do learn and obey the rules of the game.</p>
