      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>. "eruy of the potato plant, is again upon us. For- tunately this is not an American invasion in force, but a sufficiently large detachment of the enemy has been discovered in the vicinity of Til- bury to give ground for genuine alarm. It is for- tunate we have a department of agriculture now at the Treasury, and that the Board have in this ease been fully alive to their duty and prompt in their action, not only ;n searching for and destroy- ing every apparent vestige of the pest,. but in Warning all concerned of the danger that threatens. It is a quarter of a century sit-co this terrible ecourge swept over our potato fields, voraciously devouring the potato haulm, and laying waste f locust-like whole tracts of agricultural land which ,"d been planted with the estimable tuber. So ,Ividespread was the devastation that measures of a very special kind were enacted in order to Combat the evil, and with patient, rigid aPPlication it was ultimately stamped out. 21"rovision was made by the Destructive Insects 'Act, passed in 1677, and the Colorado Beetle Order , of the same year, that all persons discovering the ' beetle should give immediate notice of same to the Police, undei a penalty of £lO. The police- Constable on receipt of such information must at 4 , once inform the local authority, which is the same as that under the Contagious Diseases (Animals) 'Acts, and the authority is then required to corn- -oiunicat3 by telegraph with the Board of Agri- culture. It is also a punishable offence, involving a penalty of £lO, to keep or sell a living specimen ef the pest in any of the stages of its life. The exceptional nature of these provisions is a suffi-</p>
        <p>.. '7r:tent indication of the danger which, in the view ' et the authorities, arises wherever the unwelcome It visitor from Colorado is found. That view will be ;ftaphatically endorsed by all who can recall the eitation of twenty-five years ago, and it is well to remind the public, and the inhabitants of agri- ultural districts in particular, that the provisions are still in force. Fortunately the discovery which Was made that specimens of the beetle had been ehserved recently at Tilbury was duly reported to the Board of Agriculture, who submitted speci- trens of tbe beetle and 'arm to their technical 44risers at the Natural History Museum at 'Nth Kensington, where they were identified as the pest, Doryphors. decemlineta. Tais fine Li tin %the which it has acquired indicates its special feature, ten longitudinal black lines down the lying cases on a yellow ground. It measures from tne-third to half an inch in length, and has reddish- . Yellow and black legs. The larwe are of an orange or reddish-brown hue, with black spots on the lower Portions; and the eggs, which are laid in clusters 134 the leaves of tomatoes, poppies and even weeds, as well as the potato plant, which is preferred, are irillg, oval and yellow. The pest was found at Til- utlrY on allotments belonging to men employed at the India Docks, and no doubt they have found their way from abroad in some vessel trading with the West Indies or the United States. The men's 151°t5 of land are fortunately in a circumscribed area, and as the whole of the crops and grass Within the area have been destroyed by fire, it is hoped that the past has been exterminated. A</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I. . :areful examination of the surrounding area has 1' te•iled to discover any further specimens ; but the greatest care will have to no exercised for some tit tne by potato growers, in order to prevent the g3dgment and propagation of any eggs or larvae Ithich may have escaped observation. The meas- tires in force have been found sufficient to stamp out the pest previously, and also to prevent its</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>. . .'ecurrenee ; and this is pre-eminently a case where prevention is bettor than care.</p>
        <p>It has transpired that the amount to be spent by the Oldham people this week on their holiday ZS .1;160,000. At any rate, that amount has been withdrawn from the clubs, and the balances which Will be returned will be small. It would not be I'- -quite fair to the Borough of Oldham, which has a 40pulation of only 140,000, to say that its opera- lives alone dissipate so large a sum. Some of the , :depositors dwell outside the radius. The interest- ' ,tri, g question is whether, with the enlargement of 'lle Saturday half-holiday which was wrung from ?II unwilling Government, there will be so much spare for the annual holiday in future years. prolongation of work until one was supposed ,„`°spo'l the chances of making a real excursion on ')aturdays. The operatives had to go home, to tat, to wash, and to change their clothes, and by that time it was near the stroke of three 6.Cleek. Now, according to the Lancashire embers who pleaded so earnestly for the : concession, the worker will be able to go afield on aturday afternoons. This means the spending ..2f Money. Blackpool and the Isle of Man may ve reason to regret the change, though it is : !Mite certain that the factory workers who visit ,zhese places might do so with more economy, and u` their own better advantage. After all, how- aver, these factory hands are such splendid workers ! that it is easy to understand when once they are 1 iree they are tempted to treat themselves and .'their friends generously. Health is their prime ' asset, and in seeking it they do not stint them- ' I:elves. If they can be encouraged to go more •] alto tha fresh air at ordinary times—even when .. ithere is no football match to watch—it will be far better for them. So after all, the wider distribu- ''tion of the money will be for their benefit.</p>
        <p>There is an engaging article in one of the Inagazines this month on the subject of artists' ,Inodels. It contains no reference to ladies of the '.rilby type, but concerns itself with the faces ;Which have been made familiar to us in pictures of the imagination or so-called historical studies at !he Royal Academy. The disclosures carry with 'hem the thought how much fact is needed for the , '3levelopment of the fancies of the mind, and how necessary it is to improve on fact if a good repre- sentation is to be made of the events of past times. , What a brilliant conception ! says the visitor ,wcnen he looks at some allegorical picture. .k '•'the ever did the artist think of those faces " ;he answer, of course, is that the best were 4'laPted from life, and you may see the photo- graph of the subject and compare it with the 2., Ailful adaptation. There is the young lady with the well-proportioned features, and the carriage of 'ne head, and freshness of expression, who has been " requisitioned "by half a score of eminent three strange may see herself in some two or ,P, ' ree strange likenesses at a single exhibition. tal nere is the weather-beaten old man with a cer- fl of toil and age about his face, and ._"`lere is the dear old lady who touches our senti- lAent in so many phases—as the aged mother nsiting the return of her soldier son, as the widow looking out on the sorrow-striking ' and as the reader of a letter from a far-off an, d, the good news which her eyes permit her t tlly dimly to follow by the aid of her old-fashioned Pectacles. All these are familiar types. They ave to be varied, and, as the dramatic artist ve°llld say," made up."</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Newton constable's bravery was fittingly re- the:d.ned last week by the presentation to him of -Exoyal Humane Society's medal and diploma. `-'• Clarke, the officer in question, performed a flarly plucky feat in rescuing a boy who had While bathing in a deep clay pit. With his ;rra on, Clarke waded into ten feet of water, Ed the boy with his feet, and then with Irlee got him to the bank and brought back to life by artificial respiration. for the officer's promptness and knowledge rabulance work, the boy would certainly have his life. Mr Gillespie, the presiding magis-</p>
