      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>trate at the Newton Court, complimented P.C. Clarke on his bravery and said he felt proud of him as an Englishman. The officer's modest reply was that he "only did a policeman's duty." Such conduct as this increases our respect for the men in blue, who are often enough not only prevent ers and detectors of crime, but heroes as well. They all have a high conception of duty and for that alone we can admire them.</p>
        <p>In another column we publish a report on the tuberculosis question, which has been submitted to the Health Committee of the Warrington Corporation by Dr. J. Guest Gornall the Acting Medical Officer. of Health. It is a most interesting and instructive essay on the causes and preventives of tuberculosis or consumtion, and should be thoughtfully perused by every innabi- tant of the town. Just now tuberculosis is engaging the attention of medical men to a greater extent than any other disease, and there is hope that most of the mystery surround- ing the tuberc/e bacillus may be solved, and con- flicting opinions mado one. Dr Gornall has studied the question closely and his observations are valuable, while the sum total of his suggestions on preventive measures as summed up in the phrase: "Sanitary reform rather than bacteriophobia,"is one based on practical common- sense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Lady Amherst's seasonable comments in the last number of the " Gentlewoman " have a special interest for many people of different trades in the provinces. Dealing with Queen Alexandra's recent suggestions for the encouragement of home indus- tries, Lady Amherst shows that an immense quantity of lace, ribbons, satins, velvets and embroideries will be required for the Coronation, 1 and observes very properly that such things 89 will be wanted are not made in a day. So that it will be well for leaders of fashion not to keep manufacturers too long in the dqk as to what will be expected of them. The C)ronation Service, it has to be remem- bered, will simply be the first of many great func- tions held in honour of the King and Queen in all parts of the country, and not in London or the metropolis alone ; therefore, the demand upon manufacturers, especially, is likely to be very great. Lady Amherst offers another timely and important suggestion, too, which ought not to be overlooked, to the effect that the good work thus begun will extend beyond the limits relating to dress, to the advantage and for the benefit of trade generally in all parts of the Kingdom.</p>
        <p>As relating to the general licensing sessions which have just been held, a singular instance of red tapeism may be mentioned as having occurred at Torquay. It appears that since the amalgama- tion ot the neighbouring districts of Cockington and St Marychurch there have been no public houses in the Torquay Petty Sessional Division; but notwithstanding the fact that there was no business the county justices were summoned to II attend the annual brewster session, and same of I them did so attend. They were then told that they were further bound to adjourn the session and fix I the days for the transfer of the licences, which, of I course, ha I no existence. What would have I happened if the licensing justices had refused to be thus "utilised "is not recorded, and may perhaps have been ,too awful for those concerned to con- template; no one could have blamed any individual J.P. among them if be had recalled the line in Shakespeare ending with the words "Write me down an ass."</p>
        <p>• Another matter that has cropped uptalso in some sort of connection with the licensing ses:ions, probably is that of the difficulty which travellers experience in obPaining the kind of refreshments they require at railway stations, hotels and way- side inns. A correspondent says he called for a cup of tea at a certain railway station, and was curtly told that the "kettle was not boiling" This may have been deemed smart on the part of the fair Hebe, but the traveller would not accept it in the place of business. He had to wait an unconscionable time, however, for the "water" to boil, twenty minutes he says, before he could enjoy the cup of tea. It not only is " not business," but it is not treating tha travelling pub- lic as they have a right to expect. Travellers call- ing at railway refreshment rooms, or at hotels or inns, are entitled to whatever falls within the meaning of the word "refreshment." Cases frequ- ently occur, however, in which parsons who may have walked some distance, or travelled in some other way, are refused "reasonable refreshmsnt" because what is asked for is not what the inn- keeper likes to sell, and the latter is not what the traveller cares to order. A licensing justice re- I marked from the bench the other day that next year he should oppose the renewal of every licence the owner of which said he had nit the means to provide for both "man and beast," or was proved to have refused to make such provision. That is the way matters appear to be tending with the licens- ing authorities, and it will be bad for those in the trade who neglect to read aright the "signs of the times" in this direction.</p>
        <p>It appears that the brewers are at last beginning to take the hint. At any rate an agitation in their ranks,caused by the refusal of so many applications tor new licences by the licensing justices has led to the convening of a meeting at Birmingham, and described as " influential," at which it was "unanimously resolved to net to- gether in one body.in such defensive action as circumstances may demand." Not a very alarming threat on the part of the great brewers of the mid- lands, certainly, but one that indicates the way the wind is blowing. At the Birmingham Brewster Sessions on Friday close upon thirty applications for new licences wrnie submitted to the licewing justices, nearly every one of which was refused. This, and the tendency manifests(' in the rare direction previously by the magistrates, started the present movement among the brewers, the pro- gress of which will be regarded with interest in other parts of the provinces.</p>
        <p>Worcestershire Standing Joint Committee have declined to petition the Ilome Secretary to exempt the county from the operations of the Truck Act' in the matter referred to in this letter three weeks since. To bare yielded to the prepo,terous request of the farmers to allow them to continue giving cider in lieu of wages to their labourers, would be to belittle the law, and render the authority of the committee contemptible! If the cider given to the men were guaranteed of the purest quality, some- thing might have been sai4 by members of the committee in the farmers' favour, but even in Wor- cestershire there is cider and cider, and that which Mr Rider Haggard discovered on his recent visit to the country was stated to be a kind of "mixture of steel filings and liquid mud." If once the right of farmers to sail round the Truck Act were ack- nowledged every other employer of labour in the rural districts might be expected to put forward a similar claim. Therefore, the committee acted wisely in adopting the course they have, which will tend to protect both the po Aet and stomach of the labourers, as well as reduce the amount of drunkenness in the county.</p>
        <p>Like unto the Colorado beetle that was found in a Texan cattle bolt at Liverpool in 1887, the "insect " found during the present week thriving on a potato patch at Tilbury, and busy as ever spreading his destructive progeny around, has been duly "nipped in the bud," as Sir Boyle Roche would have said. Thus a serious injury to the potato industry in this country has probably been averted. English cultivators have no fancy f,,r the American experience of this pest, which has been known to destroy whole crops of potatoes, and almost ruin an otherwise thriving and prosperous country-side. The British growers' immunity is certainly due to official as well as their own carelessness. This kind lof beetle is popularly believed to be more dangerous than a pro-Boer, and it cannot be made too widely known that no one is allowed by the law to keep one even as a specimen to show to his friends.</p>
        <p>It will gratify many readers to have the assur- ance of Lord Aveburv, as set forth at tlie meeting of the Associated Chambers of Commerci this week, at Nottingham, that trade is not languish- ing to the extent generally reported. His lordship took quite a cheery view of British trade prospects and atter alluding to the fact that last year's total of exports and imports formed the "largest volume of commerce ever transacted by any country," urged that there was no need for our manufacturers to Le discouraged. With this there will be many ready to agree. Pessimism never helps anybody,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY SEPTEMBER 6 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY SEPTEMBER 6 1901.</p>
        <p>and there is not the slightest reason to suppose that British enterprise, which has done so much and yielded results so splendid in the past,is played out. At the same time, it would be wise not to be over-sanguine and to not go on in the old grooves in a leisurely and self-complacent fashion, simply because our statistical aggregate is as yet unap- proached. If analysis be made of the igures, and comparisons with previous years are entered into it will be found that there is ample ground for the belief that our position can only be maintained by the most determined efforts of those concerned in industrial enterprises, namely, employers and workmen. In the natural course of things there is every probability that the rivalry of toe future will be far more severe than in the past</p>
        <p>FUNERAL Or MR GEORGE BRIGHT.</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoon, the mortal remains of Mr George Bright, of Rainhill, brother-in-law of Superintendent Pearson, late of Runcorn, were interred at Runcorn Cemetery. The deceased was the manager of the tailoring department at Rain- hill Asylum, and was held in high esteem by his collpagues and workmen. He had held the position for over 21 years, having formerly resided in Shrop- shire hie native county. He succumbed on August 26th after a few days' illness.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN ROPX HALL MIS3ION'</p>
        <p>I On Wednesday evening at the abave hall, a fruit banquet and social was held in aid of the purchase of brass instruments for the band associated with the mission. The fruit, vegetables &amp;c., had been contributed by the members of the baud and at the time appointed quite a goodly number sat down to tea. Games were afterwards indulged in the M.C.'s being Messrs Brown and Strange. The refreshment stall was presided over by Mrs Gatley,and the ladies ,in charge of the tables were tne Misses Holland, Moores, Dutton, and Hazelhurst, The ladies responsible for the decorative part of the proceedings were Mesdames Jones, Walsh, Gat- ley, Moores, Find low, Brown, and the Misses Out- run and Holland. on the proposition of Messrs Jones and Stringer, a vote of tkanks was passed to all who had contributed their quota towards the success of the proceedings. The amount realized was £2, which will be devoted to the baud fund. Mr Alfred Jones, missioner, superintended the general arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WIDNES LICENSINC RET.IIIMS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES LICENSINC RET.IIIMS.</p>
        <p>At the Licensing Sessions tor the Kirkdale Divi- sion held on Thursday, at the County Sessions House, Liverpool, Superintendent Keighley, Wid nes submitted a repert with regard to licensing end other matters in the Widnes portion of the Kirk- dale Division. The number of licensed houses in this portion was stated to be 43, and the popu- lation estimated on the 1891 basis was 13,414, giv- ing an average of 312 per licensed house. The num- ber of persons proceeded against for drunkenness showed a decrease of 65, but this was stated to be due to the depressed condition of tbe shipping trade. All existing licences were renewed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SACRILEGE AT HALTON.</head>
        <p>SACRILEGE AT HALTON.</p>
        <p>Onpuesday, August 27th, it was discovered that the memorial window for the late Mr John Wesley Haddock which was put up by the pariAhoners in 1893 had again been wilfully broken for the third time during the past 16 months. It is supposed that the damage was done during Monday night, which was very stormy, and it was on a stormy night that the second attack was made last Janu- ary. The matter is in the hands of the police, and it is hoped that they will be successful in discover- ing the culprit. Great indignation is expressed throughout the parish at this wanton act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>RESCUE FROM DROWNING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>RESCUE FROM DROWNING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Friday morning, an accident attended with serious consequences, occurred at the Old Quay Lock, Runcorn. It appears that a man named George Radley, ot King,treet, Runcorn, was assist- ing to get a flat into the lock, when the winch handle, belonging to the lock gates, came off, and , caught him on the side of the head, knocking him into the Ship Canal. Fortunately the lock master and Mr William Byfield were quickly on the scene, and succeeded in rescuing the man with a boat- hook. He was conveyed to his home, and attmded by Dr Robinson. The man is now suffering from pneumonia, and as yet the doctor gives but faint hopes of recovery. - THR, SALVATION Ana-r. - -</p>
        <p>_ The work of the Salvation Army in this town has established its claims to the attention of the public by its permanent reformative work. By its meetings, outdoors and in, nightly, in all weathers the Salvation Army provides its people with ample opportunity to seek to rescue others frcm the same careers of waat, sin, and crime. -seeing that this service is voluntary, it represents in our midst a sacrificing force, making for temperance and righteousness of which the community ought to be proud. The annual harvest festival thanksgiving celebrations take place from September 7th to Sep- tember 9tb Every meeting will be special, and a display with music, songs by the children, costume demonstrations, etc. will be provided. Toe officers request us to announce that contributions in money or goods will De thankfully received at their Quarters, 2, Rutland-street, or by any Salvationist. Visitors will be heartily welcomed at any or all of the meetings advertised. RUNC3EN TECHNICLL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Once again testimony is forthcoming as to the efficiency of the educational facilities of Runcorn. John Joseph Stubbs, aged 17. of 6, Norman-road, educated at the Runcorn National School, where be pined a Connty Council Scholarship entitling him to attend the Organized Szience School at the Tech- nical Institute, has now g'sined one of the County Scholarships in Class 111, of the maximum value of £5O a year, tenahle at Owens College, Manchester. There were five of these scholarships offered by the County Council. and Stubbs ranks fourth out of 21 competitors, with a total of 293 marks and a per- centage of 745. Re has bsen a student of the In- stitute Day School during the last three years, and his recent success is eloquent testimony to the value of the inqruction given under Mr T A Lawrenson. DI A, B Sc. the headmaster, and his capable staff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ALARMING FIRE NEAR WIDNES.</head>
        <p>ALARMING FIRE NEAR WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A little before six o'clock on Saturday a firs was discovered to be raging in a Dutch barn, belonging to Mr Richard Allen,fariner and contractor (a mem- ber of the Widnes Borough Council),a; Crow Wool. l!he barn contained one by stoked with hay. another with straw and a third with oats, and shortly before the outbreak Mr Allen had himself been on the scene but had not noticed anything un- usual. Shortly afterwards the fire broke out near the roof of the barn,and made itself very evident. The Widnes fire engine was summoned, and on arrival good service was rendered by Mr flenry Beegley and eight men who came with the engine. Valu- able assistance was also rendered by a police- sergeant and eight con-tables, but in spite of all efforts to save the stock and narn,it is estimated that some £1;00 worth of damage was done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Bog's NATAL FALL AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>A Bog's NATAL FALL AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>' Coroner It Dobson, hell an inquest on the body of a little boy, Arthur Woodward, aged 6 years, the son of William Webster Woodward, 4, Union- street, Runcorn. From the evidence adduced it appeared the boy went to play near the Dolph Quarry with another lad named Alfred Woodcock. Tuey crawled under the railings and on to the head of the Quarry. Woodward then seiz ad a piece of heavy wire rope and tried to touch the bottom of the quarry with it. Either it was too heavy, when paid out a little, or it must have slipped out of his grasp. Descending to the quarry bot- tom with a rush the rops caught the little boy's tionFera and pulled him over. He was taken home in a semi-comatose condition, and died the following Sunday about eight o'clock. The height to the top of the quarry from where the little boy was found would be about 35 feet —Dr .1 B Delaney testified that the boy was braised all over, and had his shoulder partly dislocated, but the chief injury sustained was concussion of the brain. Inflamma- tion of the brain supervening,the boy died. He was of the opinion that nothing would have saved his life.—A verdict of Accidental death was returned. ACTOR ACCUSED OF THEFT AT WintrEs. On Mon day morning at the Widnes Borough , Police Coutt, before the Mayor (Alderman George , Ingram Neil), an actor named William James Maxwell, belonging t 3 the "True to the Queen" Co' which visited Widnes last week, was cuarged with stealing a gold ring, value 16s —Mrs Mary Cain, 6, Witt-road, Widnes, stated that on luesday, August 20th the defendant came to her house and engaged two room, for wujoh be said he was satisfied to nay is per week, One of the rooms that defendant occupied was previouely used by witnese's daughter, and in one of the drawers she had left a gold ring (produced). In consequence of what she was told, witness became suspicious of her lodger, and at the end of last week went to see if the ring was safe. It was gone and she gave information to the police. The ring Produced was thA property of her daughter, Mary Ellen Cain. The prisoner remarked that the ring was not in a drawer but in a box on the top of the dresser.— The Mayor (to prosecutrix) : Didn't you think it was very foolish of you to leave your jewellery lying about like that ?—Witnsss : No, I- didn't think the man was like that—The Mayor : Then take my advice. Don't leave your jewellery lying about in strangers' rooms.— p c Rutter deposed that at 10.15 on Saturday night be went to the theatre, where he saw the prisoner. Witness asked the defendant to let him see the gold ring he bkil been wearing. Defendant replied that he had got no ring and knew nothing about one. Witne,s told defendant that he would have to tale him to the police station, hut before he did so he would search him. Defendant then produced the ring from one of his pockets, and when witnes charged him with stealing it said he had only taken it" to make a show at the theatre " and intended to replace it.--Defendant, at this juncture, said he was guilty of taking the ring but not of the inten- tion of stealing it.—At the request of Superinten- dent Keighley, defendant was remanded until the Oh inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACRER3. CRISP AND CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>DEATH OF A PROMINENT RUNCORN FOOTBALLER</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A PROMINENT RUNCORN FOOTBALLER</p>
        <p>, The public will learn with deep regret of the sad death of Mr Joseph Nickson, the popular captain of the Runcorn A teani,which took place at his residence Hitth- street, Runcorn, on Thursday morning last about. 10 30, at the early age of 23 yaars, Mr Nickson's death was attended with remarkable suddenness. Ho firet exhibited signs of ill-health about six weeks ago, but his ailment was of so slight a nature that his friends and himself paid little attention to it. He was able to take his usual place in the foot- ball practices of his club, which commenced about a month ago, and exhibited all his cild form, and appeared to be enjoying the best of health. Accompanied by Houghton, and several of his footballer friends he attended the celebrated Shrewsbury Flower Show, but on his return again com- plained of illness. It was not until Wednesday, last, however, that he was compelled to take to his bed, and on that date he lay down upon what ultimately proved to be his death bed. Ho was attended by Dr McLeanan, Dr B etloune (Dr McDougall's assistant), and_ Dr Larkin (Liverpool), but notwitbs'anding all that medical skill and money could do, that virulent disease—typhus —carried him off. lie leaves a young widow, but his two children have pre-Jecease,d him. As a footballer " Joe" was one of the most popular norm in Runcorn, and figured in first class football from about 9 years ago, as undertudy to the two invincible backs Jolley and Faulkner. He was in all resp:mts the gentleman of the team, and it is recorded by his m ates that they never knew " JO9 " to use a wrong word ; whilst his achievements in the field were such as to always call forth praise, and many is the time when he has even exhibited form superior to even that of Jolley or Fau:kner, a notable instance being wham he scored two tries against Oldham about 6 years ago. He was unassuming, quiet, and never allowed his popularity to carry him away. Sam Houghton, his captain, has no hesitation in saying : " Joe was as good an all round roan as there is in England to-day, and could fill up a blank in any position of the team." Not only was Nickson good as football but he could also hold his own at cricket, bowls, and various other @ports. His loss to the Runcorn Football Club is a severe one.</p>
        <p>The funeral will pnbably take place on Sunday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>FOOTBALL PROSPECTS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL PROSPECTS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Speculative, and very much so! is the exela- relation one meets with when gleaning the football prospects for the ensuing year. All seems to hang on the success or non-success of the "New League.' Where "Jack Smith" (who knows a thing or two) has written so much, it would be invidious for me to say anything. Yet, notwithstanding, I feel dis- posed to back my opinisn that the " New League" will be an unqualified success. Of course, like all true sportsmen I deeply deprecate the degeneration of sport into a business, but that is not how the matter really stands in these go ahead days. fo give the subject its true significance it wou'd be best described as "a business to provide epart ;" and, granting that true business men are at the helm, there will be little to fear as to the issue. More particularly, I desire to deal with Run- ; corn's chances of success in the new ven- ture, consequently I hasten to the task. They are immense, magnificent,aand shou:d culminate with Cheshire at the head. Some people—and I add, with deference, kind-hearted generous TPople—tell me candidly I know nothing about Rugby. With them I do not disagree, although to be thoroughly as candid, I am in duty bound to say they know le,s. But to the p3int' of my story. what I do know about Runcorn and Run- corn Footballers, " Veteran," "Old Mar " and half a dozen other popular writers—(who, if they press 1 right once in every half-dozen " tries " at predic- tion, crow something alarmingly—have been ing to dud out for half a centtiry. I know the men individually and collectively, and have even analysed their component Farts. That they are "Cheshire burn, Cheshire bred ; Strong th' arm ; weak i' th'_yed,'' . . . .</p>
        <p>- has often been the description applied to them. bY the satirical critic ; yet, that vary critic too often forgets that other teams might even be lacking in more ivalities than the Cheesemen. How do we find them on the advent of a new seagon, and in face of the first match in a new struggle, and who are they. By answering the last question first, the ladies will be put out of their misery and need not turn to the end. The team picked for Hull, oa. Saturday i.s as follows: Houghton. back ; Inglesfield, Richardson, J Butter- worth, T Warder, three-quarters; J Faulkner and J halves; S Walker. W Lightfoot, RF'ece, Woods, F Gayter, H Farmer, Tomlinson and Mes- senger, forwards, with J Langley as re.erve. The other team to meet Fletcher Russells (Warrington) at Runcorn includes:— T Wilkinson,back;G Urmson, H Jones, T Parr, C Liesketh, threequarters; G White ' and R Brown.halves; W Bettlev,S Langley. J Dixon, J Williams, W Houghton, J Higin, C floaghton, and W Pool, forwards; with E Wilkinson reserve These ar3 but 32 of the men who will be called upon to achieve new fame and uphold the prestige established years ago by many who are now almost forgotten. _</p>
        <p>fo'r the men themselves ! Houghton is quite his old self which is a great deal to say of one, who though young. is really old at the game. He is in good form and has taken great care of him- self during the vacation. Butterworth and Warder the two old three•quarters are ready to co mmsnce with muscles and limbs well set, a steady course of training having been maintained ' during the summer and they will he found quite up to standard Richardson and Inglestield are two young bloods, who gave considerable assistance to the team last year, and baying their reputation to make can be relied upon. The fact that the 3 appear in places usually occupied by Wilkinson and D Jones is sufficient guarantee that the Selec- tion Committee have great confidence in them. Faulkner and Jolley the halve.. though not possest— ing the colour of (White and Brown) their juniors have a lustre and brilliancy entirely their own, both feel fit and know full well what is expected of them. Our for wards are a sunerb group,uushers all, just the fellows for the job. I don't quite agree with the selection for the first team as being the beet, but I feel for all that they will serve the club well at Hull, Walker is all right; Farmer, too, can account tor his share of the work if he will, Reece and Tomlinson on the other hand could not do bad if they tried ; for they are always full of spirits. Lightfoot and the staid Fletcher Gayter. have a record, and rejoice in my full confidence' Woods and Messenger are practically the youngs- ters of the team, and as both displayed good form towards the latter stages of the past season, they have been entrusted with the honour of appearing at Hull. I don't wish to discourage either audio- stead of saying what I fee', perhaps it will be suffi- cient to remind them that J Langley who is their reserve, is still their suparier,whilst S Langley who figures in the "other team" is beyond doubt the most useful forward with his heels that Runcorn has ever seen. Knowing they play for two such men as these is something to be proud of, and they may be depended upon to figure at their best. The sad death of Joe Nickson, the popular captain of the junior team, which occurred ou Thursday last, is however the one serious loss the club has sustained, and will have a saddening effect on the team. The finantial prospects of the club I consider in safe hands. for Runcorn people are dearly proud of their premier team, and provided they do the play- ing, there will be plenty to do the paying. Summed. up, Runcorn has a more magnificent prospect than it ever had, and enters the arena with a past history, reflection upon which is inspir- ing ; with a present harmonious and well grounded (the sources of men and money being well founded); and with a future the crowning glory of which should result in a champagne banquet and the " Honours of the League."</p>
        <p>When asking for °vat. insist on having Caner/RV% sold only in Paokets and Tins. as other Cos $ areoftect substi toted for the sake of extra profit—Advt. (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>BOWLING HANDICAP AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>An interesting handicap was concluded at the Wilson's Hotel green, Runcorn, on Saturday in which moss of the best players in the neighbour- hood took parr. The winner was IT Farmer who started from scratch, defeating .3 Threlfall (three start) by five points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>FISHING TqATCII AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>FISHING TqATCII AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr Fred Abel toted as captain at an interesting fishing match in the Bridgewater Canal at Run- corn, on Wednesday afternom. Sport was not very brilliant, yet some excitement was caused by the catches and an enjoyable time spent. The match resulted as follows : (1) S Bailey, Regent-street ; (2) I Clare, Regent-street; (3)T Plant, High-street; (4) 1 Worrall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>CROWD AT TRE SERSIONS.</head>
        <p>A CROWD AT TRE SERSIONS.</p>
        <p>On Monday, the troubles of the rate-collector were admirably illustrated before the Runcorn Bench, when some 101 familiar faces appeared with the quite as familiar excuses for non-payment of the poor rate. Needless to say the Bench were not popular with the large crowd who became the recipients of the usual " order." IMPUDENT WATERCRESS MEN_ AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Thomas Finn, and Patrick O'Brien, watercress men, and well-known characters at Runcorn, were summoned before Messrs Imieon and J Ockleston, on Wednesday morning for being drunk and dis- orderly on the previous evening.—Sargeant Gill proved that both men were creating a breach of the peace by using foul language, and that both were drunk.—Ttp-re were 19 previous convictions against Finn, and 42 against O'Brien.—Prisoners were fined 103 and 53 costs eacb, or 14 days' bard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>HALTON-ROAD SUNDAY SCHOOL PICNIC.</head>
        <p>HALTON-ROAD SUNDAY SCHOOL PICNIC.</p>
        <p>A vary pleasant party, coosisting of about thirty teachers and officials in connection with the above school, went on Saturday to Mouldsworth, on the confines of Delamer° Forest. The party were con- veyed in one of Mr Booth's conveyances from Halton-road at 1.30, arriving at their destination at about 3.30, after a most beautiful run throu:,..h the heart of a charming country. After many interest- ing games had been indulged in tea was served at the "Station Hotel." The return journey was made in good time, Runcora being reached at 10 o'clock. The secretaries responsible for the arrangements were Messrs W Graham and Mall.</p>
        <p>TI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S CHOIR SErtmoNs,_ RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHOIR SErtmoNs,_ RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above place of worship, the annual choir sermons were preached on Sun- day by the RSV Alfred Wells, superintendent minister of the circuit. At both services, morning and evening, there were good congregations and the discourses were learned and interesting. The anthem entitled, "Oh worship the King," wa'a given with much interpretive effect, the Solo by Mr J Leathwood being very delicately rendered. Mr T Johnson was very succeisfill in the well- known hymn " Sun of my soul." " Clan your hands "and " Glory to Thee," were splendidly exe- cuted by the choir, and the solo parts, taken by Miss Carter and Mr E Kniveton, were sung with spirit and taste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>EDUCATION FINANCE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION FINANCE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The forthcoming School Board election at Runcorn gives great interest to all figures with re- gard to eiticatiov, and the school accounts for the year ended June 30th last afford itotructive read- ing. For the three board schools, Victoria-road. Mill-brow, and Granville-street, the total annual grant was £1,122 54. and the fee grant £522 103 For the four denominational schools the annual grant was £1,781 Os 3d, and fee grant £Ol6 23 6. The board schools received from the rates ..£7Ol 124, and the aid grant to the voluntary schools reached £O4B. The principal expenditure at all the schools was salaries. At the board schools the principal teachers received £572 73 23, and at the voluntary schools £1,030 Os si. All other teachers received at the board schools £1,437 7s Gi, and at the volun- tary schools £1.900 Is 91. The total receipts at the board schools were £2,411 133 33, aid at the vol no- tary schools £4,849 33 7d. Voluntary contributions to the voluntary schools totaLed £lBl 13s 7d. THE RUNCORN MALE VOICE CHOIR,_</p>
        <p>On Saturday the above choir journeyed to Prescot to take part in one of the annual musical competi- tions, open to male voice choirs. They'. were strongly reoresented but unfortunately they missed carrying off the coveted trophy by an ace. Taeir opponents were very powerful choirs some of the strongest musical talent being arrayed against them, the prize falling to Mr Berry's " Excelsior " choir, St. Helens. The adjudicator in giving his decision said that he was carried away by the sing- ing of the Runcorn choir, the bass was excellent and the blend of voices was good, the rendering throughout being worthy of the highest praise. Un- fortunately there was one part in which the choir failed otherwise the prize would have been awarded to them. General regret is felt in Run- corn musical circles that the choir has not suc- ceeded in bringing tbe prize home,and an additional attempt is to be made to strengthen all round the different parts. It is not generally known that I some short time ego the choir won a distinguished mark of honour at Huyton. Mr E Yates conducted , with his customary ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>HEN'S B/BLE CLASS Ar WESTON POINT.</head>
        <p>HEN'S B/BLE CLASS Ar WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>At the Weston Point Wesleyan Chapel onlinday the re-opening of the Mon's Bible Class took place under the presidency of the leader Mr II Dodd. After devotional exercises he congratulated the members on the success which had attended their efforts. They had started with only five members and be was pleased to say that they had now an average of 40. there being an exceptional gat kering of 60 present that afternoon. The tnusicaPportion of the service was very much appreciated. Mrs.l Ellison was very successful with "I alone the cross will bear " and Mrs T Leach rendered sympathe- tically "Sometimes we'll understand." The quar- tette party, consisting of Messrs. J Nall, T Watson and Mesdames Leach and H Dodd, were responsible for" There is a city beautiful." "The Soul's Dream of Paradise" was sung by Mr F Ellison, and Mrs HI Dodd brought a very appropriate prozramme to a fitting conclusion with "floe harbour Bell." During, the interval an interesting address was given by Mr T Leach, Runcorn, on" Comparative Religions" in which he showed th t while duty was the main- spring of all heathen religions love was the founda- tion of Christianity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of this body was held at the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Thursday morning. There were present :—Messrs R H Poscett (chairman), W Handley, G Raison, T Jones, J H Salkeld, D Bisbrown, J Sweetman, H J Grice, J Wilson, S Walton 'l' Dean, J Pritch ad, with Mr F A Lake (law clerk). Air E Mar. shall (secretary), and Dr F McDougall (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER.-</head>
        <p>THE MEDICAL OFFICER.-</p>
        <p>Mr. Posnett remarked haw pleased they all were to see Dr McDougall back again after his recent illness and hoped he would be fully restored to bealch and strength to enable him :o continue his good work for the tewn, (Hear, _hear.)</p>
        <p>• Dr. McDomrall : I thank you very much for the hind expression. lam getting stronger, and hope still to be able to do my best for the town. (Hear, bear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>TECHNICAL SCH3OL</head>
        <p>THE TECHNICAL SCH3OL</p>
        <p>Mr Itnison thought it would be of interest for the Councii and the puhlic to know that one of the students at the Technical School had quite recently added both honour to himself and to the Institution. (Hear, bear). The elder Stubb., a boy at the school, had at the last meeting of the County Technical Instruction Committee been reported as having gained a scholarship entitling him to a place to be chosen by himself either at the University, Liverpool, or 0 wen's College, Manchester. The scholarship entitled him to the repayment of expenses such as railway farce or other expenses to the extent of £5O a year. He thought it was something that the school should be very proud ni. (Hear, bear). Stubbs was educated at the National schools, and secured a County Council scholarship from that place ; and had now gone a step further. Indeed, be thought it was the first instance of the third rune in the ladder being reached by one of their boys, and a proper subject to bring to their notice. (Hear, hear). Mr J Wiison said he waggled to hear the announce- ment. They would watch the progress of the boy with deep interest and he felt he was voicing the opinion of the council when he said they only hoped it would prove thn stepping stone to something greater and better. (Hear, hear.) He was very pleased to note the efficiency of the education given in the school, and was sure the staff would be pleased to hear the news. (Rear, hear). Mr IV Handley wished to correct Mr Imisen on one matter. He thought young Mr Bagiiley who secured a 3 years agricultural scholarship at Baogor was the first instance of its kind. Yet it was pleasing to find they had another who had also attained a like distinction. (Hear, hear.) It showed the teaching at the school was of a very high class. Mr Handley then drew attention to the fact that young Mr Stubbs having been appointed as a laboratory assistant at tbe school, naturally a vacancy would arise if he decided to follow his scholar- ship, and proposed that the committee having such matters in hand, should be emplwered to make a new appointment. This was agreed t). THE " PHOTO" OLLLERY.</p>
        <p>Mr Imison intimated that the pianos of Mr Shaw, Mr Dobgan, and Mr 9 Taylor had lama added to the " gallery," and wished to put on record the Council's appreciation of their past and present service. (Hear, hear). _ _</p>
        <p>, The Chairman intimated that the Secretary, on behalf of tho Council had suitably acknowledged the gifts. The Council then resolved itself into Committee to consider the" Baths Qlestion."</p>
        <p>"fECHNICAL IN-ST RUCTION IN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>CHESHIRE. School Board Evening Classes:</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE. School Board Evening Classes:</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Technical Instruction Commit- tee of the Cheshire County Council, hold an Friday last the School Boardsofor Bramhall, Gangleton, Dukinfield, Macclesfield, Mon Ron, Runcorn, Stalybridge, and Over, were empowered to carry on Evening Clagies for the period of one year from July 31st 1901, and to apply to the maintenance of such c'asses. such a sum out of the School Food, as may be necessary to maintain the same upan the scale of operations required for the year nand. Grants for the you 1901.2 for technical in- struction work in the districts under their control, were made to the f -glowing Boroughs arid Urban Dis- trict Councils, which have themselves levied the penny rates for the Fam9 purvsgs : --Crewe £Bl2, Dukinfidd £365, Hyde £632. Macclesfi,ld £668, Alcrincham £324. Bollingtnn £lol,Hovlake and West Kirby £250, Middle- wich £9O, N srthow'ch £330. Runcorn £3lB, Sale £255,</p>
        <p>Wallasey £1209. W.ricf ncl £2OO. A grant of £39 is was made to the North wich Tech- nical School, and one of £ll 93 to the Sandbach Techni- cal School, to meet similar grants raised by a volunta,Y rate by Mosora Brunner Mond and Co.. in their works and paid overt, these inetitutions. Grants were also made to local committees. viz : Bromborough Higher Behington £22, Eliz,l Grove £2B,and Marton £6 to carry out schemes of classes submitted and approved. A sum of £6OO was granted to the Governors of the Caldav Grange Grammar School to aid in carrying out alterations and add,tiois in tee school buildings.</p>
        <p>WARRiNGTON St. Barnabas' Bible Class.</p>
        <p>The roembers of St Barnabas' young ;en's Bible Class had their annual outing on Saturday, Black- pool being the resort visited. The party which was accompanied by the Vicar (Rev JH G Holing- worth) left Bank Quay Station at 8.35, and arrived at its destination shortly after 11 o'clock. Throug- out the day the weather was beautifully tine and an exceedingly enjoyable time was spent. The Tower and otner places of amuQement for which - Blackpool has gained such a reputation were freely patronizA. The return journey was commenced at seven o'clock-, Warrington being reached shortly before ten.</p>
        <p>School Treat at Daresbury. The scholars attending the Board Schools of Daresbury, Hatton, Preston Brook and Moore had their annual treat at Daresbury last Friday after— noon. A field adjoining the school was kindly placed at their distiosal by Mr Joseph Billington„ and swingboats, cocoanut stalls and otter amuse— ments had been erected for their pleasure. The children from the Infant School at Hatton were conveyed to Daresbury by Mr R Warburton, those from Moore by Mr Dunbabin, and those from Preston Brook by Mr Henry Whitlow. An excellent ten was partaken of in the school, to which 315 sat down, After tea the juveniles again adjourned to the field, where obstacle races,jumping and other sports for money prizes were engaged in under the superintendence of their teachers. Donkeys for the chil•lren's amusement formed a new and attrac— tive feature ef the fete, the Vicar kindly defraying the cost. At dusk the usual packets of sweets and cake were distributed to each, and another enjoy— able afternoon terminated. Thanks are due to the donors of the treat and to all who in any way aaisted in promoting the children's enjoyment.</p>
        <p>The New Mission of S Hilda.</p>
        <p>This new mission in the parish of S James', and situated behind Mersey View. Enutsford-road, Latchford. will be opened on Thursday afternoon , next. The preacher for the occasion will he the. Lord Bishop of Chester. A service will be held in the evening when the Rev W II Binney, MA. vicar of Witton, Northwich will preach, The following particulars respecting the Mission appear in ti. James' Parish Magazine for this month:—"Thanks to the hearty and willing co-op- eration of many friends and workers the Mission building for the recently extended district in the east end of S James' parish is now ready for use It is one of the largest missions in the neighbour- hood having in addition to a good reading-room and two vestries accommodation for about 350 per- sons. The chancel at the east end is well-propor- tioned, and admirably fitted and furnished. When the mission is used for purposes other than those of divine worship, this chancel is screened off from the rest of the building. A large platform at the west end will then be found useful and convenient. The total amount expended on the land, building, fittings, etc.,is up to the present £536 10s. But this sum by no means represents the actual value of the mission. For first, the materials out of which the building has been chiefly constructed were pur- chased at an exceptionally cheap rate, about £65 only; and secondly, from the architect downwards a very great deal of voluntary work has been given. —work which £lOO would inadequately represent while thirdly, numerous handsome gif;s of furni- ture have been made by kind and interested friends. And yet, in spite of all our efforts which have suc- ceeded in raising about .£386, we are still about .Cl6O short of what is required. earnest appeal is made for the speedy liquidation of this debt, so that unfettered and unhampered the mission of 8. Hilda may fulfil the most sanguine anticipations of those 1, who are praying and trusting it may with God's help prove the means of blessing, uplifting and brightening the lives of those in whose midst it is placed."</p>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions. WEDNESDAY.—B,tjore 7tiesers G Comber (in the chair). John.</p>
        <p>While and Colonel rildgWay.</p>
        <p>_ NEIGHB3IJR9 AT VARIANCM. -John asmpbell.Soapery-row, Paddington.was summoned for assaulting Freaerick ['rigger. his next-door neighbour, on August 24th.—Ialr Steel repre- sented the defendant.—Complatnant stated that he was 'standing at his gate when the defendant name boost drunk and challenged him to fight. Ifs (complainant) told him to go home, and he thereuuon struck him on the left side of the head, nearly knocking him down —For the defence It was stated that for 1.8 months complainant had been in the habit of scanda- lizing defendant. making allegations which the defendant denied. The case was dismissed—Campbell was then charged with assaulting Sarah Jane Trigger, wife of the com- plainant in the last ease on the same date.—For the defence it w.s stated that When defendant received his summons In the last case. Mrs Trigger went and acquainted all the neigh- bours of the fact. to the great annoyance of Campbell, He admitted assaulting her.—The magistrates said defendant was not justified in assaulting the woman and fined him 10s and costs.</p>
