      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>SCIIOLiTISHIPS•</head>
        <p>SCIIOLiTISHIPS•</p>
        <p>Scholarships were awarded as flows :—Major scholarships to enable students to attend at University Colleges for thres years. Bsrtram G Fawcett of Maccles- field. Henry Nuttall of Stalybridve. John Chester Cowan of Wiusford, Join J Stubbs of Runcorn, and Mabel G Holtrins of Sandbach. THE AnT SCITOL&amp;RSIIIP tenable at the Royal School of Art, South Kensington was award(d to Margaret Bris- tow Hughes of Li..card. EVENING ScnoLansures IN SCIENCE SUBJECTS.—JAmes Henri" Ward of Hyde, Tom Andrew of Stalybridue, Alfred Beard of Woodley, George Lees of Hyde, John Hardman of Bredbury, Joseph Wilsn of Hyde, John Edwards of Fivde, George Lnver of Firde, William A Oldham of Hyde. Alex. Liugard of Godl-y, Ja11199 H Grant of Hyde, Harold Scrag of Flandforth, Harty Poulson ot Crewe, Hnory Darling of Crewe, Walter Burrows of Stalybridge, Alfred P Coke of Eg,remont, Jam Marsland of Stalyhridge, John W Wiswall of Run- corn, Woq!ey etude' of N N 1099. Artlur R,rrarzh of Davenport, Frei Sesior of Dultinti.id, Jo4eph Hugoes of Crewe, Alexander Dinwiddie of Hartford, and James W Hill of Runcorn.</p>
        <p>EVENING SCIIOLARSIIIPS FOR COMMERCIAL SUBJECTS.— Orlando Oldham of Newton Hyde, W 11 Firth of Saiy- bridge, Ethel Mary Travers of Dayenttam, and Charles Runcorn of Crewe. SCHOLARSHIPS TO PUPIL TEACHERS FJR SCIENCtI, ART OR COMMERCIAL SUBJECTS —Mary Elwarda of Willing- too, Jessie L Moore of Willington, Annie Bateman acd Selina Kettell of Whea;ock, Christina ISAtomlev of Millbrook,Hilda Chorlton and MargareoiDua.lop of Staly- bridge, Amelia Dohtion and Mary Lomas of Marpte, Margaret Pate of Rostock, Annie Stretford of Weaver- ham, Emily B Edwards of Sandi way. Violet. Fisher of Sandiway, Georgina Sampka of Northwich, Frank Fowles of Winsford, Alice Hill of Weaverham, May of Blsodon. Mary Pionington of Boston, Fred G Wett and Fred T Q than of Saitney. Hobert Stewart lard Florence Sykes of Altrincham, Sarah Dodd and Ettie Phillips of Winston. PREPARATORY SCIIIIARSIIIP3 FOR SCIENCE. ART OR CONMERCIAL SUBJECTS.—Marie L McDonald of Malpas, Jlart Thomas Ardern and Herbert Shaw of Disley, Cnarlotte M NaTlor of Hople,Oliver Middleton and John of ‘Vinsford, Maul Chitwell of ‘Vcwod f ord , Allen Harrison of Fr olsham, Amy G Griffiths of Wren- bury, Agnes Foster of Sandiway, Alice Marshall of finutsford. The award of the Evening Scholarships in Art SuhintS was deferred</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. D. BISEROWN, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Oa Saturday, at the fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn ions of Temperance, held in Greenway- road Schoolroom, there being a goodly number of members present, an interesting presentation was made to Mr Daniel Bisbrown, treasurer, whose con- nection with the Order in the town extends over 24 years.— sir Thomas Heesom presided, anci was called upon to make the presentation, which con- sisted of a framed emblem conraining the following inscription :--`• The Order of the Sons of Tem- perance, Runcorn Life Boat Crew Division, No 195. To Brother Daniel Bisbrown, treasurer. We, the officers and members of the above, beg your accept- ance of this emblern,as a small token of the respect and hrgh esteem in which you are held by us, and lin recognition of the valuable and faithful services rendered by you to the Order, both as a member and an officer during - your 24 years' membership ; and trust that you may long be spared to continue your career of usefulness amongst us. Signed, on behalf of the division. Thomas Ileesom, W P; Alfred Noden, WA; Thomas R Whyte, A P W P; Thomas Cooks, P W P; George Powell, W C ; Samuel Tomlinson,W Chap ; Eetgar Vt ilkinson, I S; Tames Fowles, 0 S ; Edward W Key, S V; Thomas Clarke, SV; James Johnson. F 8; Thomas Dutton, A F 5: Thomas .1 S 31st August, 1901." Mr Bisbrown was also presented with the September number of the official organ of the Order. "The Sou of Tem- perance," containing bis portrait, and a short sketch of his life. ln making the presentation,Mr Heesom eulogized Mr Bisbrown's work in connec- nection with the Order and temperance matters generally, and hoped be would long be spared to continue his usefulness amongst them. 24r Alfred Noden followed with a few well- chosen remarks, which were endorsed by the following members : Messrs Thomas Holt, James Johnson, Thomas R Whyte, Edward W Key, Samnel Tomlinson, Thomas Cooke, and Messrs William llazlehurst and John Brown, of Weston— Mr in acknowledging the gift,expressed his warm thanks for the kindness and honour con- ferred upon him by the members of the Order, kindness which he should never forget He valued the gift very much more than he could express in words, and said that on every possible occasion his services should be at their disposal, as they had been in the past. He resumed his seat amidst great applause.</p>
        <p>DOlTGL!S.—Pierton Ifouse..6, aarence-terrace, Ceram I Promenadr. Facing the sea tea. bed and breakfast 3s 6d Private Stting room, Miss CORLET-T, Proprietress. pLACKPOOL.—Mra 3,1CK30N, 10. Brenswick-street LI booth Beach : near sea, Central Statiod, arid amuse ments : APAR ENTS ; bed and waiting for twN Zs daily piano._ _ BLACKPOOL. Apartments ' • home comforts, with or without board. Close to Central Station. sea. and all places of amusement. Piano.—Mrs if sid. and DAVEY, 34, Hornby rOad.</p>
