      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A_DRUNNEN AND NEGUECTFULFATIIER.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Curt on Monday tTe.fore Mr. Bircball and other magietrates,John Dunn, 44.M4rgaret- street was charged on a warrant with neglecting his four cnildren in 6 manner likely to cause them unneces- sary sufferieg and isjury to health—Mr Browne pro- secuted on behalf or the N.S.P.O.C. and stated that Inspector Hackett had bad the man under observation for some months. Prisoner had had every ropportunity of improving but instead of dcing so had done worse Whenever lospector Flackett went to see him he Said " You do give goud advice. I will try and follow it" but he did not make the slightest amends. He told his wife that if another summons came to him for neglecting his children he would murder hr r and the children and afterwards cut his own throat.—Mary Dunn. the pr;sonet's wife, said her heals ind was a puldler at Dallam Forge and earned 30s a week. He gave hrr vari• cue amounts ranging from 7s 61 to 14=,and ha sp-eat the rest ot the money in drink. He was.scarcely ever sober, and often ran her and the children out of the house. Ode day he brough"r his ti ten year old son borne drunk —Mrs Ferber, mother ot the last witness. said her daughter and the ehildren had frequently come to her house in the early hours of the morning. Sne had supplied the family with food.—? S Evans spoke to arresting the prisoner early on Sun lay niernine. He found three ot the children lying asle.p on the hearth rug. Prisoner was ail. ep on the sofa. The rooms upstairs were very de- y an there was scarcely any bedclothing• Prisoner's wife had constantly made complaints of his conduct.—Mr cßrowce stated thal the woman was of clean habits, and the dirty sta'e of the rooms was due to the fact that she had lately been living with her mother.—lnspector Hackett op Ike to visiting the house and finding toe priaoner la urci at mid-dac. He was lying in the bed with his dirty clothes on, juat as he bad come from work. Be had visited him from time to time since and had often seen him under the influence of liquor. Be had not found above two outcNi of food in the house on any occasion. The chat ren were emaciat- ed through want of food and were very poorly clad. The eldeen bey was suffering from a large bruise in the middle of the back, which he stated had been balloted</p>
        <p>tip his father,—Prisoner, who had previously served terms nf imprisonm,-nt for ceglect of family, was corn- mitted to gaol for two mooths with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>MINOR OASES. Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Mr Birch*ll (in the chair), Alderman Heaton, Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs. Thornton and S Wright. DIAL-ITREET'S REPOT ATl3N.—Mary Mullen. 12, Fennell- street, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Dial-street at five o'clock the IN evious evening.—The Chief Constable int .rmed the magistrates that he had received a memorial from the property owners In the neighbourhood ttating that they would have to make representations to the Watch Comrnittea if the unusual disorder la Dial-street ne'ghbourhood conti•merl. Of course the magistrates would know that the reputstion of Dial-street was no thing ef the last. decade—Prisoner was tined 5s and casts MIRCHIEVote Bore —THRoWING SAT&amp; IN THE RIVER.— Charles Hallwocd, 63. Green-street. Sidney Dale, 32, Mill- lane, and George Buser, 34. Mill lane, to.ys, were sum- moned for corrundting wilful damage to setts, the property o Monk., Hall and Company, by throwing the same in the Mersey on August 24th.—Thomas Hough. a watchman in the employ of the fir n. stated that on Saturday. August 24th, he was on duty on the work's premises. The setts were kept on the wharf and he saw five bays including tha de- fendants, throwing some of them in'o the rly-r. The other two bays were too young to be prosecuted. The value of the setts thrown Into the river was about 10s. PC. Woods stated that he had been informed that when the water was low the set.s in the river did damage to the propellers of 'Warners. The buys were ordered to be confined In the cells until six o'clock. BREY.TIEs —Thomas Ho lgett, coal dealer. 4, Pnitel- s*:eet, was fined 5s for wilfully obstructing P.C. Ilduskett while regulating the traffic in Knutsford-road on August 20th hy refusing to stop his horse and lorry—William Style, Smith, 43, Bewsev-road. was summoned for siting a bier e without a lighted lamp on the night of August 22nd. He sal,l th 3 night was very boisterous and his light must have only just gone out. wheo the offl3er stooped him. ';:he Chief Constable said it wits a particularly rough night.—The Case was dismissed</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Aldermen Sutton (in the chair) and Hutchinson. Mr Birettall and Dr Fox Manual:Fa, OAKES AcitlN.—AY INCOHelGiutot OFFINDER. —Margaret Oakes, Dal-st,e-t,was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-atreet os the previous night - r C Cooper said that he was on duty near the Central Station when he heard the prisoner, who was drunk. maaing use of dreadful language. He told h,r that he would he compelled to lock her up and she said. "I'll find you a j ib."—He got the assistance of P C Bennett, and they had a 'rough time" with her on the we' to the Police ntatinn as she was extremely dr lent.— The Chief Constable : All that I can corroborate. She was more like a fiend than it woman. 'this is her 25th appear- ance, and the fifth time within 12 months, for various offences, she is one of our most persistent Inveterate repeaters. livery effort has been made to reform her, and one of the magistrates formerly took a great interest in her and would have sent her abroad if she had shown a disposition to give up her di.solute ways. She prefers however, to wallow in the sink of iniquity which appears to he a congenial sur- rounding to her. She is a perfect pest to the streets, and she is rapidly qualifying to enable us to send her to an Inebriates' home. Perhaps', the next time she comes here we may arrange to get, her away for three years. She only came out of gaol yesterday, and she has, therefore, lost no time In gamin here again.—She Was committed to gaol for a mocth. SYMPATHETIC MAGISTRATES.—On the Case of William Hall being called the Chief Constable stated that the man was brought before the Court a week ago charged with being drunic in the street. stv.d he was locked up for hi, own pro- tection. He had been lode off from a ship at Runcorn. When arrested he was in a pitiable Condition. He had be-n to the Workhouse for a week and was now better, and he ael:ed the magistrates in the clicumetances to discharge him.—The Bench consented.—The defendant, tnformed the Bondi tisat he was All right again." He had been a cook on board a ship and wished to make his way to Manchester. Shortly before being discharged from the ship begot 'wet through:and took the drink to warm him." —The Chairman : The Bench advi e you to have hot tea in future and not spirits. However, we sympathise with you, and we have decided to pay your railway fare to Mats- chest' r.—Mr Taylor, police court missionary, promised to Drovide the man with a pair of boots and to see hint cif at the railway station.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MoNnaY.—Before Messrs J..Ocklemon (in the chair) and J.</p>
        <p>Pritchard. A CrixEcxxxxiors Ox.rAcrott,—Jobn Dunbavand applied for a certificate of exemption from vaccination for his child Eva.—Applicant stated that vaccination would be prejudicial to th health of his child. One of his children had already suffered in cor.sequence of vaccination—The application was granted. IieEACLI OF TFIE PEACE.—Two young men named Daniel McLean and William Whittaker were summoned for com- mitting a breach of the peace by fighting with each other.— The latter ciden. ant dici.not appear being represented by his liowtands stated that at 9.45 on the 24.,1a august the two defendants were lighting on the South Parade, Weston Point. and were causing a great disturbance.— McLean was bound over to- six months in two suretlts of £5 And the charge Against Whittaker Was aejournad for a fort- night in order that he might appear. A IN EISA CE IN IN ATERLOO-ROAD.—John Thompson,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>MAKING FACES IN THE STREET</head>
        <p>MAKING FACES IN THE STREET</p>
        <p>Mysterious Affair at Tottenham. A story, venial may be piaced to the category of the marvellous, c =es trom the Tottenhatn herald." A small business is carried nn at 154 Olyde-road Tottenham (L mdno), ty Mr and Mrs Bygrave, with a family of several children. Lilian Alice, daughter to the worthy couple, aged fourteen, received a [right at school, which led t extraordinary results. The mother said, "Lily used tv make such tams az times, at d suffered so much from St Vitus' dance,that CU:laugh we were grieved we frit we must laugh. See had eviaently lost the use of Ler right side as if oarilysed. She could not eceo cut her tucd ,or feed herself, and sbe was unabk to speak properly. She hccirrie awfully ttia, and when she tried to wailt her legs would often give wai, under her. I have kouwn her to f,ll four times between here and the corner of the road."</p>
        <p>The rill was present whilst the mother was speaking and noticing that she bad now a thoroughly healthy appearance, the interviewer inquired how it was sLe came to look so well. Mrs Bygrave said, " I read of cures effected by D- pink pills for pale People and purchased a box of the pills for my c;iild. By the time she h4d taken this first box her speech had become clearer, and she cauld walk much better. I continued to give her the pills. After another box had been emptied she was stie to use her knife and fork again, and when she bad finished a third a.,cl a fourth box she was as well as anyone cmid be. She is always ready for her fond—in fact she eats more than the others.</p>
        <p>Mn Bygrave was full of tb-ankfulness at having found a remedy which couid cope with a disorder ordinarily so hopeless. Dr Williams' pink pila. wlfch by enabling the system to get good from food taken, have cured an enormous number of cases of debility and indiges'ion, frequently stop St. Vitu3 dance, and even fits as if by magic. They are a tonic and they make new blood. There is no other secret about them : Buh- -1 atitates do no good, browse they are not Dr Williams' but a cheap imitation got up to look like ;ham and give a large proll". Two shillings and nineoence sent to Dr. Williams' medicine company. Holborn-viaduct, London, will procure the genuine Ole by return of post: but honest retailers will not worry their customers with sub3titutes. Paralysis, consumption. rheumatism, scia- tica, ladies' ailments, and skin alfactions have been cured by Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people. For the languor and fatigue which annoy so many they are a specific.</p>
