      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Inspection Ball. The annual inspection ball wa4 held at the Drill Hall on Monday ev-ning. Dancing to music sup- plied bv t,e bind of the battalion commenced at elght o'clock and was continued until two in the morning, about 250 guests wer,3 present. Colour- Serg,o.ut Chef.) had the charge of a temperance refreshment bar. The general arrangements were carried our. bv the nom mit.ee of the Lzergeants' Mess and the President, C,lour-Sergeant Moore,efliciated as M.C. LAWNROWBES GROUND AND REPAIRED. Green Inlv. era slwavis in stnelc—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger Bridge-stre*.t VVarrinitton —Arivt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Accident to ,a Cyclist.. •</p>
        <p>A cyclist, nam-d Peter Jones,3s Atherton-street, Hindley Or-en, nesr Wigan, was riding his bicycle in Winwick-rcal behind a waggonette on Saturday night when he attempted to pass the vehicle. Un- seen by him an ice-cream car: was coming in the opposite directi n, and, unfortunately, the cart and the cyclist collided. Jones was thrown to the ground. and on xaminatian it was found that he was suffering from Revere internal injuries. He was taken to the Infirmary, and detained as an in- palient. BEET BOOTS von ALL.—ITBDBR Tae CLOCK.—Ad</p>
        <p>NASSINETTES AND MAILCAItT3 in latest Art Sbades —J. S. GlataND,sl, 53 'lnd 55.13 ridge et. Warrlngton.—Advt</p>
        <p>P.S.A. at the ym CA.</p>
        <p>An open rneating of the Young Meo's Chris?iin Association Pieasant Sunday Afternoon So"iery was held on Sunday. A service of song. entitled " Eva," was rendered by the Hill Cliffe Baptist Choir, under the conductorship of Mr. George Nfoss Mr W. Hellas presiding at the organ. The choir, which has been carefully trained by Mr Percy Randall. organist of Trinity Church, cave some excellent renderings of the vocal part.. The reader was the Rev.) S Hughes. Mr W Clegg (president) and Mr Harry Taylor also took part in the service, which was of a hearty character.</p>
        <p>PUBLI C BRNIIFIT UDR THE CLOCKFoR BBS I? J COTS xic L lOWA Slitltli.—Adst</p>
        <p>W. T. WILLIAMS SALE is now proceeding. See Windows for Bargains in MILLINERY. %lANTLe. F COATS, DRESSES. BLOUSES ke..lllM—Sankey-Btreet, and Legb-street, Warrington.—Advt. Farm Fire at Stretton.</p>
        <p>A fire broke out in the yard adjoining the farm of Mr. Kinsey, Stratton, oa Sunday afternoon. It originated in one of the stacks and message.s were at on:e despatched to the Wariington and Wilders- po3l fire brigades. Both brigades turned out with promptitude the Warrington fi,ernen taking with them the steam engine "Major" Fortunately the firemen succeeded in confininz the dimes to the stack. The Wilderspool Brigade having pumped the pit near which the engine was situated dry. re- turrA home at ten q'clirk, but the Warrington Brigade did not leave until half-past two on Mon- day =ruing. The stack of hay, of about 20 to 2.5 tons. w.s almost entirely destroyed. The Cease of the fire is unknown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>UNDER TRE:CLOCK roR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</head>
        <p>UNDER TRE:CLOCK roR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>AEIK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL ATI:HR.—Advt. Warrington Oddfellosis at Manchester.</p>
        <p>A nutnher—of the membere of t-h-E;-W---a-riin-iit-o.n. dis- trict of the Independent Order of Oddfellows visited Manchester on Saturday in responce to an invita- tiol from the Wellingtoa Lodge No. 1 Manchester dietrict. Oa their arrival at the city the officErs of the lyige and district g4ye diem a hearty wel- come. The whole party then had the pleasure of a drive through Trafford, Baguley, and Rusholmu, returning to the headquarters of the lodge, the Cheshire Cheese Hotel, Deansgate, where an excel- lent tea was provided. When the cloth was re- moved the Manchester Deputy Grand Master, IS ro E Beeyer took the chair,and was supported by 13,0 Dickinson, D Bro E Pester,Grand Mastar ; Bro J W John..on. D 0 M (Warrington), Bro J Hewert- son and Bro J Myers (Manfthester). The Chairman said they were very pleased to have the Warrington brethren again with them, and tra,ted that tbeie pleasant by-visits would always continue. Bro Pester thanked the members on behalf of their %Warrington brethren, and invited therm to visit Warrington next year. The invitation was cordi- ally accepted. Feceral toasts were submitted and songs contributed, a pleasant evening being con- chided in time to allow the visitors to catch the 10 43 train for home. DnrECTIvE VisloN.—Don't delay bnt send at roes for</p>
        <p>rsnnhlet cm the new treatment. Astounclinir inccess ! 27,0U0 Cm, already curet 1 I MELLOR, Bye s pectalist, 286 Oxford-street, Manchester. —Advt (2) Fairfield Cricket Club.</p>
        <p>The President (Mr A E Boardman) presided at a meeting of this club which was held in the club- room at the King and Queen Hotel on Monday, and there was a good muster of members. The Secre- tary (Mr. Norman yield), in giving his report for the past season, stated that 17 matches had been played, and that 11 had been won, live lost, and one, their last match, had ended in a tie. This, he Stated, was a very kood record,and considering that the teams they had met were better than last season's, and that they had only lost one match more, showed a great improvement. Mr Ernest S. 'Haigh also presented his report. The officers for the season 1902 were then elected. Mr A E Board- man was re-elected president, and on his retaking the chair which bad been occupied by Mr Ball during the election, his health was drunk with musical honours. The other officers elected were: —Captain. Mr George H W Steel: sub-captain, Mr ft G Brandreth; hon. treasurer,leir Ernest S Haigh; and hon. secretary, Mr J W Travis, Marsh . House- lane. The following were elected the committee: —Messrs A E Boardman, G H W Steel, it G Bran- E S Haigh dretb, , J W Travis, N Nield, and J Ball.</p>
      </div>
