      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>FRE EXA INEP, FRI A SEPTEMBER 6 1901.</head>
        <p>FRE EXA INEP, FRI A SEPTEMBER 6 1901.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>SOUTH-EAST LANCASHIdE LEAGUE</p>
        <p>Swinton v. Rochdale Hornets.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire season was opened on Monday even- ing at Swintln. The Swinton team was not as strong as usual, as R Valentifie was unable to play at tbr quarter, whilst Lewis was missing from the same aiviaion. Morgan was also away at half, and Hampson was sent to partner Davies. The Hornets had an experimental seam, and there ware many new players in the fifteen. There would be 3,000 present when a . start wag made. Swinton immediately pressed. The Hornets offered a stubborn defence, Allen being very prominent. Swinton were pressed, a dead ball resulting and in turn they again penned their opponents. The Hornets, however, ptayed up well, and Turner ran in a , very clever try, no goal resulting. A minute later Wallwork equalised. ,The sides were equal at half-ime On resuming, Swinton open up a strong attack. Millwork ran splendidly down the touch, al:l(l'th* home combination improved generally. Long and faulty kicking threw two or three chances away for Swinton, but eventually Messer got an opening, and with • a dashing run scored Sseinton's second try. No goal resulted. Soon afterwards Wallwork added another try for the home team. The point was not improved, and near the end 10,aor1ey made a mistake, whin,. let Gibson in. From this Rigg kicked a goal, and Swinton won by-ona point. Swinton, three tries (nine points) ; R;chdale Hornets, one goal and two tries (eight points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>Warrington v. South Shields.</p>
        <p>Warrington played South Shields their first Northern Union match at South Shields on Wednr sday evening. The fixture was a friendly one, VVarringtnn winning by 1 goal 4 tries (14 points) to 2 goals 1 ;LT (7 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>A meettog of the Northern League was held at the Spread ENC:O Hotel. Manchester, on Tuesday evening. The action of [6O honlrary secretary, in his corres- pondence with the Yorkshire Itsferess' Society was Can. Ormed. Mr W Eidisoa's nannevit his own request, was deleted from ths relerees' list, and that of Mr T Kidd added in the former's pl . . . .co.. .</p>
        <p>A resolution was car;ied to the effact that the secre— tary of each club in membership with the nether% League should be instructed to send a copy of his Com- petition register to all the clubs in the League, and also to the Ltncashire Sanior Comp2tition, on or before September 14.</p>
        <p>The gurgle° of the registration of Mr A M Ricketts, the den,etary of the Halifax club, as an ant iteur, was referred to the jurficliction of the nrtteru Union G,neral Cominitcee.</p>
        <p>Toe following players were registered to play with tbe clubs first namea</p>
        <p>Hunslet—R H—lihs. des (K Aghloy), Walter Colby (local), Chas.,Thornpstm (Lady Lane Mission), S E Allen (Leeds Farhat) Cnurcb), T Dickinson (local). J Handforth (Woodville), and G Milbura (Wbitehaven Rees.). Salford.—John Kenny (Pendlebury), Jas. L 11211411 (Bromley), 11 Price (Wok !field), Thos. Pinches (Adelphi Lads), and Herbert Dixon (Outwood Church). w eton—N Fit zo &amp;trick (Broughton Recreation), E Ooodwin (Walkdee), A Wilkinsm (Pendlebury), and Harold Forster (Poncheton Britannia). Broughton Rangers—Thos. Horrid ( rreberbert), 8 Whits. (St Bartholomew's), and G Croats (Irlarn and Cadishead). Bradford.—J Holmes (Bingley Trinity), J R Turner (Shipley), E Harrison and E Andrews (Mstiningham), and R Johnson (Featherstone). Oldham —W Monkhouse (Abbey Hills), C E Dixon and 11 Topbam (Bromley). Leigh.—W Jones ( fyidesley), T Arrowsmi,h (Leigh St- Joseph's), T Riley (Leigh Shamtocks), W i•nton (rYldesley), W Carrington (Leigh St. Joseph's), and W Naughton (Leigh Shamrocks). Warrington.-0 Purslow (local), H Hewitt (Latchford Rangers), A Taylor (TyldeelEy), Ernest Ball (Trinity), F Meacock (Latchford Rangers), W ?Trees (St. Helens), and R Baxter (Warrington Ringers). Runcorn.—E Hesketh (Halton View), and W Poole (Runcorn Recreation). Dsvitt (Halifax Crescent), Themaa King (Queensbury), L France (Ovenden), J W Balmer (Sowerby Bridge), R Knowles (Salterhebble), and A T Brown (Cardiff). Fluldersfield—T W Dyson (Flockton), G H Raye (Melthsin), F Walker (Lf,ecla Parish Churcb),R ssclin- son (West Hartlepool), T Coldwell and Martin Rogers (local), and H Moseley (St John's). • Huh—Hugh Fulton (Askam),A Hainbrecbt (41rnley), and S Lewis (Askam). Brighouse Rangers-11 Wallace (Manninghatn), R T Strotha (13,eorley Wood Clarence), and E Whiteley (Sowerby Bridge). 1 Tne following players: were ordered to be struck off the registrar : winton—U Holm,. Broughton Rangers—ll Brown and T McCau'ey. Warrington—G Brown,T Garrity, G Cross, C Povey, A Potts, and R McLeod.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Salford Lose Two Points.</head>
        <p>Salford Lose Two Points.</p>
        <p>The case of El Barnett, of Altrincham, ended in a somewhat sensational m inner. Tae Salford Club sent in an application to register this player, but were faced by an objection from the Altrinchain Club. It was found that a breach of Be. law 22 had been committed by the Salford Club, aud it was decided that two points be deducted from whatever score they make in the Northern League table during the ensuing campaign. It was furth-r 01d9red that Salford give back Barb's transfer to Altriocharn on professional form B. . _</p>
        <p>-.71;;-t-roubles of the Roy Borough Club did not ter- minate here, the Cunmittee having next to consider a complaint from Bramley to the effect that Sa'ford had registarid John Joce3, of Aberavon, although that player was on Brarniry's Competition register. As,how- ever, nn arranerment hld yet b-en arrived at heterren the Yorkshire Senior Competition and the Northern Lea.ue with regard to no itters of this character, the Committee dicided that the Salford Club were quite in order in so registeri:•l jonea. In future the meetings of the Committee will be held on the first Tu3sday in each month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Hunslet and Warrington Fined.</head>
        <p>Hunslet and Warrington Fined.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the Union was held in Manchester on Tuesday night, Mr J El Houghton (St Helene)in the chair. Thera were several chareos against yers,and the following was the official report In hioases of Svinron and IVarrington, they will be crieptbohl a reported to the Northern League at their nest meeting. The Swinton Club and H Barnett, late of Altrincham, having been reported for breach of Professional Law 2, the COMMit tee decided that Swinton be fiued one guinea and Barnett be censured. wavingtoe Club and J Fairhurst were also reported for breach f Profesional Law 1. The Committee de- cided that ;Varrington be fined £lO. and Fairhurst was suspended up to and including Novemlyzr Ist. Mr Brierley, of the Thchdale Rovers, was charged with illegally tampering with a professional form, and he was severely censured. R:chardson was charged with having signed two forms tor Whitehaven Recreation and Partoo under 7 different names, and he was suspended up to and in- cluding January 1. Pirton Club were fined one guinea for broach of Professional Law .</p>
        <p>_ The alteration of the Y,rkshire Senior Competition by-laws was approved. The by-lawi of the Lancashire and Yorkshire combinations were approved. roe proposed rut's of the Gentral Lancashire League were approved, subject to Rule 11 being struck out. The Salterhebble Club were elected to membership. The application of W Fairclough (Wigan) to, rein- statement as an amatru:. Was left over pending his attendance. The question of support ta junior foothill WB9 adjourned until next meeting. The Cases of the Hunslet and Siddick Clubs were under consideration, and the cams of Story and White. the two Siddick players. II was fould—(1) That the Hunslet Club hr sheen guilty of branches of Professional Laws 1 (Sub-Section IS), 2, and 5, and that they be fined £lO ; (2) That Stott, and White be suspended up to and including October 1 ; (3) That the Siddick Club has been guilty of breach of P;ofessional Law 3, and they be fir ed one guinea.</p>
        <p>, The carrognoridenco from gull Marlborough Ciub ro the taking of gate money at a practice much during tbe ease season, Was adjourned until next meeting.</p>
        <p>Associated Chambers of Commerce.</p>
        <p>The autumnal meeting of the Associated Cham- bers of Commerce is now!being held in Nottingham. The Warrington CRamber is represented by the Secretary (Councillor Bennett.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>GOLF IN THE *ENGADINE!</head>
        <p>GOLF IN THE *ENGADINE!</p>
        <p>Men's Uharnpionship Won Again by</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to learn that Mr Arthur H. Cros- field is well upholding the honour of the old country in the realm of sport at the Eaacline• He ha! recently for the third yEar in succession carried off the mzfe golf championship played at ..ama- den. roe " Alpine Poet" of Tuesday contains the following accoum of tiie 'play in the championship tournameut,:— •ENGADINE 410L1? CLUB. ANNUAL CHAMPIONSHIP MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Annual Golf Meeting at Samaden which has just been brought to a close augurs well for the future of the game in the Engadine. In every res- pect it has been a thorough success. To begin with the Links, thanks to Dr Patterson's constant rind unremitting attention and the judgment and pains- taking care devoted to them by Mr Withers, Mr Lloyd and himself, are in better iirder this year than ever they were. Two or three putting greens still leave something to he desired, but they are the exception and will be put right before next year ; for the others and the lies through the green, how they would make the authorities responsible for our , best Links at home parched,yellow,and baked bard at ti .t time of the year, sigh with envy ? What would they give for the fine mountain grass of which there is such abundance nere ? The putting greens of first rate sea-side Links may be Equal, but are in no wise supelior to the pick of these Alpine greens. Fiat though the tarnaden Links are—like some of our best Britiih greens—they are none the less sporting and interesting all through, and marked by that sime qua non of a good Golf Liolts—a character of their own. frees, streams and dry water-courses form the hazards, nor has a single art ific at bunker been needed.</p>
        <p>It we turn to surroundings, where shall we find a parallel ! The glistening summits of the" palaces of nature" are in full view, and almost overshadoi us, with dark pine-clad hills for a middle distanc Very lovely all this in fine weather ; grander still when storms are threatening and aiding a gloomy Turneresgu-3 mystery to those shrouded peaks, ravines, and glacier.</p>
        <p>True there are some with whom sport is so much tin " being, aim, end " of existence that they turn a deaf ear to the distracting charm of nature. " A violet by a moss stone Half bidden from the eye" has its attraction for ordinary mortals, but we've all heard of the gruff malediction with which M F H passes it by. .</p>
        <p>Wtiether this b fact or merely makes a good story, the writer knows of one keen Golfer at any rate whose patience is sorely tried by the chorus of the spring time. "How can hs drive when those lambs start baa- ing on the very stroke ! Who," we have heard him mutter, "can putt with that lark making such a beastly nobe." We may take it these ruthless devotees are in a minority, however, and that the scenery of the Engadine is an attraction to the average golfer, just as</p>
        <p>TEE DIFFICULT- AIR OF THE ICED MOUNTAIN TOP is a tirnul us to bis game. For, be it known to jaded lowlanders,here in these Southern Highlands, ae3l and pleasant breezes over the Links never fail on the hottest day in August. The very pretty new pavilion was the nne thing needful and this we owe to the enterprise of Af9ssrs Lloyd, Withers and Patterson and our old friend Paul Springmann.</p>
        <p>Space ainalts of only brief notes on the various events of the three days' meeting. Place aux dames. In the Ladies' Championship the following qualified with the four bast scores under pedal play :</p>
        <p>Mrs Mosely 87 Mme. von Meister, 93 ; Mrs Lde Rothschild, 100; AErs Bott, 101, and in the final Mrs Leopold de Rothschild me:, Mrs Moseley, a most formidable opponent. Both were in good form, Mrs Rothschild played with skill and determina- tion, and though she was beaten by 3 and 2 we must regard that as a most creditable performance against so fine a golfer as Mrs Mo=eley. The distinguished " runner up "for the Champ- ionship was the first of many to offer congratula- tions to the winner on her success.</p>
        <p>Perhaps nothing in the tournament gave mere general satisfaction than Mrs Holland's success in winning the Ladies' Singles Handicap and with it a golf equipment, fitted up as only Jack Morris cen do these things. We wish Mrs Holland many happy returns of the day, feeling confident that so cheery a golfer will not weary in well doing, In the Mixed Foursomes Miss Bessie Payne and her partner scored a most populai. win, Mrs Lloyd and " Ted "running them ver3 close and taking second prize,. _ . .</p>
        <p>There' is no better sportswoman than Mrs Lloyd, and everyone was gla Ito see her t 3 the tore in the Tournament.</p>
        <p>Mrs Rothschild, who with Mr Tarver as her partner, holds the record of 59 strokes for the 12 , holes returned with him the best scratch F core this ! year. viz, 66 and but for a slice of very bad lack would have gone near winning as they bath played extremely well. Their opponents Miss Holmes and Mr Crosfield returned the next best scratch cort of 70. Miss Holmes, a very promising player, was for the third time this season a prize winner for she carried off the Ladies Putting. With so many older hands in the field this was no small achievement, and Miss Holmes was cordially congratulated on her success.</p>
        <p>A. Rothechill won the Men's handicap and we have seen few players with so excellent sit I pro- mising a style, while his bnther's, power of driving is, for so young a player, astonishing. The Men's Long Driving was easily won by Tarver, and it is no reflection on the other players to say that he started first favourite for the Championship. His style is as workman-like as it is clas-ical and against wind his power of driving is exceptional. In the qualifying round he and Crosfield, playing , together as of yore tied at 83, but this was eclipsed by Phelps who returned the very fine score of 78. Of the 8 men re•urning the best medal F cores who then proceeded to settle matters by match play,. Phelps success was however short lived, as in his fir•t encounter he was beaten by Moseley, the latter playing with admirable coolness and cmfidence. Dian and Butt had a tremendous struggle, and had not the former missed two very short putts towards the end the result might have been different, for our popular secretary was play- ing, the top of.his game.</p>
        <p>r, 'The matcl of the season however, was the semi- final between Tarver an 1 Bott. The former is a 6cratch man at St. Andrews—the latter at West- ward He. Both were en their metal and it was a case of Greek meeting Greek. They each completed the first half of the journey in 86 strokes, Tarver driving splendidly as usual and Bott with his very long carry pretty nearly level with him.</p>
        <p>_ It was a neck and neTA game throughout and Butt only turned the S cale in his favour by pheno- menal putting..</p>
        <p>Thanks to this and beautiful all round play, he gradually got a slight lead and won at the last hole by 2 up. The scores which were each 77 speak for themselves, and Butt may well be proud of his victory o.er such an adversary. Crosfield was in good term and going out in 36 was 3 holes to the good of Moseley at the turn eventually winning by 6up and sto play. In the final steady rather than brilliant play was the order of the day. Neither of the pair showed the same form as in the semi-finals. Bott probably feeling the effects of his great tussle in the morning could not again screw himself up to concert pitch, and Crosfield after establishing a slight lead played carefully to maintain it. He eventually "got borne" by 3 and 2 and was therefore for the third time in the last 6 years the fortunate winner of the Championship which carries with it Itirs RothSchild's prize as a memento: His score of 82 would have been im- proved but for some rather severe punishment at 3 holes.</p>
        <p>I An adjournment was promptly made by both players and spectators to the where Mr Withers in a few remarks of exceptional felicity— we have seldom heard a more appropriate speech— congratulated the winners and introduced Countess Czernin who then gracefully distributed the prizes. Mr Cro6tield proposed a vote of thanks to Ih3 Countess which. was ve...37 warmly responded to by everyone and this closed the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>PRIZES.</head>
        <p>PRIZES.</p>
        <p>MEN'S CHAMPIONSHIP OF THE ENGADINE.— Challenge Cup (and prize given by Mrs Leopold de Rothschild) won by Mr A 11 Ctosfield. Second prize won by Mr J A Bott.</p>
        <p>LADIES C HAMFIONSHIP OF THE ENGADINE.— Challenge Cup (and prize given by Mr Hansen Withers) won by Mrs Moseley. Second prize won by Mr i Leooold de Itntbmchild. Lennce HANDICAP.—First prize given by Mr A</p>
        <p>H Crosfield won by Mrs Holland. Second prize won by. Miss B van Buren.</p>
        <p>MEN'S BANDICAI3.—First prize given by Mr and Mrs A H Lloyd won by Mr A de Rothchild. second prize won by Dr Hayes. MIXED FOITICSOMES.—FinA prize won by Miss B Payne and Captain Norris. Second prize won by Mrs Lloyd and Mr T 11. Lloyd. LADIES' PUTTING—Prize given by Mrs Holland, won by Ml5B LI. Dolines.</p>
        <p>MEN'S LONG .DRICING .Pnizm given by Miss B Payne won by Mr J IC T Tarver.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON TAILOR. Alleged Assault on an Employee.</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON TAILOR. Alleged Assault on an Employee.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington CJunty Borough Polies Court, on Friday, before Mr Beptierd and other magie- trates, Joseph Partingtou, taiior, 43, Bridge-street Warrington, was charged with having assaulted one of his einploytes named Eli Hake, 6, Howard- street, on the 17th August.</p>
        <p>• Mr Steel appeared for the complainant, and Mr Browne for the defendant wto pleaded not guilty. Mr Steel said the complainant was a journeyman tailor, and had been employed by the defendant for 15 years. On Saturday the lich inst, he did not go to work and in the afternoon went to draw wr at he had earned during the vri.ek (8s 63). lie went to the cutting shop at the back of the premi,ses where the wages were customarily paid, and asked the defendant for his money. Mr Partington called him into the yard and said " Come here and I will pay you." He then seized a brush end struck the complainant on the head, knocking him to the ground. While on the ground defendant bit Hake on the shoulder and in tne side with the brush,and kicked him on the thigb. Complainant then swore at the defendant and went to the Police Station. from which piece, after having made a complaint, he went to the Infirmary, where his injuries were dressed and he went home and was in bed four days. On the 3londay defendant sent a boy down with a sovereign, which the c noplainant very properly refused to accept. Complainant's wife went to draw the Ss fid due to her husband and Mr Partington, after leaving been informed that complainant was uuable to go out, said "lam sorry but I did not think I hit him so hard." He then said to the complainant's wife that if she would go on the Saturday, he would give her £l. She went on the following Saturday and told Mr Partington that her latusband was a little better. He said " Nonsense, he can't be that bad. I will lend you a sovereign on condition that he comes to work on Monday." The wife not being able to give the assurance of her hus'iand's early return to work, went home and the summons was taken out against the defendant. Alter the summons had been taken out, the defendant sent a man named Scott down to the complainant's house with an offer to pay lOs and all costs to hare the summons with- drawn. This offer was refused, and the case pro- ceeded with.</p>
        <p>Complainant gave corroborative evidence. Cross-examined by Mr Browne: He did not go for his money on the Saturday in a drunken con- dition. He did not interfere with the other work- men going on with their work. Mr Partington did send for him to leave the workroom, but that was not because he was using abusive language and inteffering generally with the work. Joseph Malone, errand boy employed by Mr Partington said that on the day in question Mr Partingten sent him upstairs to request the com- plainant to leave. Complainant went into the yard when requested, and then Mr Partington com- mitted the assault as described by Mr Steel. John Jordan, a tailor employed by Messrs Dolan and Page,gave evidence as to having witnessed the assault. . _ _</p>
        <p>Browne, for the defence, said his client had had a great deal of trouble with his workmen. On the Saturday complainant went for his wages in a drunken condition and interfered with the other workmen and their work. Mr. Partington request- ed Hake to leave and caught hold of his collar to eject him from the preraises,when the complainant attempted to strike him. In attempting to strike, coinolainant, being in such a drunken state, fell and Mr Partington pushed him out of the yard with the yard-brush. Complainant had repeatedly gone to his work drunk, and his conduct on Saturday was suffizient provocation for Mr. Parrington to use whatever violence was:necwsary to eject him.lt was for their worships to oeciie whether tie force used was sufficient or too great for the purpose. If they decided that the latter was the ca,e then he thought justice would be met by dismissing the case on the payment of the costs. One feature of the matter was the fact that both at the Police Station and at the Infirmary the complainant in describing the assault never mentioned haying been struck on the head.</p>
        <p>Dr Mort said he examined the complainant's shoulder but could see no marks of it being bruised Ile, however, put some medicated plaster on as the complainant complained of it pinning him. Cross-examined : The bruises though invisible then would become visible in a day or two. The man was in a drunken condition; in fact, he could not stand steadily on his feet. The Bench here interposed, and intimated that they had decided to dismiss the case on the pay- ment of cost. Mr Partington had had provoca- tion, but had been unnecessarily violent in ejecting the complainant. An advocate's fee of Is would be allowed. The total amount of thecosts was £1 17e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>NEGLECTFUL PARENTS.</head>
        <p>NEGLECTFUL PARENTS.</p>
        <p>A Lazy Reservist Sent to Gaol.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Borough Police Court, on Riday, before Mr Ilepherd and other magis- trates, George Gill, shoemaker, 4'J. Church-street, was coatgect with having on the 28th August and previous dates neglected his four chijdren.—Pri- soner pleaded guilty.—Mr Browne, on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., said the prisoner lived next door to the National Schools, Church-street. and was a shoemaker by trade. He had ample work to do if he liked, but would not follow his employment. As Ihe had pleaded guilty to the charge of neglect, it was only necessary for him (Mr Browne) to give the bare facts of the case in order to show how serious the charge was. Last Sunday evening the police were called to the prisoner's house to prevent him thrashing his wife. On arriving there they found signs of a struggle having taken place, and after preventing the husband from assaulting his wife they advised her to prosecute him Nothing was done until Inspector Hackett, who was super- vising the case, called on the Wednesday. Be found the woman and the four children having a meal which they called their dinner and which conaisted of two pieces of toast and some tea. The Inspector had instructed him that there was not sufficient for a meal for one person, let alone five. Defendant had been to the front as a reaerre man'and instead of wo'lting went about in his khaki asking for charitable assistance and imposing on the public. All the money which be obtained he spent in drink. One of the pri,oner's daughters worked at the factory an i earned 74 per week but was so insufficiently fed that she had fainted from sheer weakness while at her work. The wife said that if the prisoner V 769 sent away another of her daughters who was earning good wages would come to live with her and between them they would be able to keep the house going. The wife was an industrious woman, and the house was perfectly clean, and the children , clean, but I badly clothed and fed. Corroborative evidence was given aue. prisoner was sent to gaol for one month I with bard labour.</p>
        <p>- -7 Bessie Wakefield,ll, Heath-lane, was charged with having neglected her stepson on the 22nd August and other dates. Mr Browne said the defendant's husband was a respectable man who was employed as a traction engine driver by the Corporation. The defendant had unfortunately given way to drink, and neg- lected to attend to the wants of the home. In con- sequencs of the constant thrashings he received, the lad, aged 11 years had run away to his aunt's. His body and clothing were so verminous that his aunt burnt the latter, had his hair cut, and took him to Inspector Hackett. Though the lad had been attended to, the inspector could plainly see the vermin in his head. The boy's stepmother thrashed him very often, and occasionally threw carving knives at him. The room in which the boy slept at home was examined by the Inspector, who fitly described it as being so filthy that it NVOS not At to live in, Arthur Wakefield described his wife as not being capable of managing the house. He earned 30s per week and gave his wife 22s,—most of which she spent in drink. Further evidence was heard after which the Bench adjourned the case for four weeks t o giv defendant an opportunity of making an imp hoe meat.</p>
      </div>
