      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>DR. GUEST GORNALL ON TUBERCULOSIS.</head>
        <p>DR. GUEST GORNALL ON TUBERCULOSIS.</p>
        <p>THE KOCH THEORY.</p>
        <p>On the agenda paper of the Council matins: for next week the following report on Tuberculosis by the acting Medical 01Boer of Health appears : The carefully worded resolutions which were passed at the end of the International Congress on Tuberculosis for the prevention of consumption, recently held in London, form an epitome of our knowledge of this important matter. They are of especial value as showing how this knowledge may he pet to practical uses, and while keeping within the linuts of what we may now regard as established fact, do not commit those who adopt them as a basis for administrative measures, to a definite acceptance of any of the more extreme theories which are aavecated by sorne, who, through specialising in one direction, seem to lose all sense of proportion. Such men are not uncommonly foreigners. Tuberculosis is due to the invasion of the body by a' microbe, the bacillus of tuberculosis, which sets up a progressively destructive process in the part of the body attacked. Any part of the body may became thus diseased, but the commonest is the lungs, which the microbes reach by being breathed in. The result is what is commonly known as constimption, or phtbisis, or what in old chys was one of the conditions they included under the name "decline." The expectoration ' In such cases contains bacilli of tuberculosis, often in ' enormous quantities, and when dried is scattered as dust by the wind. In this way the bacilli are disseminated, and when breathed in by clieer people may cause further attacks of the disease. Many of the lower , animals, especially the domesticated ones, are liable to I diseases of very like character. Though the sympterus ' and appearances which they give rise to differ somewhat from those in human beings, the germs causing them are identical in look and charac.er. It has generally been assumed that in all these eases we have had to de with one and the same disease, and that therefore tu- berculous meat and milk are dangerous food, but Koch denies this, lie has failed to start the disease in cattle by feeding them with food containing tuberculo- sis sputum from human beings, while he has always produced tuberculosis in cows by putting into their food tuberculous matter from animals of their own species. Hence he concludes that the tuberculosis of mankind cannot be conveyed to cows. He goes further, and sips that since vast numbers of people drink tuberculous milk daily the reverse experi- ment is actuality being carried on by us on a large scale. and that the results are strongly in favour of saying that the tuberculosis of C3WB cannot be conveyed to human beings. Otherwise tuberculosis would be far more rife than it is, and in particular large numbers of children, whose staple food is milk, would suffer Mtn tuberculosis beginning in the bowels. This, Koch says, is very rare, hence the conclusion to which be has come that human and bovine tuberculosis are two distinct diseases. At the Congress this waa received with sur- prise and dissent, and the opinions there expressed are to be found embodied in the fourth and fifth resolu- tions.</p>
        <p>It was remarked by Lsrd Liver that even if human and bovine tuberculosis were different, it was quite possible that the latter might be conveyed to infants, and infants, as the death returns show, die in large numbers of tuberculosis of the abdominal glands, prob- ably a result of infection through the intestines. More over the same cause of disease acting on widely different constitution,. • e., those of man and the cow, may lead to very unlike results. Lord Lister pointed out that cowpox and smallpox were originally thought to be distinct diseases, because all attempts failed to inoculate cows and other animals with the latter, but the pretective effects of cowpox against smallpox always suggested some relationship between the two and this was at length proved by Dr Copemau. some of whose experiments ware done with ymph obtained from me during the Warrington Small- pox Epidemic. Dr Copeman was the first to inoculate successfully with smallpox one of the lower animals, choosing for this purpose the monkey. The monkey developed smallpox, and from it cows were inoculated ' with the result—cowpox with which babies were Betels and successfully vaccinated. The passage of the virus through the body of a monkey thus considerably modi- fied it. In the case of tuberculosis also there may be I facts Set unknown, which, when brought to light will explain tbe differences between, while establishing the Identity of bovine and human tuberculosis, but just as we cannot assert this as an established fact so we are still in the dark as to other possible modes of infection. I For instaoce, we do not know for bow long the tubercle bacillus can continue to exist outside the bodies of &amp;oilmen. Is has been shown that exposure to sunlight and fresh air are rapidly fatal to it, thoueh ia damp, dark and close rooms it can retain Its vitality for an Indefinite period. It is Indeed quite possible that its disease producing properties are an acquired charaCter, which, under unfavourable conditions are in abeyance, and that in the absence oa f avowable soli. that Is to say a susceptible soiree!, it can maintain a purely vegetative existence. None the less, it can be assertei in the words of th u first resolution that tuberculous sputum is the malts agent for the conveyance of the virus from man to man, for even if tubercle bacilli exist in nature, apart from man or any animal, those of them are doubtless the most virulent that have grown in diseased animal tissue. It foil ,sve from tnis that everything should be done to prevent the scattering about of expectoration by consumptives, and this may be best achieved In (1) a general discouragement of all spittine. the Municipality I have warnings agalest the practice put up in buildings I under its control, and measures being taken to get permission for the same thing to be done in other places of public resort ; and (2) by 'actually dealing with con- sumptives themselves, previding them with proper means to destroy their expectoration, and showing them hew to use the same. The vast maim. ity of these patients come at some time or other under the observation of a medical man el her as private patients or more commonly in public hospi- tal, but it is highly desirable be ascertain at as early a stage as possible the existence of such cases, in order to apply, without delay, the necessary measures. It is for this reason that notification is advocated in phthisls as in ;other infecsinue diseases. The boo wlelge thus acquired of where the disease is, would enable us to 'supplement the eff 3rts to be carried oust through the hespitals, and also lead to the application of disinfection where needed. Disinfectlon has for some years been dose in Warrington after a death from phthisis, bust there are other cure where it is wanted, where, for instance, a consumptive has removed from one house to another. In dealing with a tuberculous patient,fortunately, it Is not only possible to prevent him being a danger to othess but actually in the early stages to cure him. It has long been known that the vast majority of dead bodies which are examined In the hospitals of our great cities chew traces of consumption of the lungs, which nes healed. In other words the tendency of persons so diseased is towards recovery, and if we want to help them to this alt we have to do is to provide them with those health giving conditions to which they are so often strangers. Hence the movement to provide Sanatoria where they can have good food, fresh air and sunlight, the two latter of which are as much enemies to tubercle bacilli as they are friends to the human victurcsa of these pests. Br means of these Sanatoria a great many per- sons may be helped to recovery, though, as the eiaor- mous number of consumptives in existence sprscludes the pessibility ef thus dealing with any Ronsiderstele proportion of them, we cannot rely much nn this as a means of diminishing the dancer of infection. But this brings meth the final and to my mind by hr the most important consideration of the whole matter. For granting that teterculous sputum is the main agent for infection from man to man, and not taking into consideration any other source of it, the universal distribution of this disease makes it pretty evident test the vast Majority of mankind are continually exposed to the risk of tuberculeeie, and yet without sinecure!, lag. A fact which I mentioned above about the large proportion of hospital patients with traces of tuber- culosis is strongly in favour of this. The remarkable freedom from consumption of doctors and nurses in consumption hospitals also suggests that there is another factor in the Case and that is predisposition, whether born in or acquired by mankind. For though it is clear that some people are born with a peculiar liability to contract the Infection of toberele, it Is also probably not an overstatement to say that anybody whatsoever can become liable to get tuberculosis if his conditions of ife are sufficiently depressing to his constitution, add hence it is that ineanitery cmutitinnessuch as overcrowd leg, want of light and air, and insanitary habit, such as alcoholism, ate:most powerful promoters of Consump- tion.</p>
        <p>_ It La therefore in my opinion of the utmost importance to g 0 on from year to, year with the gradual clesitOß nut of tho shuns of thp town, the demolition of property incapable of being put into a properly sanitary condition and the abolition of courts and alleys wlarrp the air is clnficeti and the Run rarely penetrat.a. There ia work of this kind that is sulEcinnt roc many yrass to come to occupy all thp energies of the Sanitary Authority and i their Medical Officer, hot. I feel cnntid,nt that more satisfac:ory results will accrue from a steady pursuit of this policy than frrm tnitin! measor•s to spread an exaggerated fear of bacteria among the people. At the paine time the Medical Officer of Health should ho em- powered to take such steps a, may he neederi to educate public opinion on the creation by the d'attihtitinn of literature, and to invite the er,peration of local institu- tions in combatine toberculnsi•. Also I would strongly UruP tile desirability of establishing the practice of notification with regard to phthisis,and ther.br put the Health Departmsnt in a position to deal s ttisfactnrity with such ovum. Re if I were to sum up briefly th gsneral e policy which I fav tr. I would say " Sanitary Refcrm rather than Bacterionhobia."</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON STREET FATALITY.</p>
        <p>The Adjourned Inquest. Driver Censured.</p>
        <p>The adjourned inquest was held at the Police Station on Friday morning Mr Crompton again appeared for the relative% ancliMe Forshaw fee Messrs Eddowes and Company, whilst the driver Maginis was rcpresented by Mr Jenkins. The Coroner said that he came to Warrington early and met the foremen of the jury (Mr Whittle) at ',sire- ' street, and accompanied by Inspector Rowbottom and the driver went down the street and saw the different hons.s where the witnesses lived. Afterwards he was driven along the street in the cart in question by ' Maeinis and had an opportunity of forming same opinion about it. He found that the front a the cart , was a , great deal lower than the sides. There was nothing to prevent the driver from seeing right down I the street, left or right. The witness Windt° was than called and repeated the evidence which he gave on Tuesday. Witness was cress-examined as to which side of the road the vehicle waa being driven, when The Cotoner pointed out that when a driver turned into a street be had no legal obligation to keep on the near side. He must only keep to the near side if he met another vehicle. It would not be prima facie evidenqe of neglect if they found the dater was on the off side.</p>
        <p>Richard Maginia, the driver cf the cart was the next witness and gave the same evidence as he offered at the opening of the inquest. By Mr Jenkins : From the time he left the Barracks up to the time of the accident he had no conversation with his father-in-law. His father-in law had ridden with him to Newchurch and back.</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr Jenkins witness explained that he had instructions net to take any persons with him in I the cart. If he did 83 he was liable to dismissal. The fear of being dismissed made him tell an untruth, which he now regretted. Cross-examined by Mr Crompton witnees again affirmed that he was not talking with his father-In-law. He was going to sec down his father-it-law at his residence. He brat saw the child when it was under the horseallegs and at once tried to pull up. He could not possibly see how the child got Caere. On being pressed witness said he never maw the child at all. The sun was very glaring at the jinn and was shining right in his face. John Green, a labourer in the employ of Messrs Rylacds' Bros and residing at 33. Lairs-street said be was In the cart in company with his son-in-law,Richard Maginis. He did not notice the child. He was not talking to his son-in•law and did not know what Maginis watqloing.l3e did not know that anything had happened until he felt the cart lurch. He was totally blind is his lefc eye and his other eye was very weak. By Mr Cromozon Witm Ss had not arranged with his sennin-lavi to" keep out ot the case." He waP standing outside the Court on Tuesday, and would have been willing to Rive evidence if he had been sent for. Joseph Davidson. a general Isbourer living at 27, Laira-street, stated that at 5.10 on Saturday afternoon he was standing at his door with his wife, and saw the deceasedicrossing the street. At the same time"the horse and cart came up at a walking pace. The horse knocked the child down,and he saw the wheel go over the child's neck. He saw wiedle pick deceased up, hut did not see Green in the cart. To the best of his belief, he did not think the driver saw the deceased, and did his best to pull up when the accident happened. In answer to the Coroner witness stated that ,as the horse and cart approached, the child's mother screamed and this caused the child to crouch down. Other per- sons said the deceased was in danger. The Coroner: From your evidence it would seem that everyone knew the dangec except the driver. Further questioned witness said he believed the accident would not have happened had the child's mother not screamed. . .</p>
        <p>The Coroner was unable ta elicit definite information from the witness as to the distance intervening between the child and the vehicle when deceased stepped into the road. A little girl named Allen Tomlinson residing at 32, Lairs strest was called by Mr Jenkins. She stated that the deceased was crossing the road when the horse and cart came up. She tried to save the deceased but before she could reach her the hare knocked deceased down and the wheel passed over her neck. Ellen Tomlinson. mother of the last witness, said her daughter had pointed out to her the spot where the child stepped into the road. .</p>
        <p>Witnos's'having further explained, it was ascertained that according to her statement the child stepped into the road thir;een yards in advance of the hors, and cart.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : If that is the exact distance the driver ought to have seen the child step off the footpath. In his summing up, the Corencr pointed out that it was the duty of a driver who had something under his control that was dangerous, to exercise care. The important point in the me was the question as to where the child stepped into the road and this seemed to be involved in obscurity. Of course it must be said in favour of the driver that he was not bound by law to keep on any particular side of the road. To put it plainly, be mutt do all he'could t 3 avoid necklet lel, After deliberating in private, the jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death' and added is presentment to the effect that the accident occurred theough the carelessness of the driver. They asked the Coroner to severely censure lilaginis. The Coroner said he thought that both Msginis atd his solicitor would admit that during the course of the Inquiry he (Mr Brighouse) had she wn no prejadice to Mantels lei any shape or form. He had tried to be as impartial as possible but now that the jury bad given their verdict, be would say that he thoroughly agreed I with what they said. If behad had to dispose of the matter without their assistance he should never have dreamt of allowing Alleluia to go away with an expression of opinion that he was not to blame. An ounce of practice was worth five or six witnesses, and when fdaginis drove him from Battersby--lane into Laira-street be formed his opiniod AP to bow the acci- dent happened. He could not possibly understand how the child could step off the near side into Lsira-street without the driver seeing it, unless it stepped off when the horse had passed the place. In this case it was quite clear that the child stepped off before the horse got to it, beeause it got past the horse's head and waa run over, by the offside wheel. If Maginis bad been keeping a look-out the ' socident would not have bapp-ned. Re wanted to impress upon him ad upon all arivers—he Mad co prejudice against any kind of kcomotion and what he said referred uot only to drivers of horses but to cyclists and those in charge 0! any vehicles on the high was—that the pedestrian bad the first right Cr, the highway. It was for him to travel on and therefore th age who chose to go on the highway with anything dangerous to the red( stelae Must exercise Care, and if tuey did not must bear the colorequencea. Maginis had no right to be careless teither hail anyone else, and be hoped it would be a lesson not only to him but to all drivers of horses. They must remember that cue must be exercised to avert calemity. Toe inquiry, which bad occupied • two hours, then conclud .</p>
      </div>
