      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>rerrible Fatalities.</p>
        <p>A terrible accident occarred at Messrs Mappin Brothers' works, Sheffield, on Wednesday night. The premises were being cleaned, and the white- washers had left scaffolding about the place. Several girl employees were ainuiing themselves by running up an down some pla.mks.when Mary Mil- lington, aged sixteen, was caught by the hair in the engine shafting, and was whirled roiled and round and killed, her neck being broken and her skull fractured.</p>
        <p>At an ingocat in Lendan, on Thursday, on the body of Walter Scatt (50), a painter, it was stated that on Tuesday last deceased whilst engaged at work at the top of a house suddenly fell over into the area blow, a digtance of about 35 fest, and was killed. The medical evidence showed that nearly every bane in deceased's body was smashed. Verdict, "accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>Yesterday's Cricket.</head>
        <p>Yesterday's Cricket.</p>
        <p>Close of Play Scores. YORNSIIIIiE v. REST OF ENGLAND:— Ycrk- shire, all cut, 214; Rest, 10 for 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>Armoured Train Derailed.</p>
        <p>Three Men Killed.</p>
        <p>Cfficer and Five Men Injured</p>
        <p>CAPETOWN, WEDNESDAY. An:armoured train was detailed while entering Taungs Station on the 2nd inst., a native baying turned the points the wrong wa7. Treachery is suspected as there are Boers in the neighbourhood.</p>
        <p>Three men were killed by the accident and five were wounded including an officer. Tatum; is stout 30 miles north of Kimberley on the De Aar Rhodesia railway. More Cheshires for the Front.</p>
        <p>A hearty send-cif was accorded to drafts of the Army Ordnance Corps, Royal Army Medical Corps, Royal West Surrey Regiment and the Cheshire Regiment at Aldershot on Thursday morning on their leaving for Southampton, where they will embark on the Manilla for South Africa.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Volunteer's Sad Death.</p>
        <p>our readers will regret to hear that Lance-Corporal T Hodges, who served with the Ist contingent of Warrington Volunteers who went to South Africa. died en Tuesday morning. Since his return he has euffdred from dysentery contracted in South Africa, but he gassed away peacefully. Corporal Hodges had been visited during his illness by Mr Henry Taylor, P.C.M,, on behalf of the Transvaal Relief Committee, who have attended to the wants of the family', and have kindly undertaken to make all necessary arrangements for the interment of the deceesed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>Presentation to Captain Appleton. Meeting of Local Foresters.</head>
        <p>Presentation to Captain Appleton. Meeting of Local Foresters.</p>
        <p>-The Hardships Undergone by the Volunteers. Local Foresters assembled in strong force at the Cor- poration Inn, 'Market Place. on Saturday evening, in order to give tangible expreseion of their esteem for one of their number, Brother Captain Applecon,who went to South Africa in command of the first contingent of local Volunteers. The gallant Captain is a member of Court 240, and his fellow members had subscribed to present him with a handsome smoking cabinet. The cabinet which was supplied by Mr A Stanway, bore the inscription. " Presented to Brother Appleton by his Brother Fr resters number Court 240 on his return from South Africa, Aueust, 1901." The room in which the gathering took place had been decorated with considerable taete. Among several ap- propriate mottoes upon the walls were: Success to our Court," " Welcome to all," and " Health to our members." The members were indebted to Mrs and Miss Lawless for the bright and pleasant appearance of their room, and for the excellent arrangements to the Presentation Committee (Messrs T Smith, G Allen, and J Davies (hon. secretary). Brother T Addison (Chief Ranger) presided,and was supported by Brother Captain Applet^n, Brother R Pierpoint, M.P., and Brother Dr tirmth (honorary members). The CHAIRMAN said they were met to give cordial greeting, on hie return from South Africa. to al reepect- od brother. They were proud that a member of Court 240 bad had the honour of being in charge of the first Volunteer contingent who went out to the war from the good old town of Warrington. (Applause.) They were all glad to see Brother Appleton looking so well. On behalf of the brethren of Court Mercy he gave him a cordial greeting and a hearty welcome. (Annlause.) The Chairman then proposed the toast of the King, which was received with musical honouree He afterwards explained that a presentation of a surgeon's ribbon should have been made to Brether Dr Smith, hut by some mischance it had not yet arrived, and would have to be presented on annoe future occasion. (Applause.) Brother GEono.P. MAsnuirr said that in the history of the country the Friendly Societies had played no mean pert. A hundred years ago they were hindered and hampered by a Government who leoked upon secret societies as orgarrzations which tended to undermine the constitution of the country and dethrone the King. What a marvellous vision those then in authority would have seen had they been able to lift the veil and look upon the results of the work of the century. The great Friendly Society movement to-day bad 3,537,573 mem• here, and was doing a good and even noble work. (Applauae). Friendly Societies had relieved the rates, and in many respects set before the country a noble example. lodependentio of that they had accumulated the vast sum of £25.675 381 Last year the combined societies paid'over £2,345i027 in sick m meveand £542,560 in death money. It was by combination that this great sYstem had been put upon suchf a basis. This meney did not come from the wealthy landlord.; or the aristoc- racy. It had been provided by the werkingmen of the country, and the workingmen were the backbone of the country to.day. (Aceplause.) It was his very pleasant duty that evening to make a presentation to a member of Court 240% who was the leader of that little band of Volunteers who left the town to fight in the cause of their country. Brother Captain Appleton had their higheet esteem lird regard. (Applanap.) on behalf of their brethren in Forestry of Court 240 he had the great pleasure, in their name, in asking him to accept a smoking cabinet as a token of their friendship and esteem. He hoped that when he had fieished the toil and business of the day he would, RP he sat in his room, open that beautiful cabinet and smoke the pipe of peace and contentment. (Applause.) He proposed the toast of the Arms. Navy, Colonial and Volunteer Appleton Forces, coupling ith it the names of Brother Captain and Brother R Pierpoint. M.P. (Applause.) The toast having been honoured, the company sang "For they are jolly good fellows." Brother CAPTAIN APPLETON, who was cordially re- ceived, said he could hardly find words in which to exprese to them his thanks for their bar /borne gift and bind reception of him. He thought that his senior Yearn:its er officer and senior brother ought to have replied to the toast first. It was to him a very proud reflection that he had bad the honour of leading the company of Warringtem and St Helene Volunteers in , South Africa. What had distirguished those men had been their modesty. They had never told people what they had gone throueh, hut ho (the speaker) would tell them something of it. The men had done right down hard work. (Hear, bear.) Although they had not very much fiehting—two pretty stiff fiehts and a good deal of goiping—they had done their share of march- ing. It WAS a little better, of couree, for an officer, for although he marched with prette much the same kit and carried a rifles, he had a little easier time than the men. The men had a really bard time, and for six weeks slept on the open veldt. At night the frost was thick upon their blankets, which in the morning and during the day were wet, so that they never really bad a dry blan.ket. In addition, they had very indifferent food.in tact everyone had, and many of them at the end of a long day's march had to go straight out on outpost duty and wait:for their food until it was taken to them. Be was sorry to say that two Foresters, one a member .of that Lodge, were left behind. Nn one grieved more for them than their old comrades. But if England was sot worth dying for,what was? (Applause.) He thanked</p>
        <p>the members for their beautiful present. They could not have given him anything more appropriate, because he did love his pipe. (Hear, hear.) He did not know what they would have done in South Africa without their pipes. They tided them over many difficulties and trials. He again thanked them from the bottom of his heart for what was one more token of the kindness and warm-heartedness of the people of the good old town of Warrington. (Applause.) Three cheers were given for Captain Appleten. Brother It PIERPOINT, M.P., who also had a very cordial reception, said he had been amused by the re- mark of Captaio Appleton that he (the speaker) should take precedence of him because of his senior rank long I ago in the Volunteers. Captain Appleton had attained I a rank far above any he had ever attaiaed. Speaking of ' the war he said it had been a long one, ar.d one they would be glad to be rid of. They all looked forward to the end which must be alsoluts for England. (Applause). Is was not altogether, in his apprehension, an unmixed evil that they should have had this war. It had coat them money and many valuable and well-beloved lives, but it had aroused the spirit of England, which he vas afraid had to a certain extent been asleep. It had shown that whether they were dukes or earls, aristoc- racy, middle-elass or workingmen they were all English- men,and ready to fight for their country. (Applause) They had heard from Captain Appleton what he and his men had gone through. He told them one thing they did not appreciate, and that was that he had some little advantage over his men. He practically had no such advantage. (Hear, hear) He slept on the veldt just as the men did. He had his blanket frozen, and carried a rifle just as the men did. Another thing they had reason to oimeratnlate themselves upon in regard to the war, after putting aside its evils, was that it had brought our colonies into closer connection with our- elves, and Colonials would be now much mere apt than they were two or three years ago. to say in the words of the song they had just listened to, " Its a glori- ous charter to be an Enelishman." (Applause.) He thanked them very cordially for the kindness and courtesy with which they had proposed ansl honoured the toast. (Applacse). Cheers were given for B:othGr Pierpoint and Brother Dr Smith. _ _ _</p>
        <p>During the evening several musical and other items were submitted. Messrs Lawless and Glover sang a duet in pleasing style, and Mr Glover was heard to ad- vantage afterwards in the solo" Marching.” Mr Sanders afforded geniune enjoyment w'th a couple of recitations, while Mr Whittaker contributed two extremely clever whistling solos. Mr Hipkiss sang a nattiltic song which was greatly appreciated. Mr W W Whittle sang a couple of songs in his best style, and was most enthusiastically encored. Mr Bert Smith ably accona• panied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>FRODSHAM BREWSTER SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM BREWSTER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs C ReynoldP, J Ock'eston, and J Taylor. THE POLICE REPORT.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Nteld submitted his annual report on the licences in the district; There were 17 licensed vic- tuallers, 8 beeraellers, and 2 others; a total of 27, being an average of 245 of the population to each licence. One licensed victualler was proceeded against and con visted being the same number proceeded against and convicted the previous year. One beer house seller was proceeded against but not convicted, being an increase of one proceeded against, and one not convicted. Forty-two persons were proceeded against for drunkenness and convicted, sti against 30 persons proceeded against and:29 convicted the previous year. A list of persons who were reported to the police for serving children under the ago of 13 years was submitted, and the Bench called atten- tion ta the inadvisability of serving such li'tle children. AN EXTRAORDINARY LICENCE-DEFINING THE "EXTENT." Superintendent Nield said he was directed by his Chief te call the attention of tho Banch to the " Belie Monte" Hotel, Frodsham Hills. The Chief Constable wished the premises to be clearly defined. As far as the pollee had been able to ascertain the" Belle Monte " in former years was owned by Mr Rigby who built it. It had grounds, and a large room about 114 yards from the house, and so far as they could ascertain it was a fact that drink was got in the grounds, the rooms re- I ferred to and also at the house ; so that the one licence practically did for the three places. At the last licenv. hie meeting the licence had been transferred from Mr Hill to the present proprietor Mr C W Sampson, and lFi3 licence was granted to him on the understanding that the tea rooms were to be struck off. The Chairman : They made it a condition of the granting of the licence that the rooms should be struck off. _ _ _</p>
        <p>---dperintanident Nield : That has been done at OA- mere, I have brought it forward for the thing, to be made clear between the public and the licensee. If you would now define the premises it would be sufficient. Mr H N Linaker, solicitor, who appeared for the owners and licensee, asked that the licence be granted as heretofore. The hoose had always been well-con- ducted. Superintendent Nield admitted that the houie bad always been well conducted, but objected to tt being renewed in its entirety. He also pointed out that the Inland Revenue Officers who msessed the property had always done so without taking the whole into consider- lion, until last year, when the police called their attention to the facts of the case, whereon they surcharned the tenant with the additional dmount that year, £9 he thought it was. ' Mr Donnelley, Inland Revenue Officer, Runcorn, said that was the fact, but he did not know whether it made any difference. Superintendent Nield continuing said that the rooms, the land, and the house were all separate and the pro- perty was in the hands of two distinct tenants. Mr Linaker contended that the licence was granted for the " Belle Monte" and premises, and As there had never been any complaint against the house he asked that it be renewed. Superint-ndent Nield then put his objectione in the following order :—First, the tea rooms which were a distinct building being 114 yards from the house ; 2 id, tbere being a tenant for both the land and the house and premises ; and 3rd, there had also been comp'aints raade of the people who came from the back premises causing a disturbance to the public on winter nights. The land was owned by Mrs Home, and a portion of the rooms were also in her own use, for he had seen turn'- trace, dr.c., there belonging to her. Mr Linaker said with regard to the first objection it could not be denied that the licence was granted for the Belie Monte and premises, and that the whole were in- c•udscl then could not be a shadow of doubt. With regard to the second there was certainly a contract with Mrs goose and the tenant, whereby he could net cater against her, for picnic parties, Ste., and presumably she used the rooms to that extent. And respecting the third be did trA think they had to answer that, for there had been no complaints made against them lxfore the Bench. Ultimately the Bench retired and on returning intim- ated they would define the boundary as extending to 70 yards from the front door, which would include the grounds or place for dancing and the bowling greon,but not the tea-rooms. All the premises as on the plan consulted extending east and west would be included in the licence. Superintendent Nield asked that the boundary should be marked by a fence. The Chairman whilst thinking that a fence was a reasonable tieing to ask for pointed out that there could, however, be no compulsion about the matter. Mr Linaker intimated his agreement to a fence. The Chairman : lam glad this arrangement is Con- sidered satisfacthry. The renewals were all granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>WARRINGTON'.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'.</p>
        <p>The New Wesleyan Minister. Rev. W. C. J. Neville's FirstiSermons</p>
        <p>The .current issue of the" Wesleyan Methodist Church Record "contains the following :—We have come again to the beginning cf our Connezional year. During this week some 780 ministers in our church will be changing circuits. Next Sunday they will be expected to open their commission in their new spheres of labour. The Rev H U Roberts, as Dur readers are well aware, came to Warrington three years ago, and has received the mandate from the Conference to 'move on. He goes to take charge of the Belgrave Church in the Leicester, Bishop-street circuit. This church is, we understand, to be cenducted on modern mission lines. During hir term in the Warrington Circuit he has had :special pasLoral charge of the societies in Padgate, Lymm, and Latchford. But hie sympathies and exceptional abilities as a preacher have ben freely given to all parts of the circuit. He has made hosts of warm, personal friends, whose attaeiment will be permanent. Our heartiest good wishes go with him, and he may be sure that as a circuit we shall often ' wish to see him once more amongst us. The Rev W C J Neville, his successor, comes amongst us from Bradford-on-Avon. Becomes with a high reputation as a minister and preacher. He entned the ministry in 1888, and has travelled in the Whitehaven Portsmouth, Ventnor, and the Trow bridge and Bradford circuits. While we are sorry to lose Mr Roberts, we know we may assure him of a very unanimous and affectionate welcome from every part of the circuit. And we pray that this new year may be the brightest and most Prosperous that the Warrington circuit has ever seen." —On Sunday the anniversary sermons were preached in ' the Weslee an Church, P. el :tate, by the Rev W C J Neville, the newly-appointed minister of the Warrington Circuit. At the morniog service the rev gentleman took for his text the words " Be ye therefore imitators of God as dear children." In the evening Mr Neville preached to a large congregation from the words " Ye cannot serve God and mammon." He pointed out that It was impossible for people to be true Christians and serve God and the world. Dual service was neither acceptable to God nor the devil. He beseeched his hearers to be wholly for God. Miss Lunn, of L andon, sang the solo " Consider the lilies "with much effect. Mr Neville attended the Sunday school in the afternoon, and introduced himself to the teachers and scholars, and afterwards gave an interesting adds ess. Collect ions were taken at each service in aid of the Trust Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 6, 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 6, 1901</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Police Buildings, Newton, on Monday morning, Thomas Dunleavey was charged with being drunk and disorderley in Newton on Satur- day, and was fined 2s 61 and costs. Thomas Walton was also charged with a similar offence, and was discharged with a caution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>BOWL/NG HANDICAP AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>BOWL/NG HANDICAP AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The Newton-le-Willows bowling club played a bowling handicap on their green for Messrs green- all, Whitley and Co 's prize, a pair of bowls,and the entrance fees. There were 40 entries, and the first prize was taken by G 11 Lyons; 2nd, R Smi, h; 3rd, J Crouchley and 4th, W</p>
        <p>VIADUCT INSTITUTE BOWLING CLUB A bowling handicap for Mr J W Thorpe's prize of Li Is and the entrance money was played on the Viaduct Institute Bowling Club Green on Saturday in beautiful weather. There were 32 entries and some very gooi bowling was witnessed, the first prize of a guinea being taken by S Tlipmps.on; 2nd, 51, A I.4llnas; and 3rd 34,R Partington.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO IA SCHOOLSOY. On Tuesday afternoon as the scholars from War_ grave School, was returning home, several of them ran behind a lurry belonging to Mr Reyes, farmer. Croft, in order to climb on to have the pleasure of ride. One of the youngsters named W Gough, 9 years of age got his left foot among the spokes of the wheel. The lurry was stopped and the boy ex- tricated, and he was removed home. Dr Gangs was sent for and be found that the lad had frac- tured one of the bones of his left leg just above the ankle and sustained an incised wound on the right knee. He attended to the injuries and the boy is now progressing satisfactorily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>BURNING FATALITY AT EARLIISTOWIL</head>
        <p>BURNING FATALITY AT EARLIISTOWIL</p>
        <p>On Tuesday at noon a little girl 4 year of age, named Greenhalgh living with her parents at Derby-street, Earlestown, received shocking injur- ies from burning i which later proved fatal. The child was putting a raw muFsel on the jockey bar of the fire grate, when her print frock caught fire. The mother was getting her dinner and was sitting with her back to the fire, The child ran to the mother and then'out,dde into the street scream- ing. Her mother followed and extinguished the flames with jackets &amp;I. Dr Latham was sent for and was on the spot promptly, and did all he could to alleviate the .sufferings of the poor chili, the injuries, being chiefly about ths face and shwildera but she died at noon on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earle town lodge meeting was held in the Cooperative Hall, Wargrave-road on Mon- day. Bro Cutler, PC T presiding. One candid to was proposed and hawed. The secretary read the pro7raname for the ensuing quarter, which was adopted. It was 'resolved to invite the D T for the anniversary tea. Bro Wood, LD, read the digest of the No ls Convention to be held at Ashton. Bros Woods, G Clarke, Williamson, and Warburton were appointed representatives to No 1 Convention and Bros Roberts, Cutler and Sister Grant as alternate representatives. The programme for the evening being, "Sing, read or recite," the following items were given:—Medley soog, Bro Warburton ; duet, "Jesus,Lover of my Soul," Sisters Hill and Woods reading, Bro Cutler; song. "Beyond the Smiling,' Bro Hatton; song, "The Paler... 9 of the King," Sister Williams; reading, Bro Roberts; song, "Strangers Yet," Bro Williams; song, "Norah, the Pride of Kil- dare," Sister Grant. The Lodge was closed in the usual manner by Bro Wlliamsoo C T.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>SAD DEATH OF AN OCTOGENARIAN%</p>
        <p>The min-iv coroner has had information of the death of Georie Ward, eighty years of age, a gardener, resid- ing in Springfield-lano, Eccieston. 'The deceased was in the employ of the Ray B P Clark., vicar of Ecclestcn. Shortly before two o'clock on Wednesday afternoon he was in the garden with two of the vicar's children. He intended to gathor apples, and for this purpose he was stonping to pick up a short ladder, when h. fell over, and died in a fAsv rninutn. Dr Twyford, of St Helens, who had attended the deceased on several occasions, was called in, and, on making an inspection of the body xaressed the opiniol that death was du) to heart disease.</p>
        <p>SCENE AT A GrAFISWORICS--.TIIREAT TO PUT A MANAGER IN</p>
        <p>VIE FURNACE.</p>
        <p>_ At St Helens. on Monday. before Alderman Massey and Councillor Burchall, John Seddon, glass teaser, of Back-lane, was charged with assaulting .Tames L C Shepherd, manses? of Messrs S and C Bishop's flint glassworks. Mr E W Smith pi osecuted. and said the firm had changed the hours at which Seddon and an- other man, who were responsible for the maneg,ement of tke glaeri furnaces, had to go on and off the night turn. They decided that they must change at midnioht on Thursday night instead of the week end. Seddon objeeted, and wished to change turns at the week end. —Mr Shepherd said that on Thursday morning he was told by Anthony Smith, Seddon's mate, that defendant would not come on to change turns at the arranged time. He sent the office boy, William Rimmer, to Seddon' s house, at Carr mill, with a note to tell him if he did not come to work at the right time he would have to Eska a week's notice. Seddon sent a message back that if Shepherd would not give him his wages ho would throw him in the furnace. At noon Seddon turned up in an excited condition and went down into the cellar, where Mr Shenberd veal attendine to the crashing of ROMA important glass tubes. He struck him on the temple and knocked him about. Shepherd rushed out of the cellar, and as Seddon appeared to be going ti the glasshouse to disturb the men who wero at work he ordered him off the promisee. Seddon then rwhed him into the glasshouse, took him in his orms, and said be would throw him in the -- tank. Some oF the workmen came and pulled Seddon back, releasing Mr Shepherd. Complainant said he had been obliged to go to reside on the premises in order to try and obtain something like discipline.—Seddon, in defence, said that Mr Shepherd threatened to hit him on the head with a big glees tube. That was the second bother he had had with him. Re might have threatened him in his temper, but be did not intend to do him any harm,—The Bench said it was a yew serious matter for a workman to go on in that way and to take the manager up and threaten to put him in the furnace He would be fined £2 and lie 6,1 costs or fourteen days</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>WARRINGTON BURGLARIES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BURGLARIES.</p>
        <p>The Imperial Hotel Broken Into.</p>
        <p>Important Arrest. At the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday, before Mr !fireball (in the chair), Alderman Heaton and Mr Griller, Michael or John Logan, whose address was given as 22, Leonard-street was charged with burglar- iously entering the Imperial Hotel, Bewsey-road, some- time botween 11 o clock on Monday night and six o'clock on Tuesday morning and stealing 103 in cash and a bottle of brandy, the property of Thomas Par- tridge. The Chief Constable said there was another charge against the prisoner of breaking into the shop of Mess fs Jackson, p iwnbrokers, °Nord-lane, and stealing a pair of trousers. He intended that morning to very briefly state the facts in order to obtain a remand for three days. He regarded the apprehension of the prisoner as a moat important one. For some weeks past the town and country in the neighbourhood of Warrington had been pestered by at least one burglar, probably more, who bad operated on sloops and h0u5..13 by memo of an auger. Wiih this instrument the window sashes had been bored, a finger inserted through toe hole, and the window pushed up. An attempt was made to eater the Imperial Hotel in this way but the wiudow sash resisted the efforts of the operAtors. A pane of glass was smashed and an entrance effected in that way. Ten shillings in cash and a botzle of brandy were stolen. In the case of the pawnshop, the window seemed to have been broken with some blunt instrument, proba- bly the handle of tho auger. A pair of trousers which was hanging on a rail ineide the window was stolen. A third offence had also been committed but he could not go into it that morning. Information was given to the police and the matter was placed in the: bands of Detectives Barlow and Foster. These officers followed up an almost infinitesimal Chi!' with veryfcenSiderable promptitude and ekilt which resulted on Tuesday night in the arrest of the prisoner. Logan was found to be wearing the stolen trousers. On being brought to the Police Office and charged with the burg. lanes he said he did nor actually enter the Elotel but two pairs pivairs f trousersedaoriortiono were of the money. He also stated that wn from the imwnshop. Prisoner said begot one pair and another man got the other. For the purpose of investigating the man's statement be (Mr Talbot) asked for a rernand.for three days in custody. Detective Barlow was then called and stated that he examined the pawnshop in Orford-lade and also the Imperial Hotel and found they had been burglariously entered. The C 4,11 was placed in the hands of himself and Detective Foster and on the previous night they arrested the prisoner and found him to be wearing the stolen treusers. Prisoner admitted having been con- , cerned in breaking into the Imperial Hotel. Witness wished to make further investigations and probably other arrests. Prisoner was remanded until to-day (Saturday.) The OLEN-SPEY MirrlLLeay. Situate In the finest Whisky-producing District of Szotland. is the property of W. &amp;A. IBlhey. This Whisky is made entirely from home-grown Barley and is kept absolutely unblended In his Majesty's Bonded Warehouses to mature, and bold by W.&amp; A. Gilbey's agents in every town at 39. 6d per bottle</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Lord Newton and the Council's Requests for Land.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening. In the absence of Mr Coßingwood, the chair was occupied by Mr. Orrnand (deputy chairman). There worn aNa present ; Dr. Patterson, Dr Latham, Messrs. C B F Barrou,N Siddeley, J Simpson, W G.lmour, J L Wood, W Elampsen, with C Cole (clerk), and B 0 Timmins (as engineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>ABSENCE OF THE CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>THE ABSENCE OF THE CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Me. Ormand said he had to ;apologiso for the absence O f Mr. C3llin7wood, yell was, unfortunately, not able to 17,0 present, having a business engagement in Scotland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee, the Clerk reported that with respect to the scale of fees payalo!e to Ministers for services rendered at interments under the Burial Act of 1900, which was adopted by tba Council on the 4th March last and afterwards forwarded to the Secretary of State for the Home Department for approval, the Home Secretary now required that addl. tional particulars should 'be furnished before such approval could be given, and also that the assents in writing of the Ministers interested in the scale of fees should be obtained. It was resolved that the Clerk be instructed to obtain the required information and send the suns to the Home Secretary, in order that his approval may be obtained to the scale of fees. The minute was approved on the proposition of Mr. Simpson. TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A. letter was read before this committee frsm the treasurer of the Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Committee enclosing' cheque for EU 1O beinz, the amount of the cheque sent to him direct by the County Treasurer instead of to the treasurer of the Council, oa account of technical ins`rue.ion in this district. It was resolved that it be a recommendation to the Finance Committso to pav, a cheque for £3O 103 in favour of the Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Committee.</p>
        <p>This course was agreed to on the proposition of Dr Patterson.</p>
        <p>GoAS COMMITTEE. At a meeting of this Committee, the AnginpPr sub- mitted his report on the existing gas undertaking, and his suggestions as to the alterations and extensions which, in his opinion, were neces,ary at the gas works. It was resolved that the Yenert be referred to the Gas Works Extensions Committee with instructions to con- sider the same, and report therein to the next meeting of this committee. The Elgin-er reverted with respect to the lighting of the County Council main roads in the district, the t following being a list of the roads not liehted during the summer months, and the number of lamps in each road, viz r Southworth-roed, 9 lamps ; Mill-lane, 9 ; Park-road, 5 ; Cemetery-road, 12 ; Warerave-road, 17 ; Ashtonirosel, 17; Crow-lane West, 5 ; Crow-lane Est, 15 ; tete!, 89 The witimated cost of Fighting the above lames duricg the summer months is £l9. It was resolved that the engineer's report be adopted,and that the lamps therein referred to, together with 10 !amps in Common-road, be lighted during the summer menthe —the estimated c-ist of the liehtine of the lamps being about £2l.—lt was further reselved that the Eegineer be directed to report to the next meetine of the Committee (a) With respect to those streets in the district which are not lighted ; and (1) As to those streets Which are inadequately lighted.—lt was reenived thaa the question of the accept- ance of a tender for the supply of coil be left to the Chairman of the Committee and Councillor T °emend, together ' with the Engineer, with full power to accept such tender 'as they may think be3t ; and that the tender of the Collins Green Colliery Co., Limited, for the Runnier of cennel during the period ending the 31st May, 1902, be aceepted. A letter was read from Mr W Woods, Veterinary Surgeon, with respect to the lamenees of the chestnut mare at the Gas Works. It was reeolved that the Clerk write to Mr Woods and enquire the clam of the horse's lamenese. The committee also resolved that the Engineer be directed to obtain tenders for repairing the chimney stack at the Gas Work. end that the tenders which had been obtaieed by the Engineer for the re-setting of the retort beds be left to the Chairman of the Committee with power to sceept such tender as he may think best.</p>
        <p>The minutes were approved on the proposltion of Mr Thomp3on. WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee resolved. that the Council be rerrone- ' mended at its next ordinary meeting to make applica- tion to the Local Government Board for their Reaction to the borrowing ct the sum of £.OOO, under Section 53 of therNswton District Improvement Act, 1855, to defray the cost of laying new distribution water mains throughout the district and that Professor Boyd Dawkins be engaged to examine and report to this committee as to whether the situation of the present pumping station is the best water hearing area within the district. A letter was read from Messrs Houseman and CompanY. Lord Newton's solicitoro, calling utlon the Council to forthwith carry mains along Parksiled•oad, to Wood- head, for the supply of aeate r to persona residing or marrying on businffs in Woodhead and the r eighhour- hood, under the terms of the lease (dated the 3rd December, 1900) of the site of the pumping station. It was resolved that the clerk be instructed to inform Lord Newton's solicitors that th 3 Council are proceeding as speedily as possible with the completien, of their water scheme, which soherne includes the laying of mains to Woodhead ; that the horrowino• powers of the Council under their Local Act for water works purposes have been extended to enable the Council to compete the scheme • that the plans and estimates of the cost of the wells hew) been Prepared and approved by the Council ; arid that the Council are only waiting for the sanction of the Lie at Government Board to ths borrow- I ing of the money necessary to complete the scheme before commencing with the work, the first part of which icchidcs the laying o; a weer main to Woodland. Approved on the proposition of Mr Borron, HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>' Tnie cemmittee reeemmended that the following building plans he approved and paesed, viz :—G ic Clarke (Crossdane), eight dwelling houses, enroll of Merkee-etreet and Neville-street ; William S'ephens (21, Sankey-street), tix dwellina heave% Neville- , street ; Tern Berri rog( Wdawr ge7l dwelling eg-r one bDoleg, rid;w7de 1 loicn: house, Wargr kye-road ; Joseph A Birchen (IE, Brunswicle-eoad), four street. The folloWing buildine plan was dis- approved, viz. :—Alfeed Bywater (Pemberton Saw Mills, near wiearo, 25 dwelling houses, Birch-street, The interim report of the Ilsyal Commission on sewage disposal. a printed copy of which had been supolied to each member ef the Committee, was deferred tone next meetiny for consideration. A letter was read from Lord Newton's Agent (Mr A C Leslie) reminding the Council that the fence at the corner of Water-street which had been removed by their Contractor, had not yet been replaced. It was resolved that Mr Leslie be informed that !the fencing was not removed by the Council or their contractor, that certain excavations hael to be done in making the street, and as the fencing had been erected above the prescribed level of the street it was deprived of the support of the earth and niti- mirelv fell down. and that the tCluncil. therefore, does not consider itself in any way liable for the fence A letter was read from the County Sueveyor stating, with respect to the application which was made by this Council for an increased grane towards the cost of the maintenance of main reads, that in view of the pro- posed tramways to be conetructed in this District, the County Main /toads ned Bridges Committee cannot in- crease their offer of £650 for ordinary maintenanee;elso that it was considered advisable to defer any special works until the tramways have been constructed. The report of the Inspector of Nuisances for the pest month was read, from which it appeared—(a) that a tip for dry refuels had been secured on a farm adjoining the Canal at the bottom of Common. road. (b) Tnat he had visited the Common Lodgina Houses, Slaughter Houses, and Bake Houses in the district, and had found them in a satiefectory condition. It was resolved that the Clerk be instructed to write to the Governors of the winweek E lucational Trust and rok for their per- mission to tip dry rubbish on land in lbs occupation of Mr Berry, adjoining the Cana) near cemmeaeroad. It was resolved that the attention of Mr A C Leslie (Lord Newt)n's agent) be called to u‘f•BstliYonff.ohf house ee It was resolved iunsenoobiLathnee Smith-Premier aehhin be referred to the Surveyor, the insanitary sttahtaetoft7htelecie Medical Officer of Health, and Inspector of Nuisances, with instructions to report thereon to this Committee: The Clerk reported that he had informed the butchers in the district that no place could be used or occupied as re, elauehter-bouse without a licence from this authority first having been obtained,end thatlthey were liable te penalties for so doing until such licences had teen granted by this Council. He also stated that forms of application for a licence had been sent to the butchers and teat they had been informed that this Committee would, on receipt of the application, be primared to recommend the Council to grant a licenne for 12 menthe if the premises were found in a sanitary stater It was resolved that the action of the Clerk be approved and adopted. Mr Simpson moved the ceefirmation of the minutes. Mr Gilmour called attention to the acquirement of a tip for dry refuse on a farm adjoining the Canal and said it WAS ridiculous to pass that resolution before they had obtained the permission of the Win wick Educa- tional Trust to use the tip. The Chairman raid the duty of the Council was to confirm the minutes as bring a correct record of what transpired in committee or to say by specific resolution in what particular they are wrong. The paragraph referred to was simple' a cony of the Nuisance Inspector's report. If it was wrong then it was their duty to refer it back. If i was right then it was the duty of the Council to accept it and deal with the matter at the next meeting of the committee, when the reply of the Winwick Educational Trust had been received</p>
        <p>Mr Gilmour moved an amendment that the para. granh referred to be left out with they had the report of the Winwick Educational Trust. The Chairman : Does anyone second that most un- usu II proceeding? There being no seconder the amendment fell to the ground...</p>
        <p>Dr Latham called attention to the minute relating to the fence at the corner of Water-street and stated that it seemed to suggest that the Council removed the earth which supported the fence and caused it to fall down. The Chairman: What do you propose to subitituto ? Dr Lathatn : I should not substitute anything. I should simply say the Council was not liable. The Chairman : It is customary to state in definite terms the words you wish to substitute. Dr Latham : I move that Mr Leslie be informed— , Tao Chairman : YO'l moot OVEI it in writing. D: Latham : That would takoirne half an hour.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : The only alternative is to refer it back for further consideratioo. The minutes were then passed. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>--!to WAS MR. GILMOUR LOUNGING? Before this Committee the Clerk read the corresporiT entre with Merits Kincaid, Waller. and Manville, the eugineera to the South Lancashire Tramways Company, with respect to the plans which were submitted to the Committee at its last minting and disapproved, and reported that Mr Guiseppi desired to see the Committee en the points raired by the Cauncil.—ls was resolved that the matter be referred to the Tramways Speoial Committee, and that Mr Guiseppi he asked ta attend the meeting at which the matter will be discussed Tbe letter from Mr J Jordan, postmaster, applying for the permission of the Council to the closing of Wargrave ' sublease office on Bunk Holidays,'which wag referred to the Committee by the C usmcil at its last meeting for ' consideration, was read. The Clerk reported that copies of the applicatioa bad been suppled to the rep resenta• tittle of Wargrave ward whn were not present at the Council, and read a letter which he had received from Mr Coilingwood upon the matter, he being unable to attend. It was resolved that the Clerk be directed to inform Mr Jordan that the Committee had carefully considered hie applicatioa for the permission of the Council to the closing of Wargrave sub-post office on Bank Holidays, but that whilst being of oninion that the Wargrave postmistress should have occasional relief, which could be graoted frcm the Newton Post Office, they regret they cannot recommend the Council to con- sent to the closing of the sub-office for that parpose.— The Clerk reported that with respeot to the letter which he had sent to the County Council requesting them to make bye-laws for the suppression of betting in streets, he hal bean informed that the Clerk of the County Council was tabulating bye-laws of other counties so es to bring the matter before the County Council.—A. letter was read from Lord Newton'a agent (Mr A C Leslie) stating that the several requests which hacPbeen made by the Council far land for various purposss could not be considered until the Common question had been ' settled. Ho also stated that Lord Newton was most , anxious to meet the wishes of the Council as far as possible.—lt was resolved that the letter be referred to the Commons Sub-Committee.</p>
        <p>_ Dr. Latham said he would like to know what the) nninions of the representatives of the Wargravo Ward were uo-n the proposed closing of the Wargrave-road Poet Office.</p>
        <p>The Chairman thought it was hardly fair to ask the opininn of any member given in committee. Mr Gilmour was proceeding to make some observations whilst sitting down, when _ _</p>
        <p>Trio Chairman interposed, and said if Mr Gilmour was addressing him he mugt stand up. Fie (the Chairman) would not hear Mr. Gilmour while that gentleman was sitciog down. , Mr. Gilmour : I don't know why I should rise. I think I was respectful to the chair and I think it is very hard to he dealt with as you are dealing with me. 'the Chairman : You cannot address the Council whilst you are lounging in a chair.</p>
        <p>Mr Gllmonr : I .;An- not lounging; I should be very ploased to have a lounge here. The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Garnett—Watson.</p>
        <p>At Lyrom Congregational Church on Wednesday afternoon, the marriage was solemnized of Mr Philip Carlisle Garnett, youngest son of Mr Robsrt Garnett, J.r., of Hall Nook. Penketh, and Miss Watson, daughter of Mrs Watson, of Westcliffe, Lymtn, and sister of the Rev C Ernest Watson, pastor of Lymni Congregational Church. The Church had been beautifully decorated for the oc- casion and a large congregation witnessed the cere- mony. The bride was attired in a white silk dress delicately trimmed with Mlle of lace and insertion with tranT trent yoke and sleeves. She also wore a veil which had been used on similar occasions by other members of the family, and her head was crowned with a wreath of orange blossom. She carried a charming bouquet of white exotics. The two bridesmaids in attendance were Miss Dorothea Watson (sister of the bride) and Miss Ilary Parker (niece of the bridegroom). Their dresses were of similar texture tolthat of the bride's dress with blue sashes of crepe dee/line and Tuscan hats trimmed with turquoise blue chiffon. Their bou- quets were composEd of yellow marguerites and they wore curb bracelets the gift of the bridegroom. The ceremony was performed by the Rev C E Watson (brother of the bride). Mr Arthur E yokes, of Northampton satisfactorily discharged the duties of best man. As the newly-married couple left the church, Mr F Hickman. who presided at the organ, played .Mendelssolin's"Wedding recerition was afterwards held in the grounds adjoining the chapel where abcait 60;gu ests ass mbled.Refreshments were served and the numerous beautiful presents which had been received were on view. The bride and bridegroom left tor London en route for the Isle of Wight, amid the hearty good wishes of their mary friends. The bride's travelling dress was made of grey crepe de ciinj, trimmed:with white chiffon and lace and blaok velvet. She also wore a black toque.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-06_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>TERRIBLE FAI"ALII Y TO AN ENGINE DRIVER AT EARLESTOWN</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE FAI"ALII Y TO AN ENGINE DRIVER AT EARLESTOWN</p>
        <p>' On Wednesday morning an engine driver in the employ of the London and North-Western Railway Com- pany, met with a painfully sudden death whilst in charge of a passenger train at Earlestown. The unfor- tunate man was Aigy Rarsington, about 45 years of sg^. of Stockport, and be was driving the engine of the 9-35 express train from llanchesrer to Liverpool, calling at Esrlestown. Oa arrival at Earlestown it wr s found that some of the working parts of the engine needei atten- tion, and as soon as he re-commenced the journey to Liverpool, the driver went 81°714 the outside of the engine to nil the Darts in question. Standing on the line next the main line was a train of empty coaches, and just as the express was passing these about 300 yards from Earlestown station, the driver was returning to the cab of his engine, and leaned backward to swing himself on to the footplate. In doing so the back of his head came into violent contact with the projecting por- tion of the brake van of the standing train,smashing his skull and killing him almo A instantly. The fireman was at the time engaged thing up, and it was not until he was near Collins Greea that he missed the driver. He thought he was engaged at the front of the engine, but as Le could not see him, and he did not return,he pulled the train up at St Helens Junction and found the driver was not on the erafine. Ho hod to get another driver, and the trAn was then taken on to Liverpool. The body of the dead driver was caaveyed to the mortuary to await the inquest.</p>
        <p>Personal .</p>
        <p>Captain Bradford Silverthorne, ev-adjutant of the Ist Volunteer Battalion the Prince of Wales Volunteers—South Lancashire Regiment, has been selected as first adjutant of the new Ch Battalion Royal Garrison Regiment, which is formed this week. During the war the captain was adjutant of the 2nd Battalion of the Royal Northern Reserve Regim'ent—which included Cheshire veterans—at Pembroke Dock. He wears three decorations for the Tirah expedition, 1897-8.</p>
        <p>The Rev Abel Jones Parry, D. D.. who is to preach the autumnal sermons at Hill Cliffs Baptist Chapel to-morrow, is one of the best known and ablest preachers bath in English and Welsh which Wales has produced. He has held pastorates in some of the most important churches in England and Wales For over thirty years ha has maintained a high reputation as an expository preacher and has pub- lished several works, church history and sermons, his latest being an exposition of the Epistle to the Romans. Ho has been the editor of ssveral periodi- cals, is an ex-chairman of the Welsh Baptist Union and has held all the distinguished positions of his denomination. Dr Parry is also a leader in Temperance and Philanthropic Works and estab- lished the Baptist forward movement in Wales while he is looked upon as one of the leaders of the Free Churches in Wales.</p>
        <p>A Russian seaman named Hansen ran amuck on board the schooner Volant at Yarmouth. Ile attacked Cap- tain Swan, wounding him in the face; felled the mate, Henry Sinallburri, with a blow that cut open his skull; and broke the nose of the captain's son, aged eleven, who was making a holiiay trio. Hansen thea jumped overboard and has not been seen since. All three of the Injured are in hospital, and the mate's condition is critical.</p>
